/-! ### No-clear bridge (place-closure ⇒ applyStep-closure)

The `place_*_skyline` bricks describe the bare hard-drop. The bag-indexed
`hstep` obligation, however, is phrased on the *full* move `applyStep =
clearLines ∘ place`. When the placed profile still leaves at least one real
column empty, no row is complete, so the line-clear is the identity and the full
move coincides with the bare placement. This is the lever that turns every
non-clearing `place_*` brick (O, S, Z, T, L, J, horizontal-I) into the
`applyStep` form the reduction consumes; the lone clearing piece (vertical I in a
well) is handled directly by `applyStep_vertI_well`. -/

/-- **Place-to-applyStep, no clear.** If placing `pl` on `skyline cfg h` yields
`skyline cfg h'` and `h'` keeps some real column empty, the full move (drop then
clear) yields the same skyline — the clear does nothing. -/
theorem applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq {cfg : GameConfig} {h h' : ℕ → ℕ}
    {pl : Placement}
    (hplace : Placement.place (skyline cfg h) pl = skyline cfg h')
    (hzero : ∃ j < cfg.cols, h' j = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl = skyline cfg h' := by
  rw [Placement.applyStep_eq_clearLines_place, hplace,
      clearLines_skyline (m := 0) (fun _ _ => Nat.zero_le _) hzero]
  congr 1

/-- **Skyline-preserving fold peel.** The inductive bridge that drives a *concrete*
schedule fold: if the head placement `pl` maps one skyline to another
(`pl.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) = skyline cfg h'` — exactly what every per-piece
no-clear witness like `applyStep_O_skyline_noclear` delivers), then folding `pl :: rest`
from `skyline cfg h` is the same as folding just `rest` from the *new* skyline
`skyline cfg h'`. Iterating this lemma — peeling one piece at a time, each step
discharged by the matching per-piece equation — rewrites a whole concrete bag schedule
into `skyline cfg h_final`, manufacturing the `hfold` hypothesis the well-clear ledger
(`card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card`) consumes. Proof: `List.foldl_cons` exposes the
head application, `hhead` rewrites it to the next skyline. -/
theorem foldl_applyStep_skyline_cons {cfg : GameConfig} {h h' : ℕ → ℕ}
    {pl : Placement} {rest : List Placement}
    (hhead : Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl = skyline cfg h') :
    (pl :: rest).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)
      = rest.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h') := by
  simp only [List.foldl_cons, hhead]

/-- **Non-clearing vertical-I closure on a skyline.** The no-clear companion to
`applyStep_vertI_skyline`: when a reserved well `w ≠ c` keeps some real column empty
(`h w = 0`), dropping the vertical I on column `c` completes no row, so the full move is
the bare placement — the skyline with column `c` raised by four
(`Function.update h c (h c + 4)`), the well untouched. This is the vertical-I member of the
no-clear `applyStep_*_skyline_noclear` family (O, S, Z, horizI, T, L, J), and the *filler*
brick a concrete bag schedule uses to raise the well's neighbours toward height `≥ 4` —
each such drop discharged by `foldl_applyStep_skyline_cons` — before the terminal
clearing vertical-I (`applyStep_vertI_well`) re-flattens. Proof: `place_vertI_skyline`
gives the placed skyline, whose well column stays `0` (`Function.update_of_ne`), so
`applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq` collapses the clear. -/
theorem applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update h c (h c + 4)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_vertI_skyline hc)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **Stacking `m` vertical I's lifts a single reserved-well column by `4 * m`.** The
replicate-fold generalization of `applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear` and the width-1 analog of the
O-stack atom `applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear`: dropping the vertical I `m` times onto the
same real column `c`, kept clear of a reserved empty column `w` so no drop ever completes a row,
raises that one column by exactly `4 * m` (`k ↦ k + 4m`) and fixes every other column. Proof by
induction on `m` peeling the last I (`List.replicate_succ'`): the `m`-fold fold lands column `c`
at `h c + 4m` by the IH, then one further `applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear` writes the final `+ 4`
and the nested `Function.update`s collapse pointwise. This is the height-parametric width-1 raise
tool: the building block for the width-3 tail (cols 7, 8, 9) that the O-tile pairs cannot reach,
since a flat 3-wide lift by `4 * m` needs `3 * m` vertical I's, `m` per column. -/
theorem applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hw0 : h w = 0) (m : ℕ) :
    List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c })
      = skyline cfg (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m)) := by
  induction m with
  | zero =>
    simp only [List.replicate_zero, List.foldl_nil, Nat.mul_zero, Nat.add_zero]
    refine congrArg (skyline cfg) (funext fun x => ?_)
    rcases eq_or_ne x c with rfl | hx0
    · rw [Function.update_self]
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hx0]
  | succ m ih =>
    have hw0'' : (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m)) w = 0 := by
      rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0
    rw [List.replicate_succ', List.foldl_append, ih, List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_nil,
        applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hw0'']
    exact congrArg (skyline cfg)
      (by funext x; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega)

/-- **The `m`-fold vertical-I stack adds exactly `4 * m` cells.** The cells/card face of the
vertical-I tile (shape `applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear`, height
`maxColHeight_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows`): dropping `m`
vertical I-pieces into a single reserved-well column `c` adds exactly `4 * m` filled cells. Each
vertical I contributes four cells in one column, so `m` of them add `4 * m`. Proof: rewrite the fold
to its closed `skyline (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m))` profile, expand both cardinalities via
`card_skyline` to column sums, peel the single bumped column `c` off each side
(`Finset.sum_update_of_mem` on the left, `← Finset.add_sum_erase` on the right, reconciled by
`Finset.erase_eq`), and `omega` cancels the shared tail sum. The width-1 analog of ABJP's
`card_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear`, and the per-column brick the width-3 tail cells face
chains three times. -/
theorem card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hw0 : h w = 0) (m : ℕ) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c })).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 * m := by
  have hc' : c ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc
  rw [applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hw0 m, card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc', Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **Two disjoint vertical-I stacks add exactly `8 * m` cells.** The cells face of the 2-wide
vertical-I composition (shape `applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear`): stacking
`m` vertical I's on column `c` then `m` on column `c + 1` adds `8 * m` filled cells. Width-1 analog
of the O ledger ABJW (`card_applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear`), but simpler — each
vertical I touches a single column, so no flatness carry-over is needed. Proof: `List.foldl_append`
peels the second stack, the ABKA shape collapses the leading column-`c` fold to `skyline H1`, the
atom ledger ABKF adds `4 * m` for the column-`(c+1)` stack, and a `← shape` reveal turns
`(skyline H1).card` back into the column-`c` fold so ABKF reads off its first `4 * m`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 1 })).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 8 * m := by
  have hw0' : (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m)) w = 0 := by
    rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0
  have hcard1a :
      (skyline cfg (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m))).card = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 * m := by
    rw [← applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hw0 m]
    exact card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hw0 m
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hw0 m,
      card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc1 hw hwc1 hw0' m,
      hcard1a]
  omega

/-- **Two disjoint vertical-I stacks raise a reserved-well column pair by `4 * m` each.** The
width-1 two-stack composition: concatenating an `m`-fold vertical-I stack on column `c` with one
on column `c + 1` (both kept clear of the reserved well `w`) raises each of `c` and `c + 1` by
exactly `4 * m`, independently. Unlike the O-tile pair `applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear`
no flatness hypothesis is needed, since each vertical I touches a single column. Proof chains the
width-1 atom `applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear` twice through `List.foldl_append` (the
well staying empty across the first stack, `hw0'`); the residual update-vs-update values collapse
pointwise. The two-column unit toward the width-3 tail (cols 7, 8, 9) rebuild. -/
theorem applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 1 })
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m))
          (c + 1) (h (c + 1) + 4 * m)) := by
  have hw0' : (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m)) w = 0 := by
    rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hw0 m,
      applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc1 hw hwc1 hw0' m]
  exact congrArg (skyline cfg)
    (by funext x; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega)

/-- **Three disjoint vertical-I stacks raise a reserved-well column triple by `4 * m` each.** The
width-3 tail tile (cols `c`, `c+1`, `c+2`), the vertical-I analog of the 6-wide O-tile
`applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear`: concatenating three `m`-fold vertical-I
stacks, one per column, raises each of `c`, `c+1`, `c+2` by exactly `4 * m`, independently and with
no flatness hypothesis. This is the brick the O-tile pairs structurally cannot supply — a flat
3-wide lift, the piece that closes the `10 = 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3` repack of cols `1..9` (well at col
0). Proof is the ABJT recipe one stack narrower: one `List.foldl_append` peels the third stack,
`applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear` collapses the leading two-stack fold, the
width-1 atom adds the third column, and the residual update values reconcile pointwise. -/
theorem applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 1 } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 2 })
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h
          c (h c + 4 * m)) (c + 1) (h (c + 1) + 4 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 4 * m)) := by
  have hw0' :
      (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m)) (c + 1) (h (c + 1) + 4 * m)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hw hwc hwc1 hw0,
      applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc2 hw hwc2 hw0' m]
  exact congrArg (skyline cfg)
    (by funext x; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega)

/-- **Three disjoint vertical-I stacks add exactly `12 * m` cells.** The cells/card face of the
width-3 tail tile (shape `applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear`, height
`maxColHeight_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows`, safety
`not_isLost_take_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline`): stacking `m` vertical I's on
each of columns `c`, `c + 1`, `c + 2` adds `12 * m` filled cells. Width-1 analog of the 6-wide O
ledger ABJY, but simpler — each vertical I touches a single column, so no flatness carry-over is
needed. Same chaining recipe one level up: `List.foldl_append` peels the last stack, the 2-wide
shape ABKB collapses the leading fold to `skyline H2`, the atom ledger ABKF adds `4 * m` for the
column-`(c+2)` stack, and a `← shape` reveal turns `(skyline H2).card` back into the 2-wide fold so
the 2-wide ledger ABKG reads off its `8 * m`. With this the width-3 tail tile is four-face complete,
so the full `cols 1..9` repack `10 = 1(well) + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3` has a cells brick for every width
(three O-pairs plus one vertical-I triple) — the packing carrier #77 can now read off its area
ledger. -/
theorem card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 1 } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 2 })).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 12 * m := by
  have hw0' :
      (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m)) (c + 1) (h (c + 1) + 4 * m)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hcard2 :
      (skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 4 * m))
        (c + 1) (h (c + 1) + 4 * m))).card = (skyline cfg h).card + 8 * m := by
    rw [← applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hw hwc hwc1 hw0]
    exact card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hw hwc hwc1 hw0
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_vertI_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hw hwc hwc1 hw0,
      card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_skyline_noclear hc2 hw hwc2 hw0' m,
      hcard2]
  omega

/-- **The vertical-I filler fold: a whole sequence of well-avoiding column-raises.**
Folding a list of vertical-I placements — one per column in `cs`, every column real and
distinct from the reserved well `w` — onto `skyline cfg h` (with the well empty, `h w = 0`)
lands exactly on the skyline whose profile is `h` with each listed column raised by four,
i.e. `cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h`. Because the well stays empty
throughout (no update touches `w`), no row ever completes, so every drop is non-clearing
and the board-fold tracks the profile-fold step for step. This is the *filling engine* of a
concrete bag schedule: pick `cs` to pile each of the well's neighbours up to height `≥ 4`,
and this lemma manufactures the `hfold : pls.foldl applyStep b = skyline cfg h'` that the
well-clear ledger (`card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card`) consumes — entirely by symbolic
rewrite, no kernel `decide`. Proof: list induction, each head discharged by the peel
`foldl_applyStep_skyline_cons` fed the filler equation `applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear`,
the well staying empty (`Function.update_of_ne`) to license the inductive step. -/
theorem foldl_vertI_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} {w : ℕ} (hw : w < cfg.cols) :
    ∀ (cs : List ℕ), (∀ c ∈ cs, c < cfg.cols ∧ c ≠ w) →
    ∀ {h : ℕ → ℕ}, h w = 0 →
      (cs.map (fun c => ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } : Placement))).foldl
          (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)
        = skyline cfg (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) := by
  intro cs
  induction cs with
  | nil => intro _ h _; simp
  | cons c rest ih =>
    intro hcs h hh0
    have hc : c < cfg.cols := (hcs c (List.mem_cons.mpr (Or.inl rfl))).1
    have hcw : c ≠ w := (hcs c (List.mem_cons.mpr (Or.inl rfl))).2
    have hwc : w ≠ c := fun he => hcw he.symm
    have hh1 : (Function.update h c (h c + 4)) w = 0 := by
      rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hh0
    simp only [List.map_cons]
    rw [foldl_applyStep_skyline_cons (applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hh0),
        List.foldl_cons]
    exact ih (fun c' hc' => hcs c' (List.mem_cons.mpr (Or.inr hc'))) hh1

/-- **The well survives the fill.** The iterated-update profile produced by
`foldl_vertI_skyline` leaves any column `w` untouched as long as `w` is never one
of the filled columns. This is the evaluation lemma that keeps the well empty: a
list of vertical-I drops that all avoid `w` folds to a profile still reading
`h w` at `w`, so an initially-empty well (`h w = 0`) stays clear-ready for the
terminal well-clear. -/
theorem foldl_update_skip {w : ℕ} :
    ∀ (cs : List ℕ), w ∉ cs →
    ∀ {h : ℕ → ℕ},
      (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) w = h w := by
  intro cs
  induction cs with
  | nil => intro _ h; simp
  | cons c rest ih =>
    intro hnotmem h
    have hwc : w ≠ c := fun he => hnotmem (List.mem_cons.mpr (Or.inl he))
    have hnotrest : w ∉ rest := fun hmem => hnotmem (List.mem_cons.mpr (Or.inr hmem))
    simp only [List.foldl_cons]
    rw [ih hnotrest, Function.update_of_ne hwc]

/-- **The fill never lowers a column.** Each step of the iterated-update fold
either bumps its target column by `+4` or leaves a column untouched, so the
folded profile dominates the starting one pointwise: `h j ≤ (cs.foldl … h) j` for
every column `j`. This monotonicity is the lever that turns a single `+4` bump
into a persistent `≥ 4` floor — once a column has been filled, no later drop on
another column can erode it below the height it reached. -/
theorem le_foldl_update :
    ∀ (cs : List ℕ) {h : ℕ → ℕ} (j : ℕ),
      h j ≤ (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) j := by
  intro cs
  induction cs with
  | nil => intro h j; simp
  | cons c rest ih =>
    intro h j
    simp only [List.foldl_cons]
    have hstep : h j ≤ (Function.update h c (h c + 4)) j := by
      by_cases hjc : j = c
      · subst hjc; rw [Function.update_self]; omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc]
    exact le_trans hstep (@ih (Function.update h c (h c + 4)) j)

/-- **Every filled column reaches the clear threshold.** If column `k` appears in
the fill list `cs`, the iterated-update profile reads at least `4` there: the
moment `k` is processed its value jumps to `h k + 4 ≥ 4`, and by monotonicity
(`le_foldl_update`) no subsequent drop can pull it back below `4`. Together with
`foldl_update_skip` (the well stays `0`) this certifies the post-fill skyline is
clear-ready — every neighbour of the well is at least four high, so a vertical-I
dropped into the well completes four lines. -/
theorem four_le_foldl_update :
    ∀ (cs : List ℕ) {h : ℕ → ℕ} {k : ℕ}, k ∈ cs →
      4 ≤ (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) k := by
  intro cs
  induction cs with
  | nil => intro h k hmem; simp at hmem
  | cons d rest ih =>
    intro h k hmem
    simp only [List.foldl_cons]
    rcases List.mem_cons.mp hmem with hkd | hkrest
    · have hmono := @le_foldl_update rest (Function.update h d (h d + 4)) k
      have hkv : (Function.update h d (h d + 4)) k = h d + 4 := by
        rw [hkd]; exact Function.update_self d (h d + 4) h
      rw [hkv] at hmono
      omega
    · exact ih hkrest

/-- **The filled profile is clear-ready.** Drop one vertical-I into every column
other than the reserved well `w` (the fill list `cs` covers all of
`{0,…,cfg.cols-1} \ {w}` and avoids `w`), starting from a profile with an empty
well (`h w = 0`). The resulting iterated-update profile then reads `0` at the well
and `≥ 4` at every other in-field column — exactly the `hwell`/`hothers`
preconditions that `card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card` and the terminal
vertical-I well-clear (`applyStep_vertI_well`) consume. Combines
`foldl_update_skip` (well survives) with `four_le_foldl_update` (neighbours
reach the threshold). -/
theorem foldl_update_clear_ready {cfg : GameConfig} {cs : List ℕ} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hwnotmem : w ∉ cs) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcover : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → j ∈ cs) :
    (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) w = 0 ∧
    (∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w →
      4 ≤ (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) j) := by
  refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [foldl_update_skip cs hwnotmem]; exact hw0
  · intro j hj hjw
    exact four_le_foldl_update cs (hcover j hj hjw)

/-- **One concrete fill-and-clear cycle.** Drop a vertical-I into every non-well
column (the fill list `cs` covers `{0,…,cfg.cols-1} \ {w}` and avoids the well
`w`), then a final vertical-I into the well itself. Starting from an empty-well
skyline `skyline cfg h` (`h w = 0`), the whole placement sequence folds to the
post-clear skyline `skyline cfg (fun j => if j = w then 0 else p j - 4)`, where
`p` is the filled profile: the four rows the well-I completes clear, dropping
every neighbour by `4` while the well stays empty. This is the first *closed*
concrete schedule — a board returns to an empty-well skyline of strictly lower
profile — assembled by pure symbolic rewrite (`foldl_vertI_skyline` peels the
fills, `foldl_update_clear_ready` certifies the clear-ready surface, and
`applyStep_vertI_well` discharges the terminal clear). -/
theorem foldl_fill_then_clear {cfg : GameConfig} {cs : List ℕ} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols)
    (hcs : ∀ c ∈ cs, c < cfg.cols ∧ c ≠ w)
    (hwnotmem : w ∉ cs) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcover : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → j ∈ cs) :
    (((cs.map (fun c => ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } : Placement)))
        ++ [({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w } : Placement)]).foldl
        (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h))
      = skyline cfg (fun j => if j = w then 0
          else (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) j - 4) := by
  rw [List.foldl_append, foldl_vertI_skyline hw cs hcs hw0]
  simp only [List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_nil]
  obtain ⟨hwell, hothers⟩ := foldl_update_clear_ready hwnotmem hw0 hcover
  rw [applyStep_vertI_well hw hwell hothers]

/-- **Each column filled once gains exactly `4`.** When the fill list `cs` is
duplicate-free, every member column `j` is bumped exactly once, so the
iterated-update profile reads precisely `h j + 4` there — no more (the `Nodup`
hypothesis rules out a second `+4`) and no less (the bump does happen). The
`Nodup`-sharpened companion to `four_le_foldl_update`: it upgrades the `≥ 4`
floor to an exact value, which is what lets the once-each fill be cancelled
column-for-column by the well-clear's uniform `−4`, returning the surface to its
starting profile. -/
theorem foldl_update_nodup_eq :
    ∀ (cs : List ℕ), cs.Nodup → ∀ {h : ℕ → ℕ} {j : ℕ}, j ∈ cs →
      (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) j = h j + 4 := by
  intro cs
  induction cs with
  | nil => intro _ h j hmem; simp at hmem
  | cons d rest ih =>
    intro hnodup h j hmem
    rw [List.nodup_cons] at hnodup
    obtain ⟨hdrest, hrestnodup⟩ := hnodup
    simp only [List.foldl_cons]
    rcases List.mem_cons.mp hmem with hjd | hjrest
    · have hjnotrest : j ∉ rest := by rw [hjd]; exact hdrest
      rw [foldl_update_skip rest hjnotrest, hjd, Function.update_self]
    · have hjd : j ≠ d := fun he => hdrest (he ▸ hjrest)
      rw [ih hrestnodup hjrest, Function.update_of_ne hjd]

/-- **Transient height bound for a duplicate-free fill.** Folding the vertical-I
update over a `Nodup` column list raises any column by *at most* `4`: a column
either appears once (exact `+4`, via `foldl_update_nodup_eq`) or not at all
(unchanged, via `foldl_update_skip`). Instantiated at the zero profile this caps
every transient column height in the fill phase at `4`, so — paired with
`colHeight_skyline` and `not_isLost_of_colHeight_le` — every intermediate board
of the fill-then-clear cycle stays in-field whenever `4 ≤ cfg.rows`. The placement
half of the cycle's survival certificate. -/
theorem foldl_update_nodup_le {cs : List ℕ} (hnodup : cs.Nodup) {h : ℕ → ℕ} (j : ℕ) :
    (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) j ≤ h j + 4 := by
  by_cases hj : j ∈ cs
  · rw [foldl_update_nodup_eq cs hnodup hj]
  · rw [foldl_update_skip cs hj]; omega

/-- **Every fill-phase board is in-field.** The skyline produced by a `Nodup`
vertical-I fill of the empty board is never lost once the field is at least four
rows tall: each column tops out at height `4 ≤ cfg.rows` (`foldl_update_nodup_le`
at the zero profile), so `not_isLost_skyline` applies. Since any prefix of a
`Nodup` schedule is itself `Nodup`, this certifies loss-freedom at *every*
intermediate state of the fill-then-clear cycle's fill phase — the survival half
of the closed-cycle witness, complementing the board-level fixed point
`foldl_fill_then_clear_init`. -/
theorem not_isLost_skyline_foldl_fill {cfg : GameConfig} {cs : List ℕ}
    (hnodup : cs.Nodup) (hrows : 4 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        (skyline cfg (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) (fun _ => 0))) := by
  apply not_isLost_skyline
  intro j _
  have hle : (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) (fun _ => 0)) j ≤ 4 := by
    simpa using foldl_update_nodup_le hnodup (h := fun _ => 0) j
  omega

/-- **Fill-phase survival from any bounded surface.** The arbitrary-start generalisation of
`not_isLost_skyline_foldl_fill` (which fixes the empty profile): a `Nodup` vertical-I fill — each
listed column rising exactly `+4` — applied to *any* surface `h` whose in-field columns are all
capped by `B` lands every column at most `B + 4`, so the result is never lost once `B + 4 ≤ rows`.
This is the build-phase survival face of the closed fill-then-clear cycle at a non-zero floor: the
clear's `-4` is undone by the fill's `+4` (`foldl_clear_profile_eq_in_field`), and this certifies
the intermediate fill never tops out as long as the starting surface leaves four rows of headroom. -/
theorem not_isLost_skyline_foldl_fill_of_bounded {cfg : GameConfig} {cs : List ℕ} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {B : ℕ} (hnodup : cs.Nodup) (hh : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (hcap : B + 4 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        (skyline cfg (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h)) := by
  apply not_isLost_skyline
  intro j hj
  have hle : (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) j ≤ h j + 4 :=
    foldl_update_nodup_le hnodup j
  have hhj : h j ≤ B := hh j hj
  omega

/-- **The fill-and-clear cycle restores the in-field profile.** After a
duplicate-free fill of every non-well column (`cs` covers `{0,…,cfg.cols-1}\{w}`)
and the terminal well-clear, the post-clear profile
`fun j => if j = w then 0 else p j - 4` agrees with the starting profile `h` on
every in-field column `j < cfg.cols`: the well column is reset to `0 = h w`, and
each neighbour's exact `+4` gain (`foldl_update_nodup_eq`) is undone by the
clear's `-4`. This is the algebraic heart of the closed bag cycle — it certifies
the surface returns to where it began across all columns that the board actually
occupies (out-of-field columns are irrelevant; the board-level fixed point
follows once paired with a `skyline` congruence). -/
theorem foldl_clear_profile_eq_in_field {cfg : GameConfig} {cs : List ℕ}
    {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hnodup : cs.Nodup) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcover : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → j ∈ cs) :
    ∀ j, j < cfg.cols →
      (if j = w then 0
        else (cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h) j - 4) = h j := by
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjw : j = w
  · rw [if_pos hjw, hjw, hw0]
  · rw [if_neg hjw, foldl_update_nodup_eq cs hnodup (hcover j hj hjw)]
    omega

/-- **The fill-then-clear bag schedule is a closed cycle.** Starting from a
skyline with an empty well `w` and folding the schedule "drop a vertical-I in
every other in-field column, then a vertical-I in the well (clearing 4 lines)"
returns the board *exactly* to where it started: `skyline cfg h`. Fuses the
symbolic-rewrite landing point (`foldl_fill_then_clear`), the in-field profile
reset (`foldl_clear_profile_eq_in_field`), and skyline congruence
(`skyline_congr`). This is the first concrete closed-cycle witness — a genuine
board-level fixed point of a real piece sequence, the structural core needed to
discharge a `bagPhaseCarrier` `hstep` obligation (crux #77). -/
theorem foldl_fill_then_clear_fixed {cfg : GameConfig} {cs : List ℕ} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols)
    (hcs : ∀ c ∈ cs, c < cfg.cols ∧ c ≠ w)
    (hnodup : cs.Nodup)
    (hwnotmem : w ∉ cs) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcover : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → j ∈ cs) :
    (((cs.map (fun c => ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } : Placement)))
        ++ [({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w } : Placement)]).foldl
        (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h))
      = skyline cfg h := by
  rw [foldl_fill_then_clear hw hcs hwnotmem hw0 hcover]
  exact skyline_congr (foldl_clear_profile_eq_in_field hnodup hw0 hcover)

/-- **Canonical closed cycle from any empty-well skyline.** Specialises
`foldl_fill_then_clear_fixed` to the concrete column list "every in-field column
except the well `w`" (`((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w).toList`). All four
structural side conditions — distinctness, in-field-ness, well-exclusion, and
total coverage — discharge automatically from `Finset` membership facts, so the
only remaining hypotheses are `hw : w < cfg.cols` and an empty well `hw0`. This
is the ready-to-instantiate concrete bag schedule: it returns *any* skyline with
an empty well exactly to itself, the hstep witness shape for `bagPhaseCarrier`. -/
theorem foldl_fill_then_clear_fixed_all_cols {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ((((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w).toList.map
          (fun c => ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } : Placement)))
        ++ [({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w } : Placement)]).foldl
        (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)
      = skyline cfg h := by
  refine foldl_fill_then_clear_fixed hw ?_ ?_ ?_ hw0 ?_
  · intro c hc
    rw [Finset.mem_toList, Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range] at hc
    exact ⟨hc.2, hc.1⟩
  · exact Finset.nodup_toList _
  · rw [Finset.mem_toList, Finset.mem_erase]
    rintro ⟨hne, _⟩
    exact hne rfl
  · intro j hj hjw
    rw [Finset.mem_toList, Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]
    exact ⟨hjw, hj⟩

/-- **A closed cycle anchored at the start state.** Folding the fill-then-clear
schedule from `init`'s empty board returns to `init`'s empty board. Specialises
`foldl_fill_then_clear_fixed_all_cols` to the zero profile (`hw0` is `rfl` since
`(fun _ => 0) w = 0`) and rewrites both endpoints through
`init_board_eq_skyline`. This is the reachability-bridged closed cycle (M3) at
the board level: a *concrete, explicit* piece sequence that starts at the real
initial board and provably returns to it, ready to seed the bag-state and
`¬ isLost` bookkeeping of a full `TetrisSolvableValid` witness. -/
theorem foldl_fill_then_clear_init {cfg : GameConfig} {w : ℕ} (hw : w < cfg.cols) :
    ((((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w).toList.map
          (fun c => ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } : Placement)))
        ++ [({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w } : Placement)]).foldl
        (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) GameState.init.board
      = GameState.init.board := by
  rw [init_board_eq_skyline cfg]
  exact foldl_fill_then_clear_fixed_all_cols hw rfl

/-- **colHeight bridge for no-clear moves.** If the full move `applyStep` on a
skyline reduces to another skyline `skyline cfg h'` (the no-clear case), then any
per-column ceiling `h' j ≤ h j + d` on the resulting profile transfers verbatim
to the column heights of the post-move board. The colHeight companion to
`applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq`: it lifts each per-piece `place`-level height
debit (`colHeight_place_*_skyline_le`) to a bound on the *full* move, the form
the height-accounting half of a bag-phase potential consumes. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_le_of_skyline_eq {cfg : GameConfig} {h h' : ℕ → ℕ}
    {pl : Placement} {d : ℕ}
    (hstep : Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl = skyline cfg h')
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h' j ≤ h j + d)
    {j : ℕ} (hj : j < cfg.cols) :
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) j ≤ h j + d := by
  rw [hstep, colHeight_skyline hj]
  exact hbound j hj

/-! ### The scalar surface potential

A single number that tracks how tall the stack has grown across the real field:
the maximum column height over columns `0 … cfg.cols-1`. This is the scalar a
bag-phase potential balances — each ordinary placement lifts it by at most the
per-piece debit (`+1` for horizontal I, `+2` for the rest), and the once-per-bag
vertical-I well-clear repays `4`. Over a seven-piece bag the climb is at most
`14 < 20 = cfg.rows`, leaving room for the regulating clear. -/

/-- The tallest column height across the real field — the scalar surface potential. -/
def maxColHeight (cfg : GameConfig) (b : Board) : ℕ :=
  (Finset.range cfg.cols).sup (colHeight b)

/-- Every real column sits at or below the potential. -/
theorem colHeight_le_maxColHeight {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} {j : ℕ}
    (hj : j < cfg.cols) : colHeight b j ≤ maxColHeight cfg b :=
  Finset.le_sup (f := colHeight b) (Finset.mem_range.mpr hj)

/-- **Per-column debits aggregate to a potential debit.** If every real column of
`b` stands at most `d` above the corresponding column of `a`, then the surface
potential of `b` is at most `d` above that of `a`. The scalar accumulator behind
the per-piece height-debit table: chaining it across a bag turns the `+1/+2`
per-placement caps into a single bag-level height bound. -/
theorem maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le {cfg : GameConfig} {a b : Board} {d : ℕ}
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → colHeight b j ≤ colHeight a j + d) :
    maxColHeight cfg b ≤ maxColHeight cfg a + d := by
  unfold maxColHeight
  apply Finset.sup_le
  intro j hj
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hj
  have h1 : colHeight b j ≤ colHeight a j + d := hbound j hj
  have h2 : colHeight a j ≤ (Finset.range cfg.cols).sup (colHeight a) :=
    Finset.le_sup (f := colHeight a) (Finset.mem_range.mpr hj)
  omega

/-- **Scalar-potential non-loss bridge.** A well-formed board whose surface
potential `maxColHeight` is at most `cfg.rows` is not lost. This is the
potential-level (scalar) companion to the per-column `not_isLost_of_colHeight_le`:
where that lemma needs a column bound for *every* `j`, this reads non-loss off the
single scalar `maxColHeight` — but at the cost of a well-formedness hypothesis,
since `maxColHeight` only takes the supremum over the real columns `range cfg.cols`
and so says nothing about stray cells parked in a column `≥ cfg.cols`. With `WF`
ruling those out, every filled cell `p ∈ b` has `p.1 < cfg.cols`, so it sits
strictly below its column height (`lt_colHeight`), which is in turn capped by the
potential (`colHeight_le_maxColHeight`) and hence by `cfg.rows`. Carriers that
already track a `maxColHeight ≤ rows` budget (every `maxColHeight_le_rows_of_*`
face) can discharge their `¬ isLost` obligation through this bridge without
re-expanding to a per-column argument, provided the board is well-formed (every
skyline, and every `applyStep` of a valid placement onto one, is). -/
theorem not_isLost_of_maxColHeight_le {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hwf : WF cfg b) (hmax : maxColHeight cfg b ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ isLost cfg b := by
  rw [not_isLost_iff_forall_row_lt]
  intro p hp
  have hcol : p.1 < cfg.cols := hwf p hp
  have hlt : p.2 < colHeight b p.1 := lt_colHeight (show (p.1, p.2) ∈ b from hp)
  have hle : colHeight b p.1 ≤ maxColHeight cfg b := colHeight_le_maxColHeight hcol
  omega

/-- **Line clears never raise the surface potential.** The scalar (potential-level)
companion to the per-column `colHeight_clearLines_le`: since every column of
`clearLines cfg b` sits at or below its pre-clear height, the supremum over real
columns — the surface potential `maxColHeight` — can only drop. Like its
per-column parent this holds for *arbitrary* boards (holes included), needing no
skyline shape. This is the monotonicity any genuine bounded-height argument
leans on once *real* line clears (not just the uniform well-clear) enter the
picture: placements push the potential up, clears only pull it back down, so a
potential ceiling proved before a clear survives the clear for free. -/
theorem maxColHeight_clearLines_le {cfg : GameConfig} (b : Board) :
    maxColHeight cfg (clearLines cfg b) ≤ maxColHeight cfg b := by
  unfold maxColHeight
  apply Finset.sup_le
  intro j hj
  exact le_trans (colHeight_clearLines_le b j) (Finset.le_sup (f := colHeight b) hj)

/-- **Line clears never increase the cell count.** `clearLines` keeps only the
non-full cells (a `filter`, hence a subset of the board) and maps them down under
gravity (an `image`); a `Finset` image of a subset can only shrink cardinality. So
`(clearLines cfg b).card ≤ b.card` for an *arbitrary* board — holes included, no
skyline shape needed, unlike the exact `card_clearLines_skyline`. This is the
area-ledger counterpart of `maxColHeight_clearLines_le`: in the cell-count
(packing-budget) potential, every placement adds at most four cells and clears only
remove them, so any `card ≤ budget` ceiling proved before a clear survives it. -/
theorem card_clearLines_le {cfg : GameConfig} (b : Board) :
    (clearLines cfg b).card ≤ b.card := by
  unfold clearLines
  exact le_trans (Finset.card_image_le) (Finset.card_filter_le _ _)

/-- **General per-step card ledger: a placement adds at most four cells.** The
cell-count analogue of `maxColHeight_applyStep_le`, for an *arbitrary* board (no
skyline shape, no validity needed): one full move hard-drops the piece — `place`
adds exactly four cells (`count_place`) — then clears lines, which only remove cells
(`card_clearLines_le`). So `(pl.applyStep cfg b).card ≤ b.card + 4`. This is the
foundational `+4` step of the area (packing-budget) potential: the budget rises by
at most four per piece and falls by `cfg.cols` per cleared row, the exact ledger an
area-bounded carrier balances against the once-per-bag well clear. -/
theorem card_applyStep_le {cfg : GameConfig} (b : Board) (pl : Placement) :
    (pl.applyStep cfg b).card ≤ b.card + 4 := by
  have hplace : (pl.place b).card = b.card + 4 := Placement.count_place b pl
  unfold Placement.applyStep
  exact le_trans (card_clearLines_le (pl.place b)) (le_of_eq hplace)

/-- **Per-bag scalar card ledger.** Folding a list of placements onto a board raises
the cell count by at most `4` per placement. The area-ledger companion to the
per-step `card_applyStep_le` and the cell-count mirror of `maxColHeight_foldl_applyStep_le`
— and, unlike the height version, it needs *no* validity hypothesis, since the `+4`
card step holds for any placement on any board. This is the form an area-bounded
carrier consumes to account for a whole bag of drops before a scheduled line clear
(well clear or otherwise) pays the budget back down. -/
theorem card_foldl_applyStep_le {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement) :
    ∀ {b : Board},
      (pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b).card
        ≤ b.card + 4 * pls.length := by
  induction pls with
  | nil =>
    intro b
    simp
  | cons pl rest ih =>
    intro b
    simp only [List.foldl_cons, List.length_cons]
    have hstep := card_applyStep_le (cfg := cfg) b pl
    have hrest := ih (b := pl.applyStep cfg b)
    omega

/-- **Per-bag transient (prefix) card bound.** Every *prefix* of a placement list —
not just the final fold — keeps the cell count within `+4 * pls.length` of the start
(each hard drop adds at most four cells, clears only remove). The card analogue of the
height transient `maxColHeight_foldl_take_applyStep_le`, and the form the packing-ledger
route (`card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card`) consumes to keep `card ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows`
at *each* intermediate drop, not merely at bag endpoints. Unlike the height transient
(whose `+4 * 7 = 28` overruns `rows = 20`), the card budget has the full grid `cols * rows`
of headroom — a 28-cell bag growth sits comfortably under `200` — so this prefix bound is
non-vacuous across an entire 7-bag. Proof: the prefix is no longer than the whole list
(`length_take_le'`), so the validity-free endpoint growth bound covers it. -/
theorem card_foldl_take_applyStep_le {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement) (n : ℕ)
    {b : Board} :
    ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b).card
      ≤ b.card + 4 * pls.length := by
  have hbound := card_foldl_applyStep_le (cfg := cfg) (pls.take n) (b := b)
  have hlen : (pls.take n).length ≤ pls.length := List.length_take_le' n pls
  omega

/-- **Prefix card stays within the full grid.** The card analogue of the not-lost
transient `not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_of_colHeight_le`: given a single area budget
`b.card + 4 * pls.length ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows`, *every* prefix of the placement schedule
holds at most `cfg.cols * cfg.rows` cells — the board never overflows the playing field's
total area at any intermediate drop. This is the non-vacuous per-bag survival-accounting
face of the packing ledger: where the height transient `+4 * 7 = 28` overruns `rows = 20`
and goes vacuous over a full bag, the area budget has the whole grid (`200`) of headroom,
so a 7-bag's `28`-cell growth sits comfortably under the cap and this prefix invariant
genuinely holds across an entire bag. Proof: the prefix growth bound
(`card_foldl_take_applyStep_le`) chains with the budget by transitivity. -/
theorem card_foldl_take_applyStep_le_grid {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement)
    {b : Board} (hbudget : b.card + 4 * pls.length ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows) (n : ℕ) :
    ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b).card
      ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows := by
  have hbound := card_foldl_take_applyStep_le (cfg := cfg) pls n (b := b)
  omega

/-- **Net per-bag card ledger: growth then well-clear.** Composes the two halves of
the area accounting into a single inequality. Folding any list of placements `pls`
onto `b` grows the cell count by at most `4 · pls.length` (`card_foldl_applyStep_le`);
if that folded board happens to be a reserved-well skyline `skyline cfg h'` poised for
a clear (empty well `w`, neighbours all `≥ 4`), the vertical-I well clear refunds
exactly `4 · (cfg.cols − 1)` cells (`card_applyStep_vertI_well`). Chaining the two,
the post-clear cell count plus the refund is at most the pre-fold count plus the
growth — i.e. `card_after + 4·(cols−1) ≤ b.card + 4·length`. Rearranged, a bag of
`length ≤ cfg.cols − 1` placements followed by one well clear *strictly lowers* the
cell total, the descent a 5-bag period ledger (140 cells = 14 rows) accumulates into
a net-zero-height cycle. This is the abstract net step; instantiating `hfold` with a
concrete bag schedule is the remaining closure work. -/
theorem card_wellclear_after_foldl_le {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement)
    {b : Board} {h' : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hfold : pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b = skyline cfg h')
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwell : h' w = 0)
    (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h' j) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h')
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }).card + 4 * (cfg.cols - 1)
      ≤ b.card + 4 * pls.length := by
  have hgrow := card_foldl_applyStep_le (cfg := cfg) pls (b := b)
  rw [hfold] at hgrow
  have hledger := card_applyStep_vertI_well hw hwell hothers
  omega

/-- **A clear-capped bag does not grow the cell count.** The directly-iterable face
of `card_wellclear_after_foldl_le`: when the bag is short enough that its growth is
fully repaid by the single well clear — `pls.length ≤ cfg.cols − 1`, so
`4 · length ≤ 4 · (cfg.cols − 1)` — the post-clear cell total is at most the pre-fold
total. This is the *fixed-point* shape a packing-budget invariant maintains: if the
board entered the bag with `card ≤ K`, it leaves with `card ≤ K`, so a `card ≤ K`
ceiling survives an entire (capped) bag-plus-clear cycle unchanged. With the standard
ten-wide field a bag of up to nine placements clears flush; the seven-piece 7-bag
(length `7 ≤ 9`) is comfortably inside the cap, so this is the per-bag step a
`card ≤ cfg.cols · cfg.rows` atlas invariant closes on, once a schedule lands the
fold clear-ready. -/
theorem card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement)
    {b : Board} {h' : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hlen : pls.length ≤ cfg.cols - 1)
    (hfold : pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b = skyline cfg h')
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwell : h' w = 0)
    (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h' j) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h')
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }).card ≤ b.card := by
  have h := card_wellclear_after_foldl_le pls hfold hw hwell hothers
  omega

/-- **The full-grid area ceiling is a fixed point of a capped bag-plus-well-clear cycle.**
The directly-atlas-facing face of `card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card`: a board that *enters*
a bag already within the field's total area (`b.card ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows`) and runs a
clear-capped bag (`pls.length ≤ cfg.cols − 1`, so the single vertical-I well clear repays the
growth) *leaves* still within that same area ceiling. So the packing invariant
`card ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows` is self-maintaining across a whole bag cycle — the precise
fixed-point shape an area-bounded atlas carrier closes on, and (unlike the height ceiling,
whose `+4 * 7 = 28` overruns `rows = 20` mid-bag) genuinely non-vacuous because the `200`-cell
grid budget absorbs a 7-bag's growth before the clear refunds it. Proof: the post-clear count
is at most the pre-fold count (`card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card`), which is at most the
ceiling by hypothesis — transitivity via `omega`. -/
theorem card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_grid {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement)
    {b : Board} {h' : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hbudget : b.card ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows)
    (hlen : pls.length ≤ cfg.cols - 1)
    (hfold : pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b = skyline cfg h')
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwell : h' w = 0)
    (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h' j) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h')
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }).card ≤ cfg.cols * cfg.rows := by
  have h := card_wellclear_after_foldl_le_card pls hlen hfold hw hwell hothers
  omega

/-- **Scalar per-step potential ceiling.** Hard-dropping any *valid* piece raises
the surface potential `maxColHeight` by at most `4`: the drop offset never exceeds
the current potential (a valid placement only occupies real columns, each capped by
`maxColHeight` via `dropOffset_le_sup_colHeight`), and the piece's bounding box adds
at most four more rows (`colHeight_dropped_le`), while the line clears inside the
step only lower the result (`colHeight_applyStep_le_max`). This is the scalar
(potential-level) companion to the per-column `colHeight_applyStep_le_of_colHeight_le`:
the `+4` Lipschitz bound a clean potential argument threads through a bag, paired with
`maxColHeight_clearLines_le` (clears never raise it) and the `−4` well-clear descent
(`colHeight_applyStep_vertI_well_le`). -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_le {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} {pl : Placement}
    (hpl : pl.Valid cfg) :
    maxColHeight cfg (pl.applyStep cfg b) ≤ maxColHeight cfg b + 4 := by
  have hdrop : pl.dropOffset b ≤ maxColHeight cfg b := by
    refine le_trans (dropOffset_le_sup_colHeight b pl) ?_
    apply Finset.sup_le
    intro cell hcell
    exact colHeight_le_maxColHeight (hpl cell hcell)
  unfold maxColHeight
  apply Finset.sup_le
  intro j hj
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hj
  refine le_trans (colHeight_applyStep_le_max b pl j) (max_le ?_ ?_)
  · exact le_trans (colHeight_le_maxColHeight hj) (Nat.le_add_right (maxColHeight cfg b) 4)
  · exact le_trans (colHeight_dropped_le b pl j) (Nat.add_le_add_right hdrop 4)

/-- **One valid drop onto a low-enough board never loses — independent of shape.**
The general (non-skyline) one-step survival primitive: on a well-formed board whose
surface potential has four cells of headroom (`maxColHeight cfg b + 4 ≤ cfg.rows`),
hard-dropping *any* valid placement `pl` yields a board that is not lost. Two facts
chain: the drop raises the potential by at most four
(`maxColHeight_applyStep_le`, the `+4` Lipschitz bound), so the post-move potential
stays `≤ cfg.rows`; and a valid placement preserves well-formedness
(`Placement.applyStep_wf`), so the scalar-potential non-loss bridge
(`not_isLost_of_maxColHeight_le`) converts that potential cap into the actual
`¬ isLost` verdict. Crucially this needs **no** clean landing — the piece may gouge
holes on a rough surface, since a hole sits below the field and never trips the loss
predicate (which fires only when a cell reaches row `cfg.rows`). This is exactly the
survival obligation the flat∨notched carrier cannot meet by cleanliness after an
`S`/`Z` roughens the top: as long as a scheduled well-clear keeps the potential four
below the ceiling, the next adversary draw — whatever its shape — is survived for
free. The decoupling of *non-loss* (a pure height budget) from *surface repair* (the
skyline bookkeeping) the closure programme needs. -/
theorem not_isLost_applyStep_of_maxColHeight_add_four_le {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    {pl : Placement} (hwf : WF cfg b) (hv : pl.Valid cfg)
    (hbudget : maxColHeight cfg b + 4 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ isLost cfg (pl.applyStep cfg b) := by
  refine not_isLost_of_maxColHeight_le (Placement.applyStep_wf hwf hv) ?_
  exact le_trans (maxColHeight_applyStep_le hv) hbudget

/-- **Per-bag scalar potential bound.** Folding a list of valid placements onto a
board raises the scalar potential `maxColHeight` by at most `4` per placement.
This is the scalar (board-level) companion to the per-step `maxColHeight_applyStep_le`
and to the per-column `colHeight_foldl_applyStep_le`; it is the form the
`maxColHeight`-stated surface carriers consume directly when accounting for a
whole bag's worth of drops before a scheduled well-clear pays the height back. -/
theorem maxColHeight_foldl_applyStep_le {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement) :
    ∀ {b : Board}, (∀ pl ∈ pls, pl.Valid cfg) →
      maxColHeight cfg (pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b)
        ≤ maxColHeight cfg b + 4 * pls.length := by
  induction pls with
  | nil =>
    intro b _
    simp
  | cons pl rest ih =>
    intro b hvalid
    obtain ⟨hpl, hrest_valid⟩ := List.forall_mem_cons.mp hvalid
    simp only [List.foldl_cons, List.length_cons]
    have hstep := maxColHeight_applyStep_le (b := b) hpl
    have hrest := ih (b := pl.applyStep cfg b) hrest_valid
    omega

/-- **Well-formedness survives a whole bag of valid drops.** The fold companion to
`Placement.applyStep_wf`: folding any list of *valid* placements onto a well-formed
board yields a well-formed board. Each step adds only cells in real columns (a valid
placement lands inside `range cfg.cols`) and the line clear shifts cells straight
down (`clearLines_wf`), so the `p.1 < cfg.cols` invariant is preserved drop by drop;
the induction just threads `Placement.applyStep_wf` along the fold. This is the
well-formedness backbone the *general* (non-skyline) per-bag survival route needs:
the scalar non-loss bridge `not_isLost_of_maxColHeight_le` carries a `WF` hypothesis
(the potential `maxColHeight` only sees real columns), so to certify the bag-endpoint
board un-lost via `maxColHeight_foldl_applyStep_le` one must also know that endpoint
is well-formed — which is exactly this. Together they let a carrier reason about an
entire bag of arbitrary-shape drops on the height budget alone, never appealing to
skyline cleanliness. -/
theorem foldl_applyStep_wf {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement) :
    ∀ {b : Board}, WF cfg b → (∀ pl ∈ pls, pl.Valid cfg) →
      WF cfg (pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b) := by
  induction pls with
  | nil =>
    intro b hb _
    simpa using hb
  | cons pl rest ih =>
    intro b hb hvalid
    obtain ⟨hpl, hrest_valid⟩ := List.forall_mem_cons.mp hvalid
    simp only [List.foldl_cons]
    exact ih (Placement.applyStep_wf hb hpl) hrest_valid

/-- **Per-bag transient (prefix) potential bound.** Every *prefix* of a bag's
placement list — not just the final fold — keeps the scalar potential within
`+4 * pls.length` of the start. This is the transient companion to the endpoint
bound `maxColHeight_foldl_applyStep_le`: a per-bag survival schedule must keep the
board un-lost at *each* intermediate drop, so the carrier consumes this prefix
peak (any `take n`) rather than only the bag endpoint. Proof: `pls.take n` is a
subset of `pls` (so still all-valid) and no longer (`length_take_le'`), then the
endpoint bound on the prefix plus monotonicity in the length. -/
theorem maxColHeight_foldl_take_applyStep_le {cfg : GameConfig} (pls : List Placement)
    (hvalid : ∀ pl ∈ pls, pl.Valid cfg) (n : ℕ) {b : Board} :
    maxColHeight cfg ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) b)
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg b + 4 * pls.length := by
  have hsub : ∀ pl ∈ pls.take n, pl.Valid cfg :=
    fun pl hpl => hvalid pl (List.take_subset n pls hpl)
  have hbound := maxColHeight_foldl_applyStep_le (pls.take n) (b := b) hsub
  have hlen : (pls.take n).length ≤ pls.length := List.length_take_le' n pls
  omega

/-- **The potential of a skyline is the supremum of its profile.** On real
columns the column height *is* the profile (`colHeight_skyline`), so the scalar
potential collapses to `sup` of `h` over `range cfg.cols`. This is the bridge
that lets every profile-level height debit (`+1/+2` per placement) drive the
board-level potential directly, without re-deriving column heights each step. -/
theorem maxColHeight_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} :
    maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) = (Finset.range cfg.cols).sup h := by
  unfold maxColHeight
  apply Finset.sup_congr rfl
  intro j hj
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hj
  exact colHeight_skyline hj

/-- **A skyline's surface potential is at most its cell count.** A skyline has no
holes, so `maxColHeight` is the supremum of the column profile (`maxColHeight_skyline`)
while the cell count is its sum (`card_skyline`); over `ℕ` a supremum never exceeds the
sum (`Finset.single_le_sum`). So `maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) ≤ (skyline cfg h).card`
— the forward (area-bounds-height) companion to `card_skyline_le_of_maxColHeight_le`: any
cell-count ceiling the packing ledger maintains immediately caps the surface height,
closing the loop between the area potential and the loss predicate `colHeight ≤ rows`. -/
theorem maxColHeight_le_card_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} (h : ℕ → ℕ) :
    maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) ≤ (skyline cfg h).card := by
  rw [maxColHeight_skyline, card_skyline]
  apply Finset.sup_le
  intro j hj
  exact Finset.single_le_sum (f := h) (fun i _ => Nat.zero_le _) hj

/-- **A pointwise-bounded skyline has bounded surface potential.** If every real
column's profile height is at most `B`, the scalar surface potential `maxColHeight`
is at most `B`. The general profile-to-potential bound (`maxColHeight_skyline`
followed by `Finset.sup_le`); the bounded-skyline (`B := cfg.rows`) and flat-top
(constant profile) carriers are its instances, so a potential-indexed carrier can
read its scalar ceiling straight off the profile cap. -/
theorem maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hB : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) :
    maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) ≤ B := by
  rw [maxColHeight_skyline]
  apply Finset.sup_le
  intro j hj
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hj
  exact hB j hj

/-- **Per-column readback from a skyline ceiling.** The exact inverse of
`maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall`: if a skyline's scalar surface potential is at most `B`, then
every real column's profile height is individually at most `B`. Proof: rewrite `h j` back to
`colHeight (skyline cfg h) j` (`colHeight_skyline`), which sits below the sup `maxColHeight`
(`colHeight_le_maxColHeight`), itself `≤ B`. Lets a composed packing layer recover the pointwise
ceiling its next tile needs (`∀ j, G1 j ≤ rows`) directly from the previous tile's
`maxColHeight (skyline G1) ≤ rows`, without re-deriving the per-column bound. -/
theorem forall_le_of_maxColHeight_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hmax : maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) ≤ B) :
    ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B := by
  intro j hj
  calc h j = Board.colHeight (skyline cfg h) j := (colHeight_skyline hj).symm
    _ ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) := colHeight_le_maxColHeight hj
    _ ≤ B := hmax

/-- **Skyline transient potential bound.** The scalar (carrier-return) twin of
`not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline`: from any skyline with profile bounded by
`B`, every prefix of a valid placement schedule keeps the surface potential within
`B + 4 * pls.length`. Pairs with that survival lemma — one bounds loss, this bounds
height — so a per-bag closure reads BOTH off the same headroom `B + 4 * pls.length`:
the bag endpoint is un-lost *and* its potential is capped, the height precondition a
bounded-skyline carrier needs to re-admit the endpoint. Compose of
`maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall` (start ≤ B) with the transient bound
`maxColHeight_foldl_take_applyStep_le`. -/
theorem maxColHeight_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hB : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (pls : List Placement)
    (hvalid : ∀ pl ∈ pls, pl.Valid cfg) (n : ℕ) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h))
      ≤ B + 4 * pls.length := by
  have hstart := maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall hB
  have hbound := maxColHeight_foldl_take_applyStep_le pls hvalid n (b := skyline cfg h)
  omega

/-- **Prefix peak is bounded by the endpoint peak when the reserved well stays empty.**
The tight per-prefix surface bound the reserved-well packing schedule needs. The full fold
splits as `take n ++ drop n`, so the endpoint board is the `drop n` tail folded *onto* the
prefix board (`List.foldl_append` plus `List.take_append_drop`). Because the prefix fold also
keeps the well `w` empty (`colHeight_foldl_applyStep_skyline_well_eq_zero` on `take n`) and the
`drop n` tail still dodges that well, the fold-level height floor
(`colHeight_le_foldl_applyStep_of_well_empty`) embeds every column of the prefix board into the
endpoint board: `colHeight prefix j ≤ colHeight endpoint j`. Lifting that pointwise embedding
through `maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le` with `d = 0` caps the prefix surface potential
by the endpoint potential — no loose `+4`-per-placement slack. This is the brick that converts
an endpoint height cap (e.g. `base + 4` for the repack-raise) into a transient every-step peak
bound, the missing tight ingredient for a per-prefix not-lost certificate. -/
theorem maxColHeight_foldl_take_le_foldl_of_well_empty {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0) (pls : List Placement)
    (havoid : ∀ pl ∈ pls, ∀ cell ∈ pl.shapeUp, pl.col + cell.1 ≠ w) (n : ℕ) :
    maxColHeight cfg ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h))
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  have hsplit : pls.foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)
      = (pls.drop n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc)
          ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)) := by
    rw [← List.foldl_append, List.take_append_drop]
  have hwell : colHeight ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc)
      (skyline cfg h)) w = 0 :=
    colHeight_foldl_applyStep_skyline_well_eq_zero hw hw0 (pls.take n)
      (fun pl hpl => havoid pl (List.take_subset n pls hpl))
  have havoid_drop : ∀ pl ∈ pls.drop n, ∀ cell ∈ pl.shapeUp, pl.col + cell.1 ≠ w :=
    fun pl hpl => havoid pl (List.drop_subset n pls hpl)
  rw [hsplit]
  have h0 : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols →
      colHeight ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)) j
        ≤ colHeight ((pls.drop n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc)
            ((pls.take n).foldl (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h))) j + 0 := by
    intro j hj
    rw [Nat.add_zero]
    exact colHeight_le_foldl_applyStep_of_well_empty hw j (pls.drop n) havoid_drop _ hwell
  have hfin := maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le (d := 0) h0
  rwa [Nat.add_zero] at hfin

/-- **A uniform floor clear drops the surface potential by the cleared depth.**
Clearing `m` full floor rows of a skyline lowers every column by `m`
(`clearLines_skyline`), so the potential — the `sup` of the profile
(`maxColHeight_skyline`) — falls to at most `maxColHeight - m`. The quantified
strengthening of the general `maxColHeight_clearLines_le` (which only gives `≤`
with no decrement) in the skyline case: it is the monotone *decrease* a potential
cycle leans on, turning the `+2`-per-piece placement debits into a net drop once
the bag's clears land. -/
theorem maxColHeight_clearLines_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {m : ℕ}
    (hm : ∀ j < cfg.cols, m ≤ h j) (hm0 : ∃ j < cfg.cols, h j = m) :
    maxColHeight cfg (Board.clearLines cfg (skyline cfg h))
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) - m := by
  rw [clearLines_skyline hm hm0, maxColHeight_skyline, maxColHeight_skyline]
  apply Finset.sup_le
  intro j hj
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hj
  have hle : h j ≤ (Finset.range cfg.cols).sup h :=
    Finset.le_sup (f := h) (Finset.mem_range.mpr hj)
  omega

/-- **A potential ceiling caps the cell budget.** If a skyline's surface potential
is at most `M`, every column stands at most `M` high (`colHeight_le_maxColHeight`
through `colHeight_skyline`), so summing over the `cfg.cols` columns
(`card_skyline`) bounds the cell count by `cfg.cols * M`. This is the converse
bridge to the packing lemmas: where `exists_colHeight_le_of_card_le` turns a
*card* budget into a low landing site, this turns the *potential* invariant a
carrier actually tracks into that very card budget — so a `maxColHeight ≤ rows - 4`
ceiling feeds a guaranteed safe landing column straight through. -/
theorem card_skyline_le_of_maxColHeight_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {M : ℕ}
    (hM : maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) ≤ M) :
    (skyline cfg h).card ≤ cfg.cols * M := by
  rw [card_skyline]
  have hb : ∀ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, h j ≤ M := by
    intro j hj
    rw [Finset.mem_range] at hj
    have h1 : colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ M :=
      le_trans (colHeight_le_maxColHeight hj) hM
    rwa [colHeight_skyline hj] at h1
  calc ∑ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, h j
      ≤ ∑ _j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, M := Finset.sum_le_sum hb
    _ = cfg.cols * M := by rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_range, smul_eq_mul]

/-- **Any well-formed board's cell count is capped by its bounding box.** Every filled
cell `(x, y)` of a `WF` board has `x < cfg.cols` (well-formedness) and
`y < colHeight b x ≤ maxColHeight cfg b` (`lt_colHeight`, then `colHeight_le_maxColHeight`),
so the board embeds in the `cfg.cols × maxColHeight cfg b` grid and its card is at most
`cfg.cols * maxColHeight cfg b`. The general, hole-tolerant companion to the skyline-only
`card_skyline_le_of_maxColHeight_le`: it caps the area of ANY intermediate board a bag passes
through — overhangs and buried holes included — by its surface potential, the area bound a
potential-tracking carrier needs precisely on the off-skyline boards
(`colHeight_applyStep_le_of_well_empty_of_surface` flagged as the genuine hard case) where the
skyline area identity no longer applies. -/
theorem card_le_mul_maxColHeight {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} (hwf : WF cfg b) :
    b.card ≤ cfg.cols * maxColHeight cfg b := by
  have hsub : b ⊆ Finset.range cfg.cols ×ˢ Finset.range (maxColHeight cfg b) := by
    rintro ⟨x, y⟩ hp
    rw [Finset.mem_product, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_range]
    have hx : x < cfg.cols := hwf (x, y) hp
    refine ⟨hx, ?_⟩
    have h1 : y < colHeight b x := lt_colHeight hp
    have h2 : colHeight b x ≤ maxColHeight cfg b := colHeight_le_maxColHeight hx
    omega
  calc b.card ≤ (Finset.range cfg.cols ×ˢ Finset.range (maxColHeight cfg b)).card :=
        Finset.card_le_card hsub
    _ = cfg.cols * maxColHeight cfg b := by
        rw [Finset.card_product, Finset.card_range, Finset.card_range]

/-- **Each row of a well-formed board holds at most `cfg.cols` cells.** The row-`r`
fiber `b.filter (·.2 = r)` injects into the cells `(c, r)` with `c < cfg.cols`
(`Board.row`) by well-formedness, so its card is bounded by `cfg.cols`. This is the
per-row half of the forced-full-row pigeonhole: summed over the `cfg.rows` in-field
rows it caps `b.card` by `cfg.rows * cfg.cols`, and (contrapositive) an over-full
board whose card exceeds `cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1)` must contain a completely filled
row — the saturation lever that forces a deliberate line clear in the carrier. -/
theorem card_filter_row_le_cols {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} (hwf : WF cfg b) (r : ℕ) :
    (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card ≤ cfg.cols := by
  have hsub : b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r) ⊆
      (Finset.range cfg.cols).image (fun c => (c, r)) := by
    intro p hp
    rw [Finset.mem_filter] at hp
    obtain ⟨hpb, hpr⟩ := hp
    rw [Finset.mem_image]
    refine ⟨p.1, ?_, ?_⟩
    · rw [Finset.mem_range]; exact hwf p hpb
    · rw [← hpr]
  calc (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card
      ≤ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).image (fun c => (c, r))).card := Finset.card_le_card hsub
    _ ≤ (Finset.range cfg.cols).card := Finset.card_image_le
    _ = cfg.cols := Finset.card_range cfg.cols

/-- **A non-full row holds strictly fewer than `cfg.cols` cells.** When `¬ isFull cfg b r`
exhibits a column `c0 < cfg.cols` with `(c0, r) ∉ b`, the row-`r` fiber `b.filter (·.2 = r)`
is a STRICT subset of `Board.row` (it embeds in the `cfg.cols` cells `(c, r)` but misses
`(c0, r)`), so its card is `< cfg.cols`. The strict refinement of `card_filter_row_le_cols`
that drives the forced-full-row pigeonhole: were NO in-field row full, every fiber would hold
`≤ cfg.cols - 1`, capping `b.card ≤ cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1)`, so (contrapositive) an over-full
board must contain a completely filled row. Under the reserved-empty-well model no row
auto-completes, so this reframes as: non-well saturation forces the player to spend the
per-bag well-clear vertical I. -/
theorem card_filter_row_lt_cols_of_not_isFull {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hwf : WF cfg b) {r : ℕ} (hnf : ¬ isFull cfg b r) :
    (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card < cfg.cols := by
  unfold isFull at hnf
  push_neg at hnf
  obtain ⟨c0, hc0range, hc0notin⟩ := hnf
  have hrow : b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r) ⊆
      (Finset.range cfg.cols).image (fun c => (c, r)) := by
    intro p hp
    rw [Finset.mem_filter] at hp
    obtain ⟨hpb, hpr⟩ := hp
    rw [Finset.mem_image]
    exact ⟨p.1, by rw [Finset.mem_range]; exact hwf p hpb, by rw [← hpr]⟩
  have hss : b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r) ⊂
      (Finset.range cfg.cols).image (fun c => (c, r)) := by
    rw [Finset.ssubset_iff_of_subset hrow]
    refine ⟨(c0, r), ?_, ?_⟩
    · rw [Finset.mem_image]; exact ⟨c0, hc0range, rfl⟩
    · rw [Finset.mem_filter]
      rintro ⟨hin, -⟩
      exact hc0notin hin
  calc (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card
      < ((Finset.range cfg.cols).image (fun c => (c, r))).card := Finset.card_lt_card hss
    _ ≤ (Finset.range cfg.cols).card := Finset.card_image_le
    _ = cfg.cols := Finset.card_range cfg.cols

/-- **Saturation forces a full row.** A well-formed, in-field board holding more than
`cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1)` cells must contain a completely filled row. Proof by the
per-row pigeonhole: were every in-field row `r < cfg.rows` non-full, each fiber would
hold `≤ cfg.cols - 1` cells (`card_filter_row_lt_cols_of_not_isFull`), so the fiberwise
sum (`Finset.card_eq_sum_card_fiberwise` over `Finset.range cfg.rows`) caps
`b.card ≤ cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1)`, contradicting the hypothesis. This is the saturation
lever for the well-clear carrier: combined with the area cap `card_le_mul_maxColHeight`, a
board whose surface is driven high enough is FORCED to contain a clearable row. In the
reserved-empty-well model the well column never fills, so the forced full row lives in the
non-well subgrid and certifies that a deliberate well-clear becomes available. -/
theorem exists_isFull_of_card_gt {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hwf : WF cfg b) (hif : ∀ p ∈ b, p.2 < cfg.rows)
    (hcard : cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1) < b.card) :
    ∃ r < cfg.rows, isFull cfg b r := by
  by_contra hcon
  push_neg at hcon
  have hfib : b.card = ∑ r ∈ Finset.range cfg.rows, (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card := by
    apply Finset.card_eq_sum_card_fiberwise (f := fun p : ℕ × ℕ => p.2)
    intro p hp
    exact Finset.mem_range.mpr (hif p hp)
  have hbound : ∑ r ∈ Finset.range cfg.rows, (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card
      ≤ ∑ _r ∈ Finset.range cfg.rows, (cfg.cols - 1) := by
    apply Finset.sum_le_sum
    intro r hr
    rw [Finset.mem_range] at hr
    have hlt : (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card < cfg.cols :=
      card_filter_row_lt_cols_of_not_isFull hwf (hcon r hr)
    omega
  rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_range, smul_eq_mul] at hbound
  rw [hfib] at hcard
  exact absurd (lt_of_lt_of_le hcard hbound) (lt_irrefl _)

/-- **Saturation over an arbitrary column set forces a full row.** Generalises
`exists_isFull_of_card_gt` from the full width to any finite column set `S`: if every cell
of `b` lies in a column of `S` and below `cfg.rows`, and `b` holds more than
`cfg.rows * (S.card - 1)` cells, then some in-field row `r` has ALL of `S` filled. The
reserved-empty-well instance takes `S = (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w` (the non-well
columns): a board never fills the empty well, so the full-width count caps at
`cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1)` and the full-width lever never fires; but over the non-well
subgrid the count can reach that cap, and exceeding `cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 2)` forces a
non-well row whose every column is filled — the row the player clears by dropping the
bag's vertical I into the well. Proof: by the per-row fiber pigeonhole over `S`, each
non-`S`-full row holds `≤ S.card - 1` cells, so the fiberwise sum would cap `b.card`,
contradicting the hypothesis. -/
theorem exists_full_row_over_cols {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} {S : Finset ℕ}
    (hcols : ∀ p ∈ b, p.1 ∈ S) (hif : ∀ p ∈ b, p.2 < cfg.rows)
    (hcard : cfg.rows * (S.card - 1) < b.card) :
    ∃ r < cfg.rows, ∀ c ∈ S, (c, r) ∈ b := by
  by_contra hcon
  push_neg at hcon
  have hfib : b.card = ∑ r ∈ Finset.range cfg.rows, (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card := by
    apply Finset.card_eq_sum_card_fiberwise (f := fun p : ℕ × ℕ => p.2)
    intro p hp
    exact Finset.mem_range.mpr (hif p hp)
  have hbound : ∑ r ∈ Finset.range cfg.rows, (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card
      ≤ ∑ _r ∈ Finset.range cfg.rows, (S.card - 1) := by
    apply Finset.sum_le_sum
    intro r hr
    rw [Finset.mem_range] at hr
    obtain ⟨c0, hc0S, hc0notin⟩ := hcon r hr
    have hsub : b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r) ⊆ S.image (fun c => (c, r)) := by
      intro p hp
      rw [Finset.mem_filter] at hp
      obtain ⟨hpb, hpr⟩ := hp
      rw [Finset.mem_image]
      exact ⟨p.1, hcols p hpb, by rw [← hpr]⟩
    have hss : b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r) ⊂ S.image (fun c => (c, r)) := by
      rw [Finset.ssubset_iff_of_subset hsub]
      refine ⟨(c0, r), ?_, ?_⟩
      · rw [Finset.mem_image]; exact ⟨c0, hc0S, rfl⟩
      · rw [Finset.mem_filter]
        rintro ⟨hin, -⟩
        exact hc0notin hin
    have hlt : (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card < S.card :=
      calc (b.filter (fun p => p.2 = r)).card
          < (S.image (fun c => (c, r))).card := Finset.card_lt_card hss
        _ ≤ S.card := Finset.card_image_le
    omega
  rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_range, smul_eq_mul] at hbound
  rw [hfib] at hcard
  exact absurd (lt_of_lt_of_le hcard hbound) (lt_irrefl _)

/-- **The non-well subgrid version of the saturation lever.** Instantiates
`exists_full_row_over_cols` at `S = (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w` (the `cfg.cols - 1`
non-well columns): for a board whose cells avoid an empty well column `w` and stay in-field,
holding more than `cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 2)` cells forces an in-field row in which every
non-well column is filled. This is the row a well-based strategy clears by dropping the
bag's vertical I into `w`. Unlike the full-width `exists_isFull_of_card_gt` (whose
`cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1)` threshold the empty well makes unreachable), this threshold IS
reachable: the non-well region holds up to `cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 1)` cells. -/
theorem exists_full_nonwell_row {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwf : WF cfg b) (hwell : ∀ p ∈ b, p.1 ≠ w)
    (hif : ∀ p ∈ b, p.2 < cfg.rows)
    (hcard : cfg.rows * (cfg.cols - 2) < b.card) :
    ∃ r < cfg.rows, ∀ c ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w, (c, r) ∈ b := by
  have hScard : ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w).card = cfg.cols - 1 := by
    rw [Finset.card_erase_of_mem (Finset.mem_range.mpr hw), Finset.card_range]
  refine exists_full_row_over_cols ?_ hif ?_
  · intro p hp
    rw [Finset.mem_erase]
    exact ⟨hwell p hp, Finset.mem_range.mpr (hwf p hp)⟩
  · rw [hScard]
    have heq : cfg.cols - 1 - 1 = cfg.cols - 2 := by omega
    rw [heq]
    exact hcard

/-- **Well/row-fullness decomposition.** With the well column `w < cfg.cols`, a
row `r` is `isFull` exactly when it is filled across every non-well column
together with the well cell `(w, r)` itself. This is the bridge from the
non-well pigeonhole (`exists_full_nonwell_row`, whose conclusion is fullness
*off* the well) to genuine `isFull` and hence to the existing `fullRows` and
`clearLines` machinery: a row that the player has saturated everywhere except
the reserved well becomes full the instant the well cell is topped off (the
deliberate vertical-I drop), at which point it clears. -/
theorem isFull_iff_nonwell_and_well {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (r : ℕ) :
    isFull cfg b r ↔
      (∀ c ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w, (c, r) ∈ b) ∧ (w, r) ∈ b := by
  unfold isFull
  constructor
  · intro h
    exact ⟨fun c hc => h c (Finset.mem_of_mem_erase hc), h w (Finset.mem_range.mpr hw)⟩
  · rintro ⟨hnw, hwell⟩ c hc
    rcases eq_or_ne c w with rfl | hne
    · exact hwell
    · exact hnw c (Finset.mem_erase.mpr ⟨hne, hc⟩)

/-- **A line clear strictly reduces occupancy.** If a well-formed board (with at
least one column) has any full row, then clearing lines strictly drops the cell
count: that full row alone contributes `cfg.cols ≥ 1` cells removed by gravity.
The strict companion to `card_clearLines_le`, obtained from the exact ledger
`clearLines_count_add` (`b.count = (clearLines b).count + cols * |fullRows|`).
This is the capstone of the saturation gives a clear chain
(`exists_isFull_of_card_gt`, `isFull_iff_nonwell_and_well`): it converts the mere
existence of a full row into measurable progress, the strict decrease any
bounded-occupancy or termination argument needs to rule out an unbounded stack. -/
theorem card_clearLines_lt_of_isFull {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hwf : WF cfg b) (hcols : 0 < cfg.cols) {r : ℕ} (hr : isFull cfg b r) :
    (clearLines cfg b).card < b.card := by
  have hmem : r ∈ fullRows cfg b := by
    rw [mem_fullRows_iff]
    exact ⟨Finset.mem_image.mpr ⟨(0, r), hr 0 (Finset.mem_range.mpr hcols), rfl⟩, hr⟩
  have hpos : 0 < (fullRows cfg b).card := Finset.card_pos.mpr ⟨r, hmem⟩
  have hacc := clearLines_count_add hwf
  unfold Board.count at hacc
  have hmpos : 0 < cfg.cols * (fullRows cfg b).card := Nat.mul_pos hcols hpos
  omega

/-- **The empty board has zero surface potential.** The scalar potential of the
empty board is `0`: the zero skyline caps every column at `0`, so the `sup` is
`0`. This is the `hinit` fact for any `maxColHeight`-indexed carrier — a
potential invariant `maxColHeight ≤ k` holds initially for free, with the full
`k` rows of slack available to the first bag. -/
theorem maxColHeight_empty (cfg : GameConfig) : maxColHeight cfg Board.empty = 0 := by
  have h : maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg (fun _ => 0)) ≤ 0 :=
    maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall (fun _ _ => Nat.zero_le 0)
  rw [skyline_zero_eq_empty] at h
  exact Nat.le_zero.mp h

/-- **The scalar potential resets to zero.** The `maxColHeight` analogue of
`colHeight_clearLines_eq_zero_of_forall_isFull`: clearing a board whose every cell
lies in a full row wipes it to `∅` (`clearLines_eq_empty_of_forall_isFull`), whose
surface potential is `0` (`maxColHeight_empty`). This is the form the *scalar*
potential route consumes to re-establish a `maxColHeight ≤ k` invariant from scratch
after a slab clear — the single number the whole carrier family now tracks
(`maxColHeight_le_rows_of_is*`) drops all the way back to `0`, returning the full
ceiling of slack to the next bag. -/
theorem maxColHeight_clearLines_eq_zero_of_forall_isFull {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (h : ∀ p ∈ b, isFull cfg b p.2) :
    maxColHeight cfg (clearLines cfg b) = 0 := by
  rw [clearLines_eq_empty_of_forall_isFull h]
  exact maxColHeight_empty cfg

/-- **A below-average column always exists (the packing seed).** For any board
some real column's height is at most the average: `cfg.cols * colHeight b j` is at
most the total column-height mass `∑ colHeight`. The minimum-height column witnesses
it (`Finset.exists_min_image`), since `cols` copies of the minimum sum to no more than
the true total. For a skyline this total is the cell count (`card_skyline`), so the
shallowest column sits at height `≤ card / cols` — the low landing site a piece can
drop into without the worst-case `+2` debit. This is the existence backbone the
adversarial closure needs: the worst-case per-piece height debit is too lossy under
adversarial order, but a guaranteed below-average landing column keeps the surface
growing at the *average* (`cells / cols`) rate instead. -/
theorem exists_colHeight_mul_le_sum {cfg : GameConfig} (b : Board) :
    ∃ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols,
      cfg.cols * colHeight b j ≤ ∑ k ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, colHeight b k := by
  obtain ⟨j, hj, hmin⟩ := Finset.exists_min_image (Finset.range cfg.cols) (colHeight b)
    ⟨0, Finset.mem_range.mpr cfg.cols_pos⟩
  refine ⟨j, hj, ?_⟩
  calc cfg.cols * colHeight b j
      = ∑ _k ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, colHeight b j := by
        rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_range, smul_eq_mul]
    _ ≤ ∑ k ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, colHeight b k :=
        Finset.sum_le_sum (fun k hk => hmin k hk)

/-- **The shallowest column of a skyline sits at most at `card / cols`.** The
skyline specialization of `exists_colHeight_mul_le_sum`: since a skyline's total
column-height mass is exactly its cell count (`card_skyline`), some real column `j`
satisfies `cfg.cols * colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ (skyline cfg h).card`. With the
cell count as the conserved packing budget (each placement adds `4`, each row clear
removes `cols`), this pins a guaranteed low landing column purely from how full the
board is — the bridge from the area potential to a concrete drop height. -/
theorem exists_colHeight_mul_le_card_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} (h : ℕ → ℕ) :
    ∃ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols,
      cfg.cols * colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ (skyline cfg h).card := by
  obtain ⟨j, hj, hle⟩ := exists_colHeight_mul_le_sum (cfg := cfg) (skyline cfg h)
  refine ⟨j, hj, ?_⟩
  rw [card_skyline]
  refine hle.trans (le_of_eq ?_)
  apply Finset.sum_congr rfl
  intro k hk
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hk
  exact colHeight_skyline hk

/-- **A below-average *non-well* column always exists.** The well-aware refinement
of `exists_colHeight_mul_le_card_skyline`: minimising column height over the
`cfg.cols - 1` real columns *other than* the reserved well `w`, some such column `j`
satisfies `(cfg.cols - 1) * colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ (skyline cfg h).card`.
Because an ordinary (non-clearing) placement must avoid the reserved well, the
landing column has to be drawn from the non-well columns; this lemma supplies the
guaranteed low landing site *within that restricted set* — the averaging seed the
reserved-well packing closure consumes, denominator `cfg.cols - 1` rather than
`cfg.cols` since the well is excluded. -/
theorem exists_ne_well_colHeight_mul_le_card_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} (h : ℕ → ℕ)
    {w : ℕ} (hw : w < cfg.cols) (h2 : 2 ≤ cfg.cols) :
    ∃ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, j ≠ w ∧
      (cfg.cols - 1) * colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ (skyline cfg h).card := by
  have hw' : w ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hw
  have hne : ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w).Nonempty := by
    rw [← Finset.card_pos, Finset.card_erase_of_mem hw', Finset.card_range]
    omega
  obtain ⟨j, hjmem, hmin⟩ :=
    Finset.exists_min_image ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w)
      (colHeight (skyline cfg h)) hne
  refine ⟨j, Finset.mem_of_mem_erase hjmem, Finset.ne_of_mem_erase hjmem, ?_⟩
  calc (cfg.cols - 1) * colHeight (skyline cfg h) j
      = ∑ _k ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w, colHeight (skyline cfg h) j := by
        rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_erase_of_mem hw', Finset.card_range,
          smul_eq_mul]
    _ ≤ ∑ k ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w, colHeight (skyline cfg h) k :=
        Finset.sum_le_sum (fun k hk => hmin k hk)
    _ ≤ ∑ k ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, colHeight (skyline cfg h) k :=
        Finset.sum_le_sum_of_subset (Finset.erase_subset _ _)
    _ = (skyline cfg h).card := by
        rw [card_skyline]
        exact Finset.sum_congr rfl
          (fun k hk => colHeight_skyline (Finset.mem_range.mp hk))

/-- **A board at most `M`-full on average has a column of height `≤ M`.** Dividing
the cell-count packing bound (`exists_colHeight_mul_le_card_skyline`) through by the
positive column count: if a skyline holds at most `cfg.cols * M` cells, some real
column sits at height `≤ M`. This is the directly usable low-landing-site existence
form — a one-wide vertical-I dropped into that column tops out at `≤ M + 4`, so a
packing invariant `card ≤ cfg.cols * (cfg.rows - 4)` guarantees a safe I landing
that never breaches the ceiling. -/
theorem exists_colHeight_le_of_card_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {M : ℕ}
    (hcard : (skyline cfg h).card ≤ cfg.cols * M) :
    ∃ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ M := by
  obtain ⟨j, hj, hle⟩ := exists_colHeight_mul_le_card_skyline (cfg := cfg) h
  refine ⟨j, hj, ?_⟩
  have hmul : cfg.cols * colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ cfg.cols * M := hle.trans hcard
  exact Nat.le_of_mul_le_mul_left hmul cfg.cols_pos

/-- **A non-well low-landing column from a non-well card budget.** The well-aware
companion to `exists_colHeight_le_of_card_le`: dividing the non-well averaging bound
(`exists_ne_well_colHeight_mul_le_card_skyline`) through by the positive count
`cfg.cols - 1`, a skyline holding at most `(cfg.cols - 1) * M` cells has some real
column `j ≠ w` at height `≤ M`. This is the directly usable form for the reserved-well
carrier: it exhibits a low landing site that *avoids* the reserved well, so an ordinary
(non-clearing) piece can be dropped there without ever filling the column the well-clear
needs kept empty. -/
theorem exists_ne_well_colHeight_le_of_card_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w M : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (h2 : 2 ≤ cfg.cols)
    (hcard : (skyline cfg h).card ≤ (cfg.cols - 1) * M) :
    ∃ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, j ≠ w ∧ colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ M := by
  obtain ⟨j, hj, hjw, hle⟩ := exists_ne_well_colHeight_mul_le_card_skyline h hw h2
  refine ⟨j, hj, hjw, ?_⟩
  have hmul : (cfg.cols - 1) * colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ (cfg.cols - 1) * M :=
    hle.trans hcard
  exact Nat.le_of_mul_le_mul_left hmul (by omega)

/-- **A non-full skyline always has an empty column (a well).** If a skyline holds
fewer than `cfg.cols` cells, some real column is empty: were every column at height
`≥ 1`, the cell count would be `≥ cfg.cols`. This bridges the *area* packing budget
to the *reserved-well* carrier's `h w = 0` requirement — keeping the board strictly
below one full row's worth of cells guarantees a free well column for the next
no-clear drop, with no dependence on where the mass sits. -/
theorem exists_colHeight_eq_zero_of_card_lt {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    (hcard : (skyline cfg h).card < cfg.cols) :
    ∃ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, colHeight (skyline cfg h) j = 0 := by
  by_contra hcon
  push_neg at hcon
  have hcols_le : cfg.cols ≤ (skyline cfg h).card := by
    rw [card_skyline]
    calc cfg.cols
        = ∑ _j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, 1 := by
          rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_range, smul_eq_mul, mul_one]
      _ ≤ ∑ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, h j := by
          apply Finset.sum_le_sum
          intro j hj
          have hne := hcon j hj
          rw [colHeight_skyline (Finset.mem_range.mp hj)] at hne
          exact Nat.one_le_iff_ne_zero.mpr hne
  omega

/-- **Surface-potential reset for the well clear.** The `maxColHeight`-level
companion to `colHeight_applyStep_vertI_well_le`: dropping the vertical I into the
reserved well (every other column at least four high, all under the ceiling) clears
four rows and pins the entire scalar surface potential to `cfg.rows - 4`. This is the
`-4` regulator that complements the `+2` ordinary-move debits: however much a bag of
ordinary placements roughens and raises the surface, the closing well clear resets the
potential to four full rows of slack below the ceiling. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_vertI_well_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwell : h w = 0)
    (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h j)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }) ≤ cfg.rows - 4 := by
  unfold maxColHeight
  apply Finset.sup_le
  intro j hj
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hj
  exact colHeight_applyStep_vertI_well_le hw hwell hothers hbound hj

/-! ### Reserved-well move witness (O, the template)

The first packaged `applyStep` move: the O piece dropped on a flat pair of real
columns `c, c+1`, in the presence of a **reserved empty column** `w` (`h w = 0`,
distinct from the landing columns). The reserved column guarantees the result
keeps a real column empty, so `applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq` fires and the full
move reduces to the bare `place_O_skyline` drop: profile `(k, k) ↦ (k+2, k+2)`,
no clear. This is exactly the existential shape the bag-indexed `hstep` consumes
once a concrete reserved-well `P` is supplied; the remaining non-clearing pieces
(S, Z, T, L, J, horizontal-I) follow the same two-line composition. -/

/-- **O move witness, reserved well.** On a flat pair `(c, c+1)` with a reserved
empty real column `w`, the full O move (drop then clear) raises both landing
columns by two and clears nothing. -/
theorem applyStep_O_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2)) (c + 1) (h c + 2)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_O_skyline hc hc1 heq)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **A no-clear O placement adds exactly four cells.** The cell-conservation law
for the O piece, the flat-shelf analogue of `card_place_vertI_skyline`: on a level
two-column shelf (`heq`) with a reserved well, the no-clear O writes `+2` into each
of columns `c, c+1` (`applyStep_O_skyline_noclear`), so by `card_skyline` and two
`Finset.sum_update_of_mem` peels the filled-cell count rises by `2 + 2 = 4`. This
is the packing-budget brick: every ordinary placement injects exactly four cells,
and only line clears remove them — the quantitative ledger the `card ≤ budget`
carrier balances against the once-per-bag well clear's `−4·(cols−1)`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_O_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c }).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 := by
  have hc' : c ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc
  have hc1' : c + 1 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc1
  have hc_sdiff : c ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_singleton]; exact ⟨hc', by omega⟩
  have hc_erase : c ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 1) :=
    Finset.mem_erase.mpr ⟨by omega, hc'⟩
  rw [applyStep_O_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0, card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **The O move lifts the surface potential by at most two.** Composing the O
no-clear witness (`applyStep = skyline cfg profileₒ`), the per-column place debit
(`colHeight_place_O_skyline_le`), and the potential aggregator
(`maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le`): every real column rises by at most two,
so the scalar potential rises by at most two. The first per-piece `applyStep`
height-debit on `maxColHeight` — the template the other five flush pieces follow,
and the `+2` term the once-per-bag well-clear's `−4` repays. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_O_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) + 2 := by
  apply maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le
  intro j hj
  rw [colHeight_skyline hj, applyStep_O_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0,
      ← place_O_skyline hc hc1 heq]
  exact colHeight_place_O_skyline_le hc hc1 heq hj

/-- **O move keeps the ceiling when it lands low (the template).** The
*ceiling-preserving* companion to `maxColHeight_applyStep_O_skyline_le`: that lemma
bounds the post-move potential by `maxColHeight + 2`, which drifts upward and so
cannot hold a fixed ceiling under repeated placements. Here we instead *use* that the
landing pair sits low — `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows` — together with a prior pointwise ceiling
`∀ j, h j ≤ cfg.rows`, to conclude the post-move potential is still `≤ cfg.rows`
outright. The only columns the O touches (`c`, `c+1`) rise to `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`;
every other column is untouched and already under the ceiling. This is the height-face
of the packing closure: a piece dropped on a guaranteed-low non-well column
(`exists_ne_well_colHeight_le_of_card_le`) preserves the height invariant regardless of
how tall the rest of the board is. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_O_skyline_le_rows {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_O_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]; exact hlow
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_self]; exact hlow
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]; exact hbound j hj

/-- **Two stacked O's lift a flat reserved-well pair by four.** The first packing-setup atom:
dropping the O piece twice onto the same flat real pair `(c, c+1)`, kept clear of a reserved empty
column `w` so neither drop triggers a clear, raises both columns by exactly four
(`(k, k) ↦ (k+4, k+4)`) — the four solid new rows a later vertical-`I` well clear removes in one
shot. Each O lands flush on the still-flat shelf the previous one left (the post-move pair is again
level, at `h c + 2`), so `applyStep_O_skyline_noclear` fires twice and the doubled `Function.update`
collapses pointwise to the single `+4` profile. This is the two-wide unit of the columns `1..9`
rebuild that re-arms the next Tetris from a low rich surface. -/
theorem applyStep_OO_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 4)) (c + 1) (h c + 4)) := by
  have heq1 : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2)) (c + 1) (h c + 2)) c
            = (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2)) (c + 1) (h c + 2)) (c + 1) := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hw1 : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2)) (c + 1) (h c + 2)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  rw [applyStep_O_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0,
      applyStep_O_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq1 hw hwc hwc1 hw1]
  exact congrArg (skyline cfg)
    (by funext x; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega)

/-- **Stacking `m` O's lifts a flat reserved-well pair by `2 * m`.** The replicate-fold
generalization of `applyStep_OO_skyline_noclear` (its `m = 2` instance): dropping the O piece
`m` times onto the same flat real pair `(c, c+1)`, kept clear of a reserved empty column `w` so
no drop ever triggers a clear, raises both columns by exactly `2 * m` (`(k, k) ↦ (k+2m, k+2m)`).
Proof by induction on `m` peeling the **last** O (`List.replicate_succ'`): the `m`-fold fold lands
the pair flat at `h c + 2m` by the IH, then one further `applyStep_O_skyline_noclear` writes the
final `+2` and the nested `Function.update`s collapse pointwise. This is the height-parametric
two-wide rebuild tool — raise any reserved-well pair to a chosen target before the next
vertical-`I` well-clear — that the columns `1..9` repack composes across a piece sequence. -/
theorem applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0) (m : ℕ) :
    List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c })
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) := by
  induction m with
  | zero =>
    simp only [List.replicate_zero, List.foldl_nil, Nat.mul_zero, Nat.add_zero]
    refine congrArg (skyline cfg) (funext fun x => ?_)
    rcases eq_or_ne x (c + 1) with rfl | hx1
    · rw [Function.update_self]; exact heq.symm
    · rcases eq_or_ne x c with rfl | hx0
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hx1, Function.update_self]
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hx1, Function.update_of_ne hx0]
  | succ m ih =>
    have heq'' : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) c
              = (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) := by
      simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
    have hw0'' : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) w = 0 := by
      simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
    rw [List.replicate_succ', List.foldl_append, ih, List.foldl_cons, List.foldl_nil,
        applyStep_O_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq'' hw hwc hwc1 hw0'']
    exact congrArg (skyline cfg)
      (by funext x; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega)

/-- **The `m`-fold O-stack adds exactly `4 * m` cells.** The cell-conservation ledger for
`applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear`: `m` no-clear O placements on a flat reserved-well pair each
inject four cells (`card_applyStep_O_skyline_noclear`), so the filled-cell count rises by `4 * m`.
Proved directly from the ABJO closed form: rewrite the fold to its `skyline` profile, expand both
cards via `card_skyline`, and peel the two bumped columns `(c, c+1)` with `Finset.sum_update_of_mem`
and `add_sum_erase`; the residual sums cancel and `omega` reads off `2 * (h c + 2m) − 2 * h c = 4m`
modulo `heq`. This is the quantitative budget face of ABJO that the per-bag card accounting balances
against the once-per-bag well-clear's `−36` debit. -/
theorem card_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0) (m : ℕ) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c })).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 * m := by
  have hc' : c ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc
  have hc1' : c + 1 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc1
  have hc_sdiff : c ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_singleton]; exact ⟨hc', by omega⟩
  have hc_erase : c ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 1) :=
    Finset.mem_erase.mpr ⟨by omega, hc'⟩
  rw [applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0 m, card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **The `m`-fold O-stack keeps the ceiling when its target stays low.** The height face of the
O-tile brick trio (shape `applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear`, cells
`card_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear`, height here): given a prior pointwise ceiling
`(∀ j, h j ≤ cfg.rows)` and that the stacked pair's final height stays under the ceiling
`(h c + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows)`, dropping `m` O's keeps `maxColHeight ≤ cfg.rows`. Proof: rewrite the
fold to its closed `skyline` profile, then `maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall` reduces to a
per-column bound — the two bumped columns `(c, c+1)` hit `h c + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows`, every other
column keeps its prior `≤ cfg.rows`. The structural clone of `maxColHeight_applyStep_O_skyline_le_rows`
off the replicate closed form, the height invariant the packing-carrier hstep quotes for the O-tile. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear_le_rows
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h c + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
          (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c }))
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0 m]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]; exact hlow
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_self]; exact hlow
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]; exact hbound j hj

/-- **The `m`-fold O-stack never tops out at any prefix.** The transient/safety face of the O-tile
family: for any starting surface bounded by `B` (`∀ j, h j ≤ B`) and any prefix length `n`, folding
the first `n` of `m` O-placements never reaches a lost board, provided the worst-case growth fits
under the ceiling (`B + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows`). A thin wrapper over the generic prefix-survival engine
`not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline` (every placement adds at most four rows, so the height never
exceeds `B + 4 * pls.length`), instantiated at `pls = replicate m {O}` via `List.length_replicate`.
Shape-agnostic — it needs only the height budget, not flatness or the reserved well — so this is the
per-step `isLost` obligation the packing carrier discharges while raising a pair across a piece
sequence (the safety complement of the height bound `maxColHeight_applyStep_O_replicate_..._le_rows`). -/
theorem not_isLost_take_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hh : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (c m n : ℕ) (hcap : B + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        (((List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c }).take n).foldl
          (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  apply not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline hh
  rw [List.length_replicate]
  exact hcap

/-- **Two disjoint O-stacks raise a flat 4-wide block by `2 * m`.** The first disjoint-support
composition: stacking `m` O's on pair `(c, c+1)` then `m` O's on pair `(c+2, c+3)` of an
originally-flat four-column block (each pair level, with a reserved well `w` outside the block) raises
all four columns by exactly `2 * m`. By `List.foldl_append` the concatenated fold splits into the two
ABJO folds; the first lands the left pair at `h c + 2m` leaving the right pair untouched (so still
flat and well-clear of `w`), the second lands the right pair at `h (c+2) + 2m`, and the four nested
`Function.update`s collapse pointwise. This is the 2-wide O-tile glued into a 4-wide one — two of the
four width-pieces of the `cols 1..9 = 2+2+2+3` repack — and the template for chaining O-stacks across
disjoint column ranges. -/
theorem applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols) (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 })
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h
          c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m))
          (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) := by
  have heq23' : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2)
              = (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 3) := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hw0' : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq01 hw hwc hwc1 hw0 m,
      applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc2 hc3 heq23' hw hwc2 hwc3 hw0' m]
  exact congrArg (skyline cfg)
    (by funext x; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega)

/-- **Three disjoint O-stacks, 6-wide flat lift.** Chaining
`applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear` (the 4-wide tile) with one
more `applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear` (a 2-wide tile) at `c+4`, three flat
reserved pairs `(c,c+1)`, `(c+2,c+3)`, `(c+4,c+5)` each rise by exactly `2*m`
while the empty well column `w` stays clear of all six, so nothing clears. These
are the three width-2 pieces of the cols 1..9 = 2+2+2+3 repack. -/
theorem applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hc4 : c + 4 < cfg.cols) (hc5 : c + 5 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3)) (heq45 : h (c + 4) = h (c + 5))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hwc4 : w ≠ c + 4) (hwc5 : w ≠ c + 5)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 })
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update
          (Function.update (Function.update h
          c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m))
          (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 4) (h (c + 4) + 2 * m))
          (c + 5) (h (c + 4) + 2 * m)) := by
  have heq45' :
      (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m))
        (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 4)
    = (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m))
        (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 5) := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hw0' :
      (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m))
        (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq01 heq23
        hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0,
      applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc4 hc5 heq45' hw hwc4 hwc5 hw0' m]
  exact congrArg (skyline cfg)
    (by funext x; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega)

/-- **Ceiling face of the disjoint 4-wide O-tile.** The height companion of
`applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear`: given a prior pointwise ceiling
`(∀ j, h j ≤ cfg.rows)` and that each lifted pair stays under the ceiling
`(h c + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows)` and `(h (c+2) + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows)`, the 4-wide lift keeps
`maxColHeight ≤ cfg.rows`. Rewrite the fold to its closed `skyline` profile via the shape
lemma, then `maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall` reduces to a per-column bound: the four
bumped columns hit their pair budget, every other column keeps its prior `≤ cfg.rows`. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols) (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlow01 : h c + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow23 : h (c + 2) + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
          (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
           List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 }))
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq01 heq23
        hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 3),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    exact hlow01
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 3),
          Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      exact hlow01
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 2 ≠ c + 3), Function.update_self]
        exact hlow23
      · by_cases hjc3 : j = c + 3
        · rw [hjc3, Function.update_self]; exact hlow23
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc3, Function.update_of_ne hjc2,
              Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
          exact hbound j hj

/-- **Prefix survival of the disjoint 4-wide O-tile.** The transient (every-prefix)
not-lost face of the 4-wide composition: for any starting surface bounded by `B` and any
prefix length `n`, folding the first `n` of the `2 * m` O-placements (the concatenated
`(c,c+1)` then `(c+2,c+3)` stacks) never reaches a lost board, provided the worst-case
growth fits under the ceiling `(B + 8 * m ≤ cfg.rows)`. Shape-agnostic wrapper over
`not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline`: the append has length `2 * m` by
`List.length_append`, so the cap reduces to `B + 4 * (m + m) ≤ cfg.rows`. -/
theorem not_isLost_take_applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hh : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (c m n : ℕ) (hcap : B + 8 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        (((List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } : Placement)).take n).foldl
          (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  apply not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline hh
  rw [List.length_append, List.length_replicate, List.length_replicate]
  omega

/-- **Cell ledger of the disjoint 4-wide O-tile.** The cells face completing the four-face set for
the 4-wide composition (shape `applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear`, height
`maxColHeight_..._le_rows`, safety `not_isLost_take_...`, cells here): the two disjoint O-stacks add
exactly `8 * m` cells (`4 * m` per pair). Proved by chaining the single-pair ledger ABJP twice through
`List.foldl_append`: the left fold lands `skyline H2a` (shape ABJO), the right fold over `skyline H2a`
adds `4 * m` by ABJP, and a `← shape` reveal turns `(skyline H2a).card` back into the left fold to read
off its own `4 * m`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols) (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 })).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 8 * m := by
  have heq23' : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2)
              = (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 3) := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hw0' : (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hcard2a :
      (skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m))).card
        = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 * m := by
    rw [← applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq01 hw hwc hwc1 hw0 m]
    exact card_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq01 hw hwc hwc1 hw0 m
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq01 hw hwc hwc1 hw0 m,
      card_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc2 hc3 heq23' hw hwc2 hwc3 hw0' m,
      hcard2a]
  omega

/-- **Prefix survival of the disjoint 6-wide O-tile.** The transient (every-prefix) not-lost face of
the 6-wide composition `applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear`: folding any prefix of
the `3 * m` O-placements (stacks on `(c,c+1)`, `(c+2,c+3)`, `(c+4,c+5)`) never tops out, provided
`B + 12 * m ≤ cfg.rows`. Shape-agnostic wrapper over `not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline`; the
two-fold append has length `3 * m` by `List.length_append`, so the cap reduces to
`B + 4 * ((m + m) + m) ≤ cfg.rows`. -/
theorem not_isLost_take_applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hh : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (c m n : ℕ) (hcap : B + 12 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        (((List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 } : Placement)).take n).foldl
          (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  apply not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline hh
  rw [List.length_append, List.length_append, List.length_replicate, List.length_replicate,
      List.length_replicate]
  omega

/-- **Cell ledger of the disjoint 6-wide O-tile.** The cells face of the 6-wide composition
`applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear`: three disjoint O-stacks add exactly `12 * m`
cells. Same chaining recipe as the 4-wide ledger ABJW, one level up: `List.foldl_append` peels the last
stack, the ABJS shape collapses the leading 4-wide fold to `skyline H4`, the third-stack ledger ABJP
adds `4 * m`, and a `← shape` reveal turns `(skyline H4).card` back into the 4-wide fold so ABJW reads
off its `8 * m`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hc4 : c + 4 < cfg.cols) (hc5 : c + 5 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3)) (heq45 : h (c + 4) = h (c + 5))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hwc4 : w ≠ c + 4) (hwc5 : w ≠ c + 5)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } ++
         List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 })).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 12 * m := by
  have heq45' :
      (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m))
        (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 4)
    = (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m))
        (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 5) := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hw0' :
      (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m))
        (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hcard4 :
      (skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2 * m))
        (c + 1) (h c + 2 * m)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m)) (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * m))).card
        = (skyline cfg h).card + 8 * m := by
    rw [← applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq01 heq23
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0]
    exact card_applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq01 heq23
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_O_replicate_two_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq01 heq23
        hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0,
      card_applyStep_O_replicate_skyline_noclear hc4 hc5 heq45' hw hwc4 hwc5 hw0' m,
      hcard4]
  omega

/-- **Ceiling face of the disjoint 6-wide O-tile.** The height companion of
`applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear`: given a prior pointwise ceiling
`(∀ j, h j ≤ cfg.rows)` and that each of the three lifted pairs stays under the ceiling
(`h c + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows`, `h (c+2) + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows`, `h (c+4) + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows`), the
6-wide lift keeps `maxColHeight ≤ cfg.rows`. Same recipe as the 4-wide ceiling ABJU, one level
up: rewrite the fold to its closed `skyline` profile via the shape lemma, then
`maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall` reduces to a per-column bound — the six bumped columns hit
their pair budget, every other column keeps its prior `≤ cfg.rows`. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hc4 : c + 4 < cfg.cols) (hc5 : c + 5 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3)) (heq45 : h (c + 4) = h (c + 5))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hwc4 : w ≠ c + 4) (hwc5 : w ≠ c + 5)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlow01 : h c + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow23 : h (c + 2) + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlow45 : h (c + 4) + 2 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
          (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
           List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } ++
           List.replicate m { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 }))
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5
        heq01 heq23 heq45 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 5),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 4),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 3),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    exact hlow01
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 5),
          Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 4),
          Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 3),
          Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      exact hlow01
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 2 ≠ c + 5),
            Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 2 ≠ c + 4),
            Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 2 ≠ c + 3), Function.update_self]
        exact hlow23
      · by_cases hjc3 : j = c + 3
        · rw [hjc3, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 3 ≠ c + 5),
              Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 3 ≠ c + 4), Function.update_self]
          exact hlow23
        · by_cases hjc4 : j = c + 4
          · rw [hjc4, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 4 ≠ c + 5), Function.update_self]
            exact hlow45
          · by_cases hjc5 : j = c + 5
            · rw [hjc5, Function.update_self]; exact hlow45
            · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc5, Function.update_of_ne hjc4,
                  Function.update_of_ne hjc3, Function.update_of_ne hjc2,
                  Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
              exact hbound j hj

/-- **Ceiling face of the width-3 vertical-I tail tile.** The height companion of
`applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear`: given a prior pointwise ceiling
`(∀ j, h j ≤ cfg.rows)` and that each of the three raised columns stays under the ceiling
(`h c + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows`, `h (c+1) + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows`, `h (c+2) + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows`), the flat
3-wide lift keeps `maxColHeight ≤ cfg.rows`. Identical recipe to the O-tile ceilings ABJU and ABJZ:
rewrite the fold to its closed `skyline` via the shape lemma, then `maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall`
reduces to a per-column bound — the three raised columns hit their own `4 * m` budget, every other
column keeps its prior `≤ cfg.rows`. Each per-column budget is independent here (no shared pair),
matching the no-flatness shape lemma. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w m : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlow0 : h c + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow1 : h (c + 1) + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlow2 : h (c + 2) + 4 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
          (List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } ++
           List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 1 } ++
           List.replicate m { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 2 }))
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    exact hlow0
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      exact hlow1
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_self]; exact hlow2
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
        exact hbound j hj

/-- **Prefix survival of the width-3 vertical-I tail tile.** The transient (every-prefix) not-lost
face of `applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear` and the vertical-I analog of the
O-tile ABJX: folding any prefix of the `3 * m` vertical-I placements (stacks on columns `c`, `c+1`,
`c+2`) never tops out, provided `B + 12 * m ≤ cfg.rows`. Shape-agnostic wrapper over
`not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline`: the two-fold append has length `3 * m` by
`List.length_append` and `List.length_replicate`, so the cap reduces to `B + 4 * ((m + m) + m)`.
The three record literals carry `: Placement` ascriptions, since the `(append).take n).foldl` dot
form elaborates the receiver before `Placement.applyStep` can fix the element type. -/
theorem not_isLost_take_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hh : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (c m n : ℕ) (hcap : B + 12 * m ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        (((List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 1 } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate m ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 2 } : Placement)).take n).foldl
          (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  apply not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline hh
  rw [List.length_append, List.length_append, List.length_replicate, List.length_replicate,
      List.length_replicate]
  omega

/-- **Disjoint-tile composition for the skyline fold.** If a placement list `L1` drives the board
from profile `h` to profile `g1`, and a follow-on list `L2` drives `g1` to `g2`, then the
concatenation `L1 ++ L2` drives `h` straight to `g2`. This is the generic sequential composition
primitive behind every multi-tile packing layer (milestone #77): the caller supplies the two
single-tile shape lemmas and the composed profile `g2` is read off `L2`'s output, with no need to
respell a deep nested-update equality (the spelling that made the monolithic nine-wide repack
time out at `whnf`). Proof: `List.foldl_append` splits the fold at the boundary and the two
hypotheses rewrite each half. -/
theorem foldl_append_skyline_compose
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h g1 g2 : ℕ → ℕ} {L1 L2 : List Placement}
    (h1 : List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h) L1 = skyline cfg g1)
    (h2 : List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg g1) L2 = skyline cfg g2) :
    List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h) (L1 ++ L2) = skyline cfg g2 := by
  rw [List.foldl_append, h1, h2]

/-- **Cell ledger of the full `cols 1..9` repack layer.** Stacking `p` O-pieces on each reserved
flat pair `(c,c+1)`, `(c+2,c+3)`, `(c+4,c+5)` and then `q` vertical-I pieces on each tail column
`c+6`, `c+7`, `c+8`, over an empty well `w`, adds exactly `12 * p + 12 * q` filled cells: the three
O-pairs contribute `12 * p` (each pair raises two columns by `2p`) and the three vertical-I stacks
contribute `12 * q` (each column raised by `4q`). Nothing clears (the well stays empty), so the cell
count is purely additive. Proof: `List.foldl_append` splits at the O and vertical-I boundary, the
O-shape lemma lands the leading fold on the six-fold profile `G1`, the vertical-I card lemma adds
`12 * q` over `G1`, and `card G1 = card h + 12 * p` closes by `omega`. This is the cell face of the
one-layer packing brick, paired with the shape composition `foldl_append_skyline_compose`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline_noclear
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w p q : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hc4 : c + 4 < cfg.cols) (hc5 : c + 5 < cfg.cols)
    (hc6 : c + 6 < cfg.cols) (hc7 : c + 7 < cfg.cols) (hc8 : c + 8 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3)) (heq45 : h (c + 4) = h (c + 5))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols)
    (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hwc4 : w ≠ c + 4) (hwc5 : w ≠ c + 5) (hwc6 : w ≠ c + 6) (hwc7 : w ≠ c + 7)
    (hwc8 : w ≠ c + 8) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        ((List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
          List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } ++
          List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 }) ++
         (List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 6 } ++
          List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 7 } ++
          List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 8 }))).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 12 * p + 12 * q := by
  have hw0' :
      (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update
        (Function.update (Function.update h
        c (h c + 2 * p)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * p)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * p))
        (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * p)) (c + 4) (h (c + 4) + 2 * p))
        (c + 5) (h (c + 4) + 2 * p)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  have hcardO :
      (skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update
        (Function.update (Function.update h
        c (h c + 2 * p)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * p)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * p))
        (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * p)) (c + 4) (h (c + 4) + 2 * p))
        (c + 5) (h (c + 4) + 2 * p))).card = (skyline cfg h).card + 12 * p := by
    rw [← applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5
          heq01 heq23 heq45 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5 hw0]
    exact card_applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5
          heq01 heq23 heq45 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5 hw0
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5
        heq01 heq23 heq45 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5 hw0,
      card_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc6 hc7 hc8 hw
        hwc6 hwc7 hwc8 hw0']
  omega

/-- **Ceiling face, full repack layer (reserved well).** The combined O-triple
(cols `c..c+5`) then vertI-triple (cols `c+6..c+8`) keeps every column height
`≤ cfg.rows`, given each tile's own ceiling budget. Composes the O-layer ceiling
`maxColHeight_applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows` (ABJZ)
with the vertI ceiling `..._vertI_..._le_rows` (ABKD) across the `List.foldl_append`
seam; `forall_le_of_maxColHeight_skyline_le` reads the O-layer ceiling back into the
per-column bound the vertI face demands. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline_noclear_le_rows
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w p q : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hc4 : c + 4 < cfg.cols) (hc5 : c + 5 < cfg.cols)
    (hc6 : c + 6 < cfg.cols) (hc7 : c + 7 < cfg.cols) (hc8 : c + 8 < cfg.cols)
    (heq01 : h c = h (c + 1)) (heq23 : h (c + 2) = h (c + 3)) (heq45 : h (c + 4) = h (c + 5))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols)
    (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hwc4 : w ≠ c + 4) (hwc5 : w ≠ c + 5) (hwc6 : w ≠ c + 6) (hwc7 : w ≠ c + 7)
    (hwc8 : w ≠ c + 8) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlowO01 : h c + 2 * p ≤ cfg.rows) (hlowO23 : h (c + 2) + 2 * p ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlowO45 : h (c + 4) + 2 * p ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlowI6 : h (c + 6) + 4 * q ≤ cfg.rows) (hlowI7 : h (c + 7) + 4 * q ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hlowI8 : h (c + 8) + 4 * q ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
          ((List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
            List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } ++
            List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 }) ++
           (List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 6 } ++
            List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 7 } ++
            List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 8 })))
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  have hw0' :
      (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update
        (Function.update (Function.update h
        c (h c + 2 * p)) (c + 1) (h c + 2 * p)) (c + 2) (h (c + 2) + 2 * p))
        (c + 3) (h (c + 2) + 2 * p)) (c + 4) (h (c + 4) + 2 * p))
        (c + 5) (h (c + 4) + 2 * p)) w = 0 := by
    simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  rw [List.foldl_append,
      applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5
        heq01 heq23 heq45 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_applyStep_vertI_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows
    hc6 hc7 hc8 hw hwc6 hwc7 hwc8 hw0'
  · apply forall_le_of_maxColHeight_skyline_le
    rw [← applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5
          heq01 heq23 heq45 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5 hw0]
    exact maxColHeight_applyStep_O_replicate_three_disjoint_skyline_noclear_le_rows
      hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5 heq01 heq23 heq45 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5 hw0
      hbound hlowO01 hlowO23 hlowO45
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 ≠ c + 5 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 ≠ c + 4 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 ≠ c + 3 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 ≠ c + 2 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 ≠ c + 1 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 ≠ c by omega)]
    exact hlowI6
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 1 ≠ c + 5 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 1 ≠ c + 4 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 1 ≠ c + 3 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 1 ≠ c + 2 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 1 ≠ c + 1 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 1 ≠ c by omega)]
    exact hlowI7
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 2 ≠ c + 5 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 2 ≠ c + 4 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 2 ≠ c + 3 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 2 ≠ c + 2 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 2 ≠ c + 1 by omega),
        Function.update_of_ne (show c + 6 + 2 ≠ c by omega)]
    exact hlowI8

/-- **Safety face, full repack layer (reserved well).** Every prefix of the combined
O-triple (cols `c..c+5`) then vertI-triple (cols `c+6..c+8`) schedule keeps the board
un-lost, given the headroom `B + 12 * p + 12 * q ≤ cfg.rows`. Like the per-tile prefix
faces ABJX and ABKE this needs no seam composition: the generic
`not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline` bounds each placement's rise by four, so the
whole list of length `3p + 3q` lifts the surface by at most `12p + 12q`. This is the
fourth and final face of the one-layer packing brick (shape via
`foldl_append_skyline_compose`, cells, ceiling, now prefix-safety). -/
theorem not_isLost_take_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hh : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (c p q n : ℕ)
    (hcap : B + 12 * p + 12 * q ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        ((((List.replicate p ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } : Placement) ++
            List.replicate p ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } : Placement) ++
            List.replicate p ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 } : Placement)) ++
           (List.replicate q ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 6 } : Placement) ++
            List.replicate q ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 7 } : Placement) ++
            List.replicate q ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 8 } : Placement))).take n).foldl
          (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  apply not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline hh
  simp only [List.length_append, List.length_replicate]
  omega

/-- **Endpoint survival of the full repack layer (reserved well).** The endpoint companion to
the prefix face `not_isLost_take_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline`: where that certifies every
*prefix* of the O-triple then vertI-triple schedule stays un-lost, this certifies the *final*
folded board — the surface the next phase (a well-clear or the next bag) inherits sits below
`cfg.rows`. Same headroom `B + 12 * p + 12 * q ≤ cfg.rows`; the generic endpoint lemma
`not_isLost_foldl_applyStep_skyline` bounds the length `3p + 3q` schedule's total rise by
`12p + 12q`. -/
theorem not_isLost_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {B : ℕ}
    (hh : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ B) (c p q : ℕ)
    (hcap : B + 12 * p + 12 * q ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost cfg
        (((List.replicate p ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate p ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate p ({ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 } : Placement)) ++
          (List.replicate q ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 6 } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate q ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 7 } : Placement) ++
           List.replicate q ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 8 } : Placement))).foldl
          (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  apply not_isLost_foldl_applyStep_skyline hh
  simp only [List.length_append, List.length_replicate]
  omega

/-- **Flat-input ceiling of the full repack layer.** Specialises the per-tile-budget
ceiling `maxColHeight_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline_noclear_le_rows` to a surface
that is flat at height `k` across all nine packed columns `c..c+8`, with the O-count
locked to `p = 2 * q`. Then every O-pair rises `2p = 4q` and every vertI column rises
`4q`, so all nine columns land at the common height `k + 4q`; the single fit condition
`k + 4 * q ≤ cfg.rows` discharges all six per-tile budgets. This is the height-maintenance
fact a leveled reserved-well carrier quotes after absorbing one block of O and I draws
before the well-clear. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline_flat_le_rows
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w k q : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hc4 : c + 4 < cfg.cols) (hc5 : c + 5 < cfg.cols)
    (hc6 : c + 6 < cfg.cols) (hc7 : c + 7 < cfg.cols) (hc8 : c + 8 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols)
    (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hwc4 : w ≠ c + 4) (hwc5 : w ≠ c + 5) (hwc6 : w ≠ c + 6) (hwc7 : w ≠ c + 7)
    (hwc8 : w ≠ c + 8) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hflat : ∀ j, c ≤ j → j ≤ c + 8 → h j = k)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hfit : k + 4 * q ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
          ((List.replicate (2 * q) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
            List.replicate (2 * q) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } ++
            List.replicate (2 * q) { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 }) ++
           (List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 6 } ++
            List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 7 } ++
            List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 8 })))
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  apply maxColHeight_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline_noclear_le_rows
    hc hc1 hc2 hc3 hc4 hc5 hc6 hc7 hc8 ?_ ?_ ?_ hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hwc4 hwc5
    hwc6 hwc7 hwc8 hw0 hbound ?_ ?_ ?_ ?_ ?_ ?_
  · rw [hflat c (by omega) (by omega), hflat (c + 1) (by omega) (by omega)]
  · rw [hflat (c + 2) (by omega) (by omega), hflat (c + 3) (by omega) (by omega)]
  · rw [hflat (c + 4) (by omega) (by omega), hflat (c + 5) (by omega) (by omega)]
  · rw [hflat c (by omega) (by omega)]; omega
  · rw [hflat (c + 2) (by omega) (by omega)]; omega
  · rw [hflat (c + 4) (by omega) (by omega)]; omega
  · rw [hflat (c + 6) (by omega) (by omega)]; omega
  · rw [hflat (c + 7) (by omega) (by omega)]; omega
  · rw [hflat (c + 8) (by omega) (by omega)]; omega

/-- **Reserved well stays empty through the full repack layer.** After stacking the three flat
O-pairs (cols `c..c+5`) and the three vertical-I tail columns (cols `c+6..c+8`) over an empty
reserved well `w`, the well column is still height `0`. Every placed cell sits in one of the real
columns `c..c+8` (O cells at column offsets `0,1`; vertical-I cells at offset `0`), all distinct
from `w`, so the `havoid` premise of `colHeight_foldl_applyStep_skyline_well_eq_zero` holds and no
draw ever pokes the well. This is the re-entry hinge: the well stays clear for the subsequent
well-clear (`foldl_fill_then_clear_fixed_all_cols`) that returns the surface to itself. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_O_vertI_repack_skyline_noclear_well_eq_zero
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w p q : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols)
    (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hwc4 : w ≠ c + 4) (hwc5 : w ≠ c + 5) (hwc6 : w ≠ c + 6) (hwc7 : w ≠ c + 7)
    (hwc8 : w ≠ c + 8) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    colHeight (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep cfg) (skyline cfg h)
        ((List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c } ++
          List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 2 } ++
          List.replicate p { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c + 4 }) ++
         (List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 6 } ++
          List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 7 } ++
          List.replicate q { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c + 8 }))) w = 0 := by
  apply colHeight_foldl_applyStep_skyline_well_eq_zero hw hw0
  intro pl hpl cell hcell
  simp only [List.mem_append, List.mem_replicate] at hpl
  rcases hpl with (((⟨-, rfl⟩ | ⟨-, rfl⟩) | ⟨-, rfl⟩) | ((⟨-, rfl⟩ | ⟨-, rfl⟩) | ⟨-, rfl⟩))
  · rw [shapeUp_O] at hcell
    simp only [Finset.mem_insert, Finset.mem_singleton] at hcell
    rcases hcell with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl <;> dsimp only <;> omega
  · rw [shapeUp_O] at hcell
    simp only [Finset.mem_insert, Finset.mem_singleton] at hcell
    rcases hcell with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl <;> dsimp only <;> omega
  · rw [shapeUp_O] at hcell
    simp only [Finset.mem_insert, Finset.mem_singleton] at hcell
    rcases hcell with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl <;> dsimp only <;> omega
  · rw [shapeUp_vertI] at hcell
    simp only [Finset.mem_insert, Finset.mem_singleton] at hcell
    rcases hcell with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl <;> dsimp only <;> omega
  · rw [shapeUp_vertI] at hcell
    simp only [Finset.mem_insert, Finset.mem_singleton] at hcell
    rcases hcell with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl <;> dsimp only <;> omega
  · rw [shapeUp_vertI] at hcell
    simp only [Finset.mem_insert, Finset.mem_singleton] at hcell
    rcases hcell with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl <;> dsimp only <;> omega

/-- **S move witness, reserved well.** On the staircase notch `(h c = h(c+1)`,
`h(c+2) = h c + 1)` with a reserved empty real column `w`, the full S move lands
flush — profile `(k, k, k+1) ↦ (k+1, k+2, k+2)` — and clears nothing. The notch
precondition is the very surface S demands (a flat pair then a single step);
absorbing the bag's forced S without a hole is exactly this brick. -/
theorem applyStep_S_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h c = h (c + 1)) (hstep : h (c + 2) = h c + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 1))
          (c + 1) (h c + 2)) (c + 2) (h c + 2)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_S_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1,
                   Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **Surface-potential debit for the S move.** Placing an S on its notch
(`h c = h(c+1)`, `h(c+2) = h c + 1`) into a skyline with a reserved empty
column raises the scalar surface potential `maxColHeight` by at most `2`. The
applyStep-level companion to `colHeight_place_S_skyline_le`, composing the
no-clear surface equation with the per-column height debit. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_S_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h c = h (c + 1)) (hstep : h (c + 2) = h c + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) + 2 := by
  apply maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le
  intro j hj
  rw [colHeight_skyline hj,
      applyStep_S_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
      ← place_S_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep]
  exact colHeight_place_S_skyline_le hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hj

/-- **Ceiling-preserving S placement.** The first notch ceiling brick: an S on its
notch (`heq : h c = h(c+1)`, `hstep : h(c+2) = h c + 1`) with a reserved empty well
(`hw0`). The S writes `h c + 1` into column `c` and `h c + 2` into `c+1, c+2`; with a
pointwise ceiling (`hbound`) and a low landing (`hlow : h c + 2 ≤ rows`) the
post-placement surface potential stays `≤ cfg.rows` outright — no `+2` drift. Same
3-column peel template as the flat-triple ceiling lemmas, but on the notch shape
rather than a flat shelf. Adds S to the O/T/L/J/horizI ceiling family; only the
mirror Z notch and the well-clearing vertical I remain. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_S_skyline_le_rows {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h c = h (c + 1)) (hstep : h (c + 2) = h c + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_S_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_self]
        omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
        exact hbound j hj

/-- **A no-clear S placement adds exactly four cells.** The first notch
cell-conservation brick: on the staircase notch (`heq : h c = h(c+1)`,
`hstep : h(c+2) = h c + 1`) with a reserved well, the no-clear S writes
`+1, +2, +2` into columns `c, c+1, c+2` over heights `(k, k, k+1)`
(`applyStep_S_skyline_noclear`), a net `+4`. Same three-fold
`sum_update_of_mem` / `add_sum_erase` / `erase_eq` peel as the flat-triple pieces;
`omega` closes using the notch constraints `heq, hstep` (touched columns sum to
`3·(h c) + 5`, the base notch to `3·(h c) + 1`). -/
theorem card_applyStep_S_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h c = h (c + 1)) (hstep : h (c + 2) = h c + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 := by
  have hc2' : c + 2 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc2
  have hc1_sdiff : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc_sdiff2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2}) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc1_erase : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  have hc_erase2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2)).erase (c + 1) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  rw [applyStep_S_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
    card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc2', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1_sdiff,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff2,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc2', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1_erase,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase2,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **Z move witness, reserved well.** The mirror of `applyStep_S_skyline_noclear`:
on the mirror notch `(h(c+1) = h(c+2)`, `h c = h(c+1) + 1)` with a reserved empty
real column `w`, the full Z move lands flush — profile `(k+1, k, k) ↦ (k+2, k+2,
k+1)` — and clears nothing. Together with the S witness this packages both of the
bag's forced hole-makers as no-clear moves on their respective notches. -/
theorem applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h (c + 1) = h (c + 2)) (hstep : h c = h (c + 1) + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h (c + 1) + 2))
          (c + 1) (h (c + 1) + 2)) (c + 2) (h (c + 1) + 1)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_Z_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1,
                   Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **Surface-potential debit for the Z move.** The mirror of
`maxColHeight_applyStep_S_skyline_le`: placing a Z on its mirror notch
(`h(c+1) = h(c+2)`, `h c = h(c+1) + 1`) into a skyline with a reserved empty
column raises the scalar surface potential `maxColHeight` by at most `2`. With
this the per-piece applyStep potential-debit table is complete for all seven
tetrominoes (O/T/L/J/S/Z `+2`, horizontal-I `+1`). -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_Z_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h (c + 1) = h (c + 2)) (hstep : h c = h (c + 1) + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) + 2 := by
  apply maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le
  intro j hj
  rw [colHeight_skyline hj,
      applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
      ← place_Z_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep]
  exact colHeight_place_Z_skyline_le hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hj

/-- **Ceiling-preserving Z placement.** The mirror notch ceiling brick completing the
seven-piece surface family: a Z on its mirror notch (`heq : h(c+1) = h(c+2)`,
`hstep : h c = h(c+1) + 1`) with a reserved empty well (`hw0`). The Z writes
`h(c+1) + 2` into columns `c, c+1` and `h(c+1) + 1` into `c+2`; with a pointwise
ceiling (`hbound`) and a low landing (`hlow : h(c+1) + 2 ≤ rows`) the post-placement
surface potential stays `≤ cfg.rows` outright — no `+2` drift. Same 3-column notch
peel template as the S brick. With this every ordinary piece (O/T/L/J/horizI/S/Z) has
its ceiling-preserving placement; only the well-clearing vertical I remains. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_Z_skyline_le_rows {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h (c + 1) = h (c + 2)) (hstep : h c = h (c + 1) + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h (c + 1) + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_self]
        omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
        exact hbound j hj

/-- **A no-clear Z placement adds exactly four cells.** The mirror-notch
cell-conservation brick completing the seven-piece card table: on the mirror notch
(`heq : h(c+1) = h(c+2)`, `hstep : h c = h(c+1) + 1`) with a reserved well, the
no-clear Z writes `+2, +2, +1` into columns `c, c+1, c+2` over heights `(k+1, k, k)`
(`applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear`), a net `+4`. Same three-fold
`sum_update_of_mem` / `add_sum_erase` / `erase_eq` peel as the S brick; `omega` closes
using the mirror-notch constraints `heq, hstep` (touched columns sum to
`3·(h (c+1)) + 5`, the base notch to `3·(h (c+1)) + 1`). With this every ordinary
piece (O/T/L/J/horizI/S/Z) has its `+4` no-clear cell-conservation brick. -/
theorem card_applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h (c + 1) = h (c + 2)) (hstep : h c = h (c + 1) + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 := by
  have hc2' : c + 2 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc2
  have hc1_sdiff : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc_sdiff2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2}) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc1_erase : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  have hc_erase2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2)).erase (c + 1) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  rw [applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
    card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc2', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1_sdiff,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff2,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc2', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1_erase,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase2,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **Horizontal-I move witness, reserved well.** On a flat four-column stretch
`(h(c+1) = h(c+2) = h(c+3) = h c)` with a reserved empty real column `w`, the full
horizontal-I move raises each of the four columns by one and clears nothing — the
cheapest flat-shelf line-builder, packaged as a no-clear move. -/
theorem applyStep_horizI_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c) (heq3 : h (c + 3) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 0, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update
          (Function.update h c (h c + 1)) (c + 1) (h c + 1)) (c + 2) (h c + 1))
          (c + 3) (h c + 1)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_horizI_skyline hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc3, Function.update_of_ne hwc2,
                   Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **The horizontal-I move lifts the surface potential by at most one.** The
cheapest line-builder: on a flat quad it raises all four columns by exactly one,
so the scalar potential rises by at most one (the only `+1` entry in the debit
table, versus `+2` for the rest). Same template as the flat-triple pieces, with
`colHeight_place_horizI_skyline_le` supplying the per-column `+1` cap. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_horizI_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c) (heq3 : h (c + 3) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) + 1 := by
  apply maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le
  intro j hj
  rw [colHeight_skyline hj,
      applyStep_horizI_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0,
      ← place_horizI_skyline hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3]
  exact colHeight_place_horizI_skyline_le hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3 hj

/-- **Ceiling-preserving horizontal-I placement.** The cheapest ceiling brick:
the flat-I (`+1` to all four columns) on a flat quad (`heq1`–`heq3`) with a
reserved empty well (`hw0`). Because every touched column rises only to `h c + 1`,
the low-landing premise needed is the weaker `hlow : h c + 1 ≤ rows` (one less
than the `+2` pieces). With a pointwise ceiling (`hbound`) the post-placement
surface potential stays `≤ cfg.rows` outright. Same 4-column peel template as the
flat-triple ceiling lemmas; touched columns close by `omega` from `hlow`, the
rest inherit `hbound`. Adds the horizontal I to the O/T/L/J ceiling family,
leaving only the notch pieces S, Z and the well-clearing vertical I. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_horizI_skyline_le_rows {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c) (heq3 : h (c + 3) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h c + 1 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_horizI_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 3),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 3),
          Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 2 ≠ c + 3), Function.update_self]
        omega
      · by_cases hjc3 : j = c + 3
        · rw [hjc3, Function.update_self]
          omega
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc3, Function.update_of_ne hjc2,
              Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
          exact hbound j hj

/-- **A no-clear horizontal-I placement adds exactly four cells.** The 4-column
cell-conservation brick: on a flat quad (`heq1`–`heq3`) with a reserved well, the
no-clear flat-I writes `+1` into each of columns `c, c+1, c+2, c+3`
(`applyStep_horizI_skyline_noclear`), summing to `+4`. The 4-fold extension of the
flat-triple peel: four `sum_update_of_mem` (outermost update `c+3` first) + four
`add_sum_erase` (same order) + four `erase_eq` (bridge erase↔sdiff), each touched
column cancelling from both sides, leaving `omega` the arithmetic
`1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4` modulo `heq1, heq2, heq3`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_horizI_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c) (heq3 : h (c + 3) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 0, col := c }).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 := by
  have hc3' : c + 3 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc3
  have hc2_sdiff : c + 2 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 3} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc1_sdiff2 : c + 1 ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 3}) \ {c + 2} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc_sdiff3 : c ∈ (((Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 3}) \ {c + 2}) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc2_erase : c + 2 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 3) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  have hc1_erase2 : c + 1 ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 3)).erase (c + 2) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  have hc_erase3 : c ∈ (((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 3)).erase (c + 2)).erase (c + 1) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  rw [applyStep_horizI_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0,
    card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc3', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc2_sdiff,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1_sdiff2, Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff3,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc3', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc2_erase,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1_erase2, ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase3,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **T move witness, reserved well.** On a flat three-column shelf
`(h(c+1) = h(c+2) = h c)` with a reserved empty real column `w`, the full T move
(rotation 2, flat base with a centre bump) raises the middle column by two and the
flanks by one, clearing nothing. -/
theorem applyStep_T_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.T, rot := 2, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 1))
          (c + 1) (h c + 2)) (c + 2) (h c + 1)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_T_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1,
                   Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **A no-clear T placement adds exactly four cells.** The 3-column cell-conservation
brick (mirror of `card_applyStep_O_skyline_noclear`): on a level shelf
(`heq1, heq2`) with a reserved well, the no-clear T writes `+1, +2, +1` into columns
`c, c+1, c+2` (`applyStep_T_skyline_noclear`), summing to `+4`. Proof by the reusable
three-fold `sum_update_of_mem` / `add_sum_erase` / `erase_eq` peel: each touched column
is removed from both sides and the residual sums coincide, leaving `omega` the
arithmetic `1 + 2 + 1 = 4` modulo `heq1, heq2`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_T_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.T, rot := 2, col := c }).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 := by
  have hc2' : c + 2 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc2
  have hc1_sdiff : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc_sdiff2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2}) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc1_erase : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  have hc_erase2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2)).erase (c + 1) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  rw [applyStep_T_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
    card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc2', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1_sdiff,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff2,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc2', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1_erase,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase2,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **The T move lifts the surface potential by at most two.** The flat-triple
analogue of `maxColHeight_applyStep_O_skyline_le`, via the same template: the T
no-clear witness reduces the move to its profile, `colHeight_place_T_skyline_le`
caps each real column at `+2`, and `maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le` lifts
the per-column cap to the scalar potential. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_T_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.T, rot := 2, col := c })
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) + 2 := by
  apply maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le
  intro j hj
  rw [colHeight_skyline hj, applyStep_T_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
      ← place_T_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2]
  exact colHeight_place_T_skyline_le hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hj

/-- **T move keeps the ceiling when it lands low.** The 3-column analogue of
`maxColHeight_applyStep_O_skyline_le_rows`: on a flat triple shelf with a reserved
well, dropping a T whose tallest cell stays under the ceiling (`h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`)
keeps the post-move potential `≤ cfg.rows`. The T raises its centre by two and its
flanks by one, so the single bound `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows` caps all three touched
columns; every untouched column keeps its prior `≤ cfg.rows` height. Another
height-face brick of the packing closure, now for the T piece. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_T_skyline_le_rows {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.T, rot := 2, col := c })
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_T_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_self]
        omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
        exact hbound j hj

/-- **L move witness, reserved well.** On a flat three-column shelf
`(h(c+1) = h(c+2) = h c)` with a reserved empty real column `w`, the full L move
(rotation 0, flat base with a right riser) raises the right column by two and the
two left columns by one, clearing nothing. -/
theorem applyStep_L_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 1))
          (c + 1) (h c + 1)) (c + 2) (h c + 2)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_L_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1,
                   Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **A flat L manufactures an S-notch — the valley REBUILDER brick.** Dropping a
no-clear L on a level triple shelf `c, c+1, c+2` (with a reserved empty well) writes the
profile `+1, +1, +2`, leaving the three columns at heights `h c + 1, h c + 1, h c + 2`.
Those heights satisfy the exact S-notch convention consumed by
`colHeight_applyStep_skyline_Snotch_of_avoid` and the S-piece landing lemmas: the front
pair is level (`colHeight (c+1) = colHeight c`) and the third column stands one higher
(`colHeight (c+2) = colHeight c + 1`). So an L played flat re-digs the staircase notch an
owed S draw needs — the regeneration half of the bag-phase ledger (a flat surface owes an
S a valley; the L manufactures one). Proof: rewrite by `applyStep_L_skyline_noclear` to
land on the explicit triple `Function.update`, then peel each update with
`Function.update_of_ne` / `Function.update_self` exactly as in
`maxColHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_le_rows`. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_makes_Snotch {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }) c = h c + 1 ∧
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 1) = h c + 1 ∧
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 2) = h c + 2 := by
  rw [applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_self]
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc2, Function.update_self]

/-- **A flat L touches only its own triple — the frame companion to
`colHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_makes_Snotch`.** A no-clear L at column `c` writes only
columns `c, c+1, c+2`; the reserved well `w` stays empty (`colHeight w = 0`) and every
other in-field column keeps its prior height (`colHeight j = h j`). Together with the
makes-Snotch heights this fully characterises the post-L surface: an S-notch staircase at
the placed triple, the band untouched everywhere else. That is exactly what the downstream
"L re-digs a notch in an otherwise level band" step needs — the notch appears at the triple
without disturbing the well or the rest of the reservoir. Proof: the well peel is the same
`Function.update_of_ne` chain used in `applyStep_L_skyline_noclear`'s no-clear witness; the
off-triple columns peel identically against the three update points. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_frame {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }) w = 0 ∧
    ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ c → j ≠ c + 1 → j ≠ c + 2 →
      colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }) j = h j := by
  rw [applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hw, Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1,
        Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0
  · intro j hj hjc hjc1 hjc2
    rw [colHeight_skyline hj, Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1,
        Function.update_of_ne hjc]

/-- **Post-L board is an S-notch skyline — the rebuild-to-consume interface.** Packages
`colHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_makes_Snotch` and `..._frame` into the exact shape the S
landing brick `applyStep_S_skyline_noclear` consumes: after a no-clear L on a level triple
(with reserved well `w`), the board is `skyline cfg h'` for an explicit `h'` whose triple
`c, c+1, c+2` satisfies the S-notch precondition (`h' c = h' (c+1)`, `h' (c+2) = h' c + 1`)
and whose well stays empty (`h' w = 0`). So the next owed S draw can land hole-free at the
very column the L rebuilt: `obtain ⟨h', hb, heq, hstep, hw0'⟩ := …; rw [hb]; exact
applyStep_S_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0'`. Proof: the witness
is `applyStep_L_skyline_noclear`'s explicit triple update; each notch/well equality unfolds
by `Function.update_apply` and `split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧
      h' c = h' (c + 1) ∧ h' (c + 2) = h' c + 1 ∧ h' w = 0 := by
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Post-L board is an S-notch skyline with absolute heights exposed.** Strengthens
`applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline`, which exposed only the relative notch equalities
(`h' c = h' (c+1)`, `h' (c+2) = h' c + 1`) and so could not pin where the surface sits. Here the
witness `h'` is named and its triple is given in absolute terms against the flat input level
`h c`: `h' c = h c + 1`, `h' (c+1) = h c + 1`, `h' (c+2) = h c + 2`, with the well still empty.
This is the prerequisite for showing the L-then-S round trip re-enters a height-bounded spread
band: once the absolute post-L heights are known, the subsequent S landing yields absolute final
heights (`h c + 2`, `h c + 3`, `h c + 3`) rather than the bare well-preservation of
`applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well`. Proof: the witness is `applyStep_L_skyline_noclear`'s explicit
triple update; each absolute equality and the well unfold by `Function.update_apply` plus
`split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_abs {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧
      h' c = h c + 1 ∧ h' (c + 1) = h c + 1 ∧ h' (c + 2) = h c + 2 ∧ h' w = 0 := by
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Frame-augmented post-`L` interface: the flat `L` touches only its own triple.** Strengthens
`applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_abs` with the off-triple frame conjunct
`∀ j, j ≠ c → j ≠ c + 1 → j ≠ c + 2 → h' j = h j`. The no-clear `L` rewrites the skyline to a
threefold `Function.update`, so every column outside the worked triple `c, c+1, c+2` keeps its prior
height verbatim — in particular the reserved well and the entire surrounding band are left exactly as
they were. This is the missing face the round-trip band re-entry needs: the absolute heights pin the
three worked columns while this frame pins all the others, so the post-`L` board (and, composed with
the owed `S`, the L-then-S board) can be read back into the floor-pinned band vocabulary
`IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt` column by column rather than only at the triple. Proof: the same
`Function.update_apply` / `split_ifs <;> omega` peel as the absolute interface, plus one extra
`intro j ...` branch where all three update guards miss and the height collapses to `h j`. -/
theorem applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_absFrame {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧
      h' c = h c + 1 ∧ h' (c + 1) = h c + 1 ∧ h' (c + 2) = h c + 2 ∧ h' w = 0 ∧
      (∀ j, j ≠ c → j ≠ c + 1 → j ≠ c + 2 → h' j = h j) := by
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
    ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · intro j hjc hjc1 hjc2; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **The L-then-S round trip is hole-free and keeps the reserved well — the rebuild-and-consume
atom.** Composes `applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline` with `applyStep_S_skyline_noclear`:
starting from a level triple `c, c+1, c+2` with a reserved empty well `w`, playing the L (which
re-digs the S-notch) and then the bag's owed S (which lands hole-free on that very notch) leaves
the board a reserved-well skyline — both placements are no-clear and the well `w` stays empty
(`h' w = 0`). This is the smallest end-to-end witness of the bag-phase ledger working as designed:
an L manufactures the landing site the later S needs, the S is absorbed without a hole, and the
drain column the once-per-bag I relies on is untouched throughout. Proof: obtain the post-L
S-notch skyline, rewrite to it, apply the S no-clear witness with the notch equalities as its
landing precondition, and discharge the well via `Function.update_apply` / `split_ifs`. -/
theorem applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧ h' w = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨hL, hLeq, hLnotch_eq, hLnotch_step, hLw0⟩ :=
    applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hLeq]
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_S_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hLnotch_eq hLnotch_step hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hLw0, ?_⟩
  simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Absolute heights of the L-then-S round trip.** Sharpens `applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well`
(which only kept the well) using the absolute post-L interface
`applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_abs`: starting from a level triple `c, c+1, c+2` at
height `h c` with a reserved empty well `w`, after the L rebuilds the S-notch and the owed S
lands on it, the triple sits at `(h c + 2, h c + 3, h c + 3)` and the well is still empty. So the
rebuild-and-consume pair raises the triple's surface by `+2` on its low column and `+3` on the
other two — a fully pinned two-piece transition, the height face the spread-band re-entry
argument needs (the surface stays within a bounded band above the reset level). Proof: obtain the
absolute post-L skyline, rewrite to it, feed the absolute equalities to the S no-clear witness as
its notch precondition (`omega`), then read each post-S column by `colHeight_skyline` plus
`Function.update_apply` / `split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) c = h c + 2 ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 1) = h c + 3 ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 2) = h c + 3 ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) w = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨hL, hLeq, hLc, hLc1, hLc2, hLw0⟩ :=
    applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hLeq, applyStep_S_skyline_noclear (h := hL) hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hLw0]
  refine ⟨?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc1]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc2]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hw]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Off-triple frame of the L-then-S round trip.** The frame companion to
`colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs` (which pins the three worked columns to `h c + 2, h c + 3,
h c + 3`): every column `j` outside the worked triple `c, c+1, c+2` (and inside the board,
`j < cfg.cols`) reads back its original height `h j` after the L-then-S pair. Both moves are no-clear
threefold `Function.update`s on the same triple, so the frame-augmented post-`L` interface carries
`hL j = h j` off the triple and the post-`S` update misses `j` as well. Together with the absolute
on-triple heights and the well face this is the full column profile of the rebuild-and-consume atom:
the surface is raised only on `c, c+1, c+2`, the reserved well stays empty, and the entire
surrounding band is untouched — exactly the data the floor-pinned band re-entry consumes to read the
post-pair board as an `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt` member. Proof: obtain the absolute frame post-`L`
skyline, rewrite through the `S` no-clear witness and `colHeight_skyline`, drop the three update
guards by `if_neg`, then close with the post-`L` frame equality. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_frame {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w j : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hj : j < cfg.cols) (hjc : j ≠ c) (hjc1 : j ≠ c + 1) (hjc2 : j ≠ c + 2) :
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) j = h j := by
  obtain ⟨hL, hLeq, hLc, hLc1, hLc2, hLw0, hLframe⟩ :=
    applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_absFrame hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hLeq, applyStep_S_skyline_noclear (h := hL) hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hLw0, colHeight_skyline hj]
  simp only [Function.update_apply]
  rw [if_neg hjc2, if_neg hjc1, if_neg hjc]
  exact hLframe j hjc hjc1 hjc2

/-- **The L-then-S board stays inside the floor-pinned band.** The band face of the
rebuild-and-consume atom: on a flat triple `c, c+1, c+2` sitting at the band floor `base`, inside a
floor-`base` spread-`s` band with a reserved empty well `w`, the L-then-S pair keeps every off-well
column inside `[base, base + s]` provided the band carries at least three units of slack (`3 ≤ s`).
The three worked columns rise to `base + 2, base + 3, base + 3` (`colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs`)
— the `+3` peak is exactly why three units of buffer are required — while every other column keeps its
prior in-band height (`colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_frame` plus the band hypothesis on `h`). This
is the per-column band-membership data the floor-pinned smart constructor
`isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline` consumes, so the next step reads the post-pair board straight
back into the carrier vocabulary `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base` at the unchanged floor.
Proof: case on whether `j` is one of the three worked columns (read its absolute height and bound by
`omega` against `3 ≤ s`) or off the triple (frame it back to `h j` and quote the band hypothesis). -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcb : h c = base) (hc1b : h (c + 1) = base) (hc2b : h (c + 2) = base)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hs3 : 3 ≤ s)
    (j : ℕ) (hj : j < cfg.cols) (hjw : j ≠ w) :
    base ≤ Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) j ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) j ≤ base + s := by
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · subst hjc
    have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).1
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · subst hjc1
      have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
        hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.1
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · subst hjc2
        have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.2.1
        omega
      · have h2 := colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_frame hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hj hjc hjc1 hjc2
        have hb := hband j hj hjw
        omega

/-- **The L-then-S round trip keeps the band at an arbitrary in-band level — the level-`f`
generalization of `colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band`.** Drops the floor-pinned hypothesis
`h c = base` (and `3 ≤ s`) in favour of a flat triple at any in-band level `f` with `base ≤ f` and
`f + 3 ≤ base + s`: starting from a flat triple `c, c+1, c+2` all reading `f` inside a spread-`s`
band (`hband`, reserved well `w`), after an L manufactures the S-notch and the owed S lands, every
in-field column `j ≠ w` still satisfies `base ≤ colHeight j ≤ base + s`. The placed triple rises to
`(f+2, f+3, f+3)` — capped by `f + 3 ≤ base + s` — while the off-triple columns keep their prior
in-band heights (`colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_frame`). This is the form the bag-phase ledger
needs to RE-DIG a reserved S-valley sitting above the band floor: the valley the next owed S
consumes need not be pinned to the exposed floor. Proof: identical case split to the floor-pinned
band bound, reading in-triple heights off the `h c`-relative
`colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs` and closing each branch by `omega` with `hflo`/`hfhi`. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band_at {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s f c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcf : h c = f) (hc1f : h (c + 1) = f) (hc2f : h (c + 2) = f)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hflo : base ≤ f) (hfhi : f + 3 ≤ base + s)
    (j : ℕ) (hj : j < cfg.cols) (hjw : j ≠ w) :
    base ≤ Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) j ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) j ≤ base + s := by
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · subst hjc
    have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).1
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · subst hjc1
      have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
        hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.1
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · subst hjc2
        have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.2.1
        omega
      · have h2 := colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_frame hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hj hjc hjc1 hjc2
        have hb := hband j hj hjw
        omega

/-- **The L move lifts the surface potential by at most two.** Same flat-triple
template as O and T: the L no-clear witness reduces the move to its profile,
`colHeight_place_L_skyline_le` caps each real column at `+2`, and the aggregator
lifts that to the scalar potential. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) + 2 := by
  apply maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le
  intro j hj
  rw [colHeight_skyline hj, applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
      ← place_L_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2]
  exact colHeight_place_L_skyline_le hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hj

/-- **L move keeps the ceiling when it lands low.** The L analogue of
`maxColHeight_applyStep_T_skyline_le_rows`: on a flat triple shelf with a reserved
well, dropping an L whose tallest cell stays under the ceiling (`h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`)
keeps the post-move potential `≤ cfg.rows`. The L raises its right column by two and
the two left columns by one, so the single bound `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows` caps all three
touched columns; untouched columns keep their prior `≤ cfg.rows` height. The
height-face packing brick for the L piece. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_le_rows {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_self]
        omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
        exact hbound j hj

/-- **A no-clear L placement adds exactly four cells.** The 3-column cell-conservation
brick (mirror of `card_applyStep_T_skyline_noclear`): on a level shelf
(`heq1, heq2`) with a reserved well, the no-clear L writes `+1, +1, +2` into columns
`c, c+1, c+2` (`applyStep_L_skyline_noclear`), summing to `+4`. Proof by the reusable
three-fold `sum_update_of_mem` / `add_sum_erase` / `erase_eq` peel: each touched column
is removed from both sides and the residual sums coincide, leaving `omega` the
arithmetic `1 + 1 + 2 = 4` modulo `heq1, heq2`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_L_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 := by
  have hc2' : c + 2 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc2
  have hc1_sdiff : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc_sdiff2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2}) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc1_erase : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  have hc_erase2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2)).erase (c + 1) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  rw [applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
    card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc2', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1_sdiff,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff2,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc2', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1_erase,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase2,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-- **J move witness, reserved well.** The mirror of `applyStep_L_skyline_noclear`:
on a flat three-column shelf `(h(c+1) = h(c+2) = h c)` with a reserved empty real
column `w`, the full J move (rotation 0, flat base with a left riser) raises the
left column by two and the two right columns by one, clearing nothing. With this
the six non-clearing pieces (O, S, Z, horizontal-I, T, L, J) are all packaged as
reserved-well no-clear moves; the lone clearing piece (vertical I in a well) is
`applyStep_vertI_well`. -/
theorem applyStep_J_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }
      = skyline cfg (Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2))
          (c + 1) (h c + 1)) (c + 2) (h c + 1)) :=
  applyStep_skyline_of_place_eq (place_J_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2)
    ⟨w, hw, by rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1,
                   Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **A flat J manufactures a Z-notch — the mirror valley REBUILDER brick.** Dropping a
no-clear J on a level triple shelf `c, c+1, c+2` (with a reserved empty well) writes the
profile `+2, +1, +1`, leaving the three columns at heights `h c + 2, h c + 1, h c + 1`.
Those heights satisfy the exact Z-notch convention consumed by
`colHeight_applyStep_skyline_Znotch_of_avoid` and the Z-piece landing lemmas: the lone high
step stands at the left (`colHeight c = colHeight (c+1) + 1`) and the floor pair is level
(`colHeight (c+2) = colHeight (c+1)`). So a J played flat re-digs the mirror staircase an
owed Z draw needs — the mirror of `colHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_makes_Snotch`, completing
the L and J valley-rebuilder pair the bag-phase ledger uses to re-present the two notch
sites each bag owes. Proof: rewrite by `applyStep_J_skyline_noclear` to land on the explicit
triple `Function.update`, then peel each update with `Function.update_of_ne` /
`Function.update_self`. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_J_skyline_makes_Znotch {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }) c = h c + 2 ∧
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 1) = h c + 1 ∧
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 2) = h c + 1 := by
  rw [applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_self]
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc2, Function.update_self]

/-- **A flat J touches only its own triple — the frame companion to
`colHeight_applyStep_J_skyline_makes_Znotch`.** Mirror of `colHeight_applyStep_L_skyline_frame`:
a no-clear J at column `c` writes only columns `c, c+1, c+2`; the reserved well `w` stays
empty (`colHeight w = 0`) and every other in-field column keeps its prior height
(`colHeight j = h j`). With the makes-Znotch heights this fully characterises the post-J
surface: a Z-notch mirror staircase at the placed triple, the band untouched everywhere
else — the input the downstream "J re-digs a Z-notch in an otherwise level band" step needs.
Proof: the same `Function.update_of_ne` well peel and off-triple peel as the L frame, against
`applyStep_J_skyline_noclear`'s explicit triple update. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_J_skyline_frame {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }) w = 0 ∧
    ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ c → j ≠ c + 1 → j ≠ c + 2 →
      colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }) j = h j := by
  rw [applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hw, Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1,
        Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0
  · intro j hj hjc hjc1 hjc2
    rw [colHeight_skyline hj, Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1,
        Function.update_of_ne hjc]

/-- **Post-J board is a Z-notch skyline — the mirror rebuild-to-consume interface.** Mirror
of `applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline`: after a no-clear J on a level triple (with
reserved well `w`), the board is `skyline cfg h'` for an explicit `h'` whose triple satisfies
the Z-notch precondition `applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear` consumes (`h' (c+1) = h' (c+2)`,
`h' c = h' (c+1) + 1`) and whose well stays empty (`h' w = 0`). So the next owed Z draw lands
hole-free at the column the J rebuilt: `obtain ⟨h', hb, heq, hstep, hw0'⟩ := …; rw [hb]; exact
applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hstep hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0'`. Proof: the witness is
`applyStep_J_skyline_noclear`'s explicit triple update; each notch/well equality unfolds by
`Function.update_apply` and `split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧
      h' (c + 1) = h' (c + 2) ∧ h' c = h' (c + 1) + 1 ∧ h' w = 0 := by
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **The J-then-Z round trip is hole-free and keeps the reserved well — the mirror
rebuild-and-consume atom.** Companion to `applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well`. Composes
`applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline` with `applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear`: a flat J on a
level triple (with reserved well `w`) manufactures the Z-notch the next owed Z draw consumes,
and after the Z lands the board is again a `skyline cfg h'` with the well still empty
(`h' w = 0`). So J rebuilds and Z consumes the valley the bag ledger owes, with the drain
column untouched throughout. Proof: obtain the post-J Z-notch skyline, rewrite the board to it,
feed its notch equalities to the Z no-clear witness, and discharge the well by
`Function.update_apply` plus `split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem applyStep_JZ_skyline_flat_well {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧ h' w = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨hJ, hJeq, hJnotch_eq, hJnotch_step, hJw0⟩ :=
    applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hJeq]
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hJnotch_eq hJnotch_step hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hJw0, ?_⟩
  simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Post-J board is a Z-notch skyline with absolute heights exposed.** Mirror of
`applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_abs`, strengthening
`applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline` from the relative Z-notch equalities to absolute
heights against the flat input level `h c`: `h' c = h c + 2`, `h' (c+1) = h c + 1`,
`h' (c+2) = h c + 1`, well still empty. This pins where the post-J surface sits so the subsequent
owed-Z landing yields absolute final heights — the height face the J-then-Z spread-band re-entry
argument needs. Proof: the witness is `applyStep_J_skyline_noclear`'s explicit triple update; each
absolute equality and the well unfold by `Function.update_apply` plus `split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_abs {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧
      h' c = h c + 2 ∧ h' (c + 1) = h c + 1 ∧ h' (c + 2) = h c + 1 ∧ h' w = 0 := by
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **A flat J manufactures a Z-notch and touches nothing else — the absolute height face
with an off-triple frame.** Mirror of `applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_absFrame`,
augmenting `applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_abs` with the frame conjunct
`∀ j, j ≠ c → j ≠ c+1 → j ≠ c+2 → h' j = h j`: a no-clear J on a level triple at height `h c`
(with reserved empty well `w`) writes only its three columns to `(h c + 2, h c + 1, h c + 1)`,
keeps the well empty, and leaves every other column at its prior height. Bundling the absolute
heights with the off-triple frame in one witness is exactly what the J-then-Z band re-entry step
consumes, so it need not re-derive the untouched columns separately. Proof: the witness is
`applyStep_J_skyline_noclear`'s explicit triple update; each height, the well, and the frame
unfold by `Function.update_apply` plus `split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_absFrame {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    ∃ h' : ℕ → ℕ,
      Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }
        = skyline cfg h' ∧
      h' c = h c + 2 ∧ h' (c + 1) = h c + 1 ∧ h' (c + 2) = h c + 1 ∧ h' w = 0 ∧
      (∀ j, j ≠ c → j ≠ c + 1 → j ≠ c + 2 → h' j = h j) := by
  refine ⟨_, applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
    ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · intro j hjc hjc1 hjc2; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Absolute heights of the J-then-Z round trip.** Mirror of `colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_abs`,
using the absolute post-J interface `applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_abs`: starting from a
level triple `c, c+1, c+2` at height `h c` with a reserved empty well `w`, after the J rebuilds the
Z-notch and the owed Z lands on it, the triple sits at `(h c + 3, h c + 3, h c + 2)` and the well is
still empty. So the mirror rebuild-and-consume pair raises the triple by `+3` on its two high
columns and `+2` on the low one — the symmetric height face (peak `h c + 3`, matching the L-then-S
pair) the spread-band re-entry argument needs for the bag's owed Z. Proof: obtain the absolute
post-J skyline, rewrite to it, feed the absolute equalities to the Z no-clear witness as its notch
precondition (`omega`), then read each post-Z column by `colHeight_skyline` plus
`Function.update_apply` / `split_ifs <;> omega`. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) c = h c + 3 ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 1) = h c + 3 ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) (c + 2) = h c + 2 ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) w = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨hJ, hJeq, hJc, hJc1, hJc2, hJw0⟩ :=
    applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hJeq, applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear (h := hJ) hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hJw0]
  refine ⟨?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc1]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hc2]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hw]; simp only [Function.update_apply]; split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **The J-then-Z round trip touches only its own triple — the off-triple frame.** Mirror of
`colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_frame`: after a no-clear J on a level triple (with reserved well
`w`) manufactures the Z-notch and the owed Z lands on it, every column `j` outside the placed
triple `c, c+1, c+2` keeps its prior height (`colHeight j = h j`). Together with
`colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs`'s in-triple heights this pins the whole post-JZ surface: a
`+3, +3, +2` bump at the triple, everything else untouched — the readback the J-then-Z band
re-entry step needs to certify the band bound off the triple. Proof: obtain the framed absolute
post-J interface (iter440), rewrite the J board to it, feed the in-triple heights to the Z
no-clear witness as its mirror-notch precondition (`omega`), read the off-triple column by
`colHeight_skyline`, peel the three `Function.update`s with `if_neg`, then close by the post-J
frame. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_frame {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w j : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hj : j < cfg.cols) (hjc : j ≠ c) (hjc1 : j ≠ c + 1) (hjc2 : j ≠ c + 2) :
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) j = h j := by
  obtain ⟨hJ, hJeq, hJc, hJc1, hJc2, hJw0, hJframe⟩ :=
    applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_absFrame hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hJeq, applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear (h := hJ) hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hJw0, colHeight_skyline hj]
  simp only [Function.update_apply]
  rw [if_neg hjc2, if_neg hjc1, if_neg hjc]
  exact hJframe j hjc hjc1 hjc2

/-- **The J-then-Z round trip keeps the band — every column stays in `[base, base+s]`.** Mirror
of `colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band`: starting from a flat triple `c, c+1, c+2` at the band
floor `base` inside a spread-`s` band (`hband`, with reserved empty well `w`) and `3 ≤ s`, after
the J manufactures the Z-notch and the owed Z lands, every in-field column `j ≠ w` still satisfies
`base ≤ colHeight j ≤ base + s`. The placed triple rises to `(base+3, base+3, base+2)` — capped by
`3 ≤ s` — while `colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_frame` keeps every other column at its prior
in-band height. So the mirror rebuild-and-consume pair never breaches the reservoir band, the
band-membership face the J-then-Z spread re-entry packages. Proof: case on whether `j` hits the
triple; in-triple columns read off `colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs` and close by `omega` with
`hs3`, the off-triple column uses the frame plus `hband`. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_band {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcb : h c = base) (hc1b : h (c + 1) = base) (hc2b : h (c + 2) = base)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hs3 : 3 ≤ s)
    (j : ℕ) (hj : j < cfg.cols) (hjw : j ≠ w) :
    base ≤ Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) j ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) j ≤ base + s := by
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · subst hjc
    have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).1
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · subst hjc1
      have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
        hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.1
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · subst hjc2
        have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.2.1
        omega
      · have h2 := colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_frame hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hj hjc hjc1 hjc2
        have hb := hband j hj hjw
        omega

/-- **Level-`f` JZ band bound.** Generalizes `colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_band`
from a floor-pinned triple (`h c = base`) to a triple resting at an arbitrary interior
level `f`, requiring only `base ≤ f` and `f + 3 ≤ base + s`. The J-then-Z round trip
peaks at `f + 3` on its two lip columns and bottoms at `f + 2` on the third, so this is
exactly the slack the band must reserve to host a JZ atom whose feet sit above the floor.
Mirror of `colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band_at`. -/
theorem colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_band_at {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s f c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcf : h c = f) (hc1f : h (c + 1) = f) (hc2f : h (c + 2) = f)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hflo : base ≤ f) (hfhi : f + 3 ≤ base + s)
    (j : ℕ) (hj : j < cfg.cols) (hjw : j ≠ w) :
    base ≤ Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) j ∧
    Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) j ≤ base + s := by
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · subst hjc
    have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).1
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · subst hjc1
      have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
        hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.1
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · subst hjc2
        have h2 := (colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_abs hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0).2.2.1
        omega
      · have h2 := colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_frame hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
          hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hj hjc hjc1 hjc2
        have hb := hband j hj hjw
        omega

/-- **The J move lifts the surface potential by at most two.** The mirror of the L
debit and the last of the flat-triple pieces, via the same template: J's no-clear
witness reduces the move to its profile, `colHeight_place_J_skyline_le` caps each
real column at `+2`, and the aggregator lifts that to the scalar potential. With
this, four of the seven pieces (O, T, L, J) have their `+2` potential debit; the
horizontal I (`+1`) and the two notch pieces S, Z (`+2`) remain. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_J_skyline_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) + 2 := by
  apply maxColHeight_le_of_forall_colHeight_le
  intro j hj
  rw [colHeight_skyline hj, applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
      ← place_J_skyline hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2]
  exact colHeight_place_J_skyline_le hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hj

/-- **Ceiling-preserving J placement.** The mirror-J companion to the O/T/L
templates: on a flat shelf (`heq1`, `heq2`) with a reserved well held empty
(`hw0`), a pointwise ceiling (`hbound`) plus a low landing (`hlow : h c + 2 ≤
rows`) forces the post-placement surface potential to stay `≤ cfg.rows`
**outright** — no `+2` drift. J writes `h c + 2` into column `c` and `h c + 1`
into `c+1, c+2`; each touched column is `≤ h c + 2 ≤ rows`, and the untouched
columns inherit `hbound`. Completes the flat-triple ceiling family (O, T, L, J);
horizontal I (`+1`) and the notch pieces S, Z remain. -/
theorem maxColHeight_applyStep_J_skyline_le_rows {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hlow : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    maxColHeight cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
      ≤ cfg.rows := by
  rw [applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  apply maxColHeight_skyline_le_of_forall
  intro j hj
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · rw [hjc, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 2),
        Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c ≠ c + 1), Function.update_self]
    omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · rw [hjc1, Function.update_of_ne (by omega : c + 1 ≠ c + 2), Function.update_self]
      omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · rw [hjc2, Function.update_self]
        omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2, Function.update_of_ne hjc1, Function.update_of_ne hjc]
        exact hbound j hj

/-- **A no-clear J placement adds exactly four cells.** The 3-column cell-conservation
brick completing the flat-triple trio (O, T, L, J): on a level shelf (`heq1, heq2`)
with a reserved well, the no-clear J writes `+2, +1, +1` into columns `c, c+1, c+2`
(`applyStep_J_skyline_noclear`), summing to `+4`. Same reusable three-fold
`sum_update_of_mem` / `add_sum_erase` / `erase_eq` peel as T and L: each touched column
is removed from both sides and the residual sums coincide, leaving `omega` the
arithmetic `2 + 1 + 1 = 4` modulo `heq1, heq2`. -/
theorem card_applyStep_J_skyline_noclear {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0) :
    (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }).card
      = (skyline cfg h).card + 4 := by
  have hc2' : c + 2 ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mpr hc2
  have hc1_sdiff : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc_sdiff2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols) \ {c + 2}) \ {c + 1} := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_range, Finset.mem_singleton]; omega
  have hc1_erase : c + 1 ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  have hc_erase2 : c ∈ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase (c + 2)).erase (c + 1) := by
    simp only [Finset.mem_erase, Finset.mem_range]; omega
  rw [applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0,
    card_skyline, card_skyline,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc2', Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc1_sdiff,
    Finset.sum_update_of_mem hc_sdiff2,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc2', ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc1_erase,
    ← Finset.add_sum_erase _ h hc_erase2,
    Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq, Finset.erase_eq]
  omega

/-! ### Bounded-skyline carrier: the reusable height discharge

A board is a *bounded skyline* when it equals `skyline cfg h` for some profile `h`
capped by `cfg.rows` on every real column. This is the carrier shape of every
reserved-well invariant: hole-free, profile-recoverable, and closed-form enough
that the `hheight` obligation of the bag-indexed reduction discharges uniformly —
via `colHeight_skyline_le` — with no reference to any closure argument. Isolating
this obligation lets the genuinely hard part of the assembly (closure under the
adversarial bag) be attacked on its own. -/

/-- A board that is a height-bounded skyline. -/
def IsBoundedSkyline (cfg : GameConfig) (b : Board) : Prop :=
  ∃ h : ℕ → ℕ, b = skyline cfg h ∧ ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows

/-- **Bounded skylines discharge `hheight`.** Every bounded skyline has every
column height at most `cfg.rows`. This is the reusable height obligation for any
skyline-carried bag-indexed invariant, dischargeable independently of closure. -/
theorem colHeight_le_of_isBoundedSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsBoundedSkyline cfg b) (j : ℕ) : colHeight b j ≤ cfg.rows := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound⟩ := hb
  exact colHeight_skyline_le hbound j

/-- **Bounded skylines are never lost (`hheight ⇒ ¬ lost`).** The root non-loss
certificate for the entire skyline carrier hierarchy: any height-bounded skyline
keeps every column profile within the field, so no filled cell reaches row
`cfg.rows`. Strictly more general than `not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline` —
every reserved-well, flat-top, and notched face forgets down to an
`IsBoundedSkyline`, so each inherits its `¬ lost` obligation through this single
lemma rather than re-deriving the skyline height bound. -/
theorem not_isLost_of_isBoundedSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsBoundedSkyline cfg b) : ¬ Board.isLost cfg b := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound⟩ := hb
  exact not_isLost_skyline hbound

/-- **Gravity preserves the bounded-skyline carrier.** Clearing lines on a
height-bounded skyline yields another height-bounded skyline: the minimum real-
column profile `m` is subtracted uniformly (`clearLines_skyline`), and
`h j - m ≤ h j ≤ cfg.rows` keeps every column within the field. This is the
clear-step half of any per-bag closure argument — placement may raise the
surface, but the subsequent line clear lands back inside `IsBoundedSkyline`
rather than escaping the carrier. Needs `0 < cfg.cols` so the profile minimum
over the real columns exists. -/
theorem isBoundedSkyline_clearLines {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hcols : 0 < cfg.cols) (hb : IsBoundedSkyline cfg b) :
    IsBoundedSkyline cfg (Board.clearLines cfg b) := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound⟩ := hb
  have hne : (Finset.range cfg.cols).Nonempty := Finset.nonempty_range_iff.mpr hcols.ne'
  have hm : ∀ j < cfg.cols, (Finset.range cfg.cols).inf' hne h ≤ h j :=
    fun j hj => Finset.inf'_le h (Finset.mem_range.mpr hj)
  have hm0 : ∃ j < cfg.cols, h j = (Finset.range cfg.cols).inf' hne h := by
    obtain ⟨a, ha, hae⟩ := Finset.exists_mem_eq_inf' hne h
    exact ⟨a, Finset.mem_range.mp ha, hae.symm⟩
  rw [clearLines_skyline hm hm0]
  exact ⟨fun j => h j - (Finset.range cfg.cols).inf' hne h, rfl,
    fun j hj => le_trans (Nat.sub_le _ _) (hbound j hj)⟩

/-- **The empty board is a bounded skyline (`hinit`).** The zero profile is the
empty board and is trivially capped by `cfg.rows`, discharging the initial
obligation of the bag-indexed reduction for the bounded-skyline carrier. With
`colHeight_le_of_isBoundedSkyline` this leaves closure (`hstep`) as the sole
remaining obligation any reserved-well invariant must meet. -/
theorem isBoundedSkyline_empty (cfg : GameConfig) :
    IsBoundedSkyline cfg Board.empty :=
  ⟨fun _ => 0, (skyline_zero_eq_empty cfg).symm, fun _ _ => Nat.zero_le _⟩

/-- **A potential ceiling certifies a bounded skyline.** If a skyline's surface
potential is at most `cfg.rows`, it is a bounded skyline: each column height is
the profile (`colHeight_skyline`) and sits below the potential
(`colHeight_le_maxColHeight`), so the per-column field bound the carrier demands
holds for free. This is the bridge from the *scalar* invariant the potential
machinery actually maintains (`maxColHeight ≤ rows`, pushed up `+2` per placement
and pulled down by `maxColHeight_clearLines_skyline_le`) to membership in the
bounded-skyline carrier of the headline reduction — so a height-bounding argument
phrased purely in `maxColHeight` discharges the carrier-membership half of
`hstep` without ever touching individual columns. -/
theorem isBoundedSkyline_of_maxColHeight_le {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    (hM : maxColHeight cfg (skyline cfg h) ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsBoundedSkyline cfg (skyline cfg h) := by
  refine ⟨h, rfl, ?_⟩
  intro j hj
  have h1 : colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ cfg.rows :=
    le_trans (colHeight_le_maxColHeight hj) hM
  rwa [colHeight_skyline hj] at h1

/-- **Skyline shape plus a potential ceiling certifies a bounded skyline.** The
applyStep-facing generalisation of `isBoundedSkyline_of_maxColHeight_le`: it drops
the *syntactic* `skyline cfg h` requirement and accepts any board merely *equal*
to a skyline (`heq`) together with a potential ceiling (`hM`). This is the keystone
for the bounded-skyline `hstep`: each move's `applyStep_*_skyline_noclear` witness
supplies `heq` (the post-drop board is a skyline) and the matching
`maxColHeight_applyStep_*_skyline_le_rows` supplies `hM`, so all seven placements
land their *carrier-membership* obligation through this one bridge — no per-piece
membership wrapper, no `Function.update` bookkeeping at the call site. -/
theorem isBoundedSkyline_of_eq_skyline_of_maxColHeight_le {cfg : GameConfig}
    {b : Board} {h : ℕ → ℕ} (heq : b = skyline cfg h)
    (hM : maxColHeight cfg b ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsBoundedSkyline cfg b := by
  rw [heq] at hM ⊢
  exact isBoundedSkyline_of_maxColHeight_le hM

/-- **A bounded skyline has potential at most `cfg.rows`.** The scalar *forward*
companion to `isBoundedSkyline_of_maxColHeight_le`: where that lemma turns a
potential ceiling into carrier membership, this one extracts the ceiling back out
of membership. Since a bounded skyline's profile is capped column-by-column and the
potential is the supremum of that profile (`maxColHeight_skyline`), the cap lifts to
the supremum. Together the two directions give the equivalence
`IsBoundedSkyline (skyline h) ↔ maxColHeight ≤ rows`, so a closure argument may move
freely between the *scalar* potential the height machinery actually maintains and
the *carrier membership* the headline reduction consumes — neither phrasing loses
information. -/
theorem maxColHeight_le_rows_of_isBoundedSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsBoundedSkyline cfg b) : maxColHeight cfg b ≤ cfg.rows := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound⟩ := hb
  rw [maxColHeight_skyline]
  exact Finset.sup_le (fun j hj => hbound j (Finset.mem_range.mp hj))

/-! ### Reserved-well refinement: priming the no-clear bridge

The bounded-skyline carrier proves height-boundedness but says nothing about
*where* a piece may land without completing a row. Every `applyStep_*_skyline_noclear`
witness needs a real column the placement keeps empty — that is exactly a *well*.
The refinement below augments the carrier with a designated well column `w` held
at height `0`. The well is what discharges the `hzero` premise of the no-clear
bridge for any flat-region drop, so a reserved-well closure can quote the seven
move witnesses directly. A reserved-well skyline is still a bounded skyline, so
it inherits the height discharge unchanged; only `hinit` needs the (always
available, via `cfg.cols_pos`) positivity of `cfg.cols` to seat the initial well. -/

/-- A bounded skyline carrying a designated empty *well* column `w < cfg.cols`
held at height `0`. -/
def IsReservedWellSkyline (cfg : GameConfig) (b : Board) : Prop :=
  ∃ h : ℕ → ℕ, b = skyline cfg h ∧ (∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) ∧
    ∃ w < cfg.cols, h w = 0

/-- A reserved-well skyline is in particular a bounded skyline (forget the well). -/
theorem isBoundedSkyline_of_isReservedWellSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) : IsBoundedSkyline cfg b := by
  obtain ⟨h, hbeq, hbound, _⟩ := hb
  exact ⟨h, hbeq, hbound⟩

/-- **Reserved-well skylines discharge `hheight`.** Inherited from the bounded
forgetful image, so no closure reasoning is needed. -/
theorem colHeight_le_of_isReservedWellSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) (j : ℕ) : colHeight b j ≤ cfg.rows :=
  colHeight_le_of_isBoundedSkyline (isBoundedSkyline_of_isReservedWellSkyline hb) j

/-- **The reserved well is an externally empty column (`colHeight` readback).**
Surfaces the internal profile witness `h w = 0` of a reserved-well skyline as the
engine-level fact `colHeight b w = 0` for some in-range column `w`. This is the
precondition a regulator drop needs stated at the `Board.colHeight` abstraction:
the well column is genuinely empty, so a vertical I (or any well filler) has full
clearance there. Proved by reading the profile back through `colHeight_skyline`. -/
theorem exists_well_colHeight_eq_zero_of_isReservedWellSkyline {cfg : GameConfig}
    {b : Board} (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) :
    ∃ w, w < cfg.cols ∧ colHeight b w = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, _, w, hw, hw0⟩ := hb
  exact ⟨w, hw, by rw [colHeight_skyline hw]; exact hw0⟩

/-- **Reserved-well skylines discharge the *scalar* height ceiling.** The
`maxColHeight` companion to `colHeight_le_of_isReservedWellSkyline`: forget the
well to land in the bounded-skyline carrier, then read off its potential ceiling
(`maxColHeight_le_rows_of_isBoundedSkyline`). Lets a potential-route closure on the
reserved-well carrier quote `maxColHeight ≤ rows` straight from membership, without
unfolding to per-column heights. -/
theorem maxColHeight_le_rows_of_isReservedWellSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) : maxColHeight cfg b ≤ cfg.rows :=
  maxColHeight_le_rows_of_isBoundedSkyline (isBoundedSkyline_of_isReservedWellSkyline hb)

/-- **A reserved-well skyline is never lost.** The survival certificate at the very
abstraction every per-piece landing concludes in: each `isReservedWellSkyline_*_hstep`
fragment (and the flat/notched all-seven dispatches) produces an `IsReservedWellSkyline`
post-board, and this lemma certifies *that predicate alone* rules out a top-out. Unfold
to the witnessing skyline (`b = skyline cfg h` with the profile bounded by `cfg.rows` on
every real column) and apply `not_isLost_skyline`. Lifts non-loss from the concrete
`skyline cfg h` form to the reserved-well carrier, so a closure argument can discharge
`¬ isLost` straight from a landing fragment's conclusion without re-exhibiting the
profile — the `Board`-level companion to `maxColHeight_le_rows_of_isReservedWellSkyline`. -/
theorem not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) : ¬ Board.isLost cfg b := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound, _⟩ := hb
  exact not_isLost_skyline hbound

/-- **A reserved-well skyline is well-formed.** The structural companion to
`not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline`: unpack the carrier to its profile `h`, so the
board is `skyline cfg h`, then `mem_skyline'` lands every filled cell in a real column
(`p.1 < cfg.cols`), which is exactly `Board.WF`. This is the base well-formedness brick
the whole reserved-well tower (flat-top, notched, spread-bounded) inherits, discharging
the `WF` side-condition every carrier-indexed survival argument owes alongside the
`¬ isLost` bridge. The `Board.WF` analogue of `not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline`. -/
theorem wf_of_isReservedWellSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) : Board.WF cfg b := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, _, _⟩ := hb
  intro p hp
  exact ((mem_skyline' cfg h p).mp hp).1

/-- **Cell budget of a reserved-well skyline.** Every column sits at height `≤ rows`
and the well column `w` is empty, so the `cfg.cols` column heights sum to at most
`(cfg.cols - 1) * cfg.rows` — one full column of headroom is permanently reserved
by the empty well, strictly sharper than the whole-grid `cfg.cols * cfg.rows`. The
generic ledger behind `bagPhaseCarrier_card_le`: peel the empty well off the
column-height sum (`Finset.sum_erase`, `s` explicit) and cap each of the remaining
`cfg.cols - 1` columns by `cfg.rows` (`Finset.sum_le_card_nsmul`). -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_card_le {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) :
    b.card ≤ (cfg.cols - 1) * cfg.rows := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound, w, hw, hw0⟩ := hb
  rw [card_skyline, ← Finset.sum_erase (Finset.range cfg.cols) hw0]
  calc ∑ j ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w, h j
      ≤ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w).card • cfg.rows :=
        Finset.sum_le_card_nsmul _ _ _
          (fun j hj => hbound j (Finset.mem_range.mp (Finset.mem_of_mem_erase hj)))
    _ = (cfg.cols - 1) * cfg.rows := by
        rw [Finset.card_erase_of_mem (Finset.mem_range.mpr hw), Finset.card_range, smul_eq_mul]

/-- **Surface-potential-tight area ledger for the reserved-well carrier.** The
`maxColHeight`-parameterized sharpening of `isReservedWellSkyline_card_le`: a reserved-well skyline
whose surface potential is capped by `M` holds at most `(cols - 1) * M` filled cells. Two strict
improvements over the existing area bounds fused into one: it BANKS the empty well (the `cols - 1`
factor, vs the whole-grid `cols * M` of `card_le_mul_maxColHeight`) AND replaces the static `rows`
ceiling with the live potential `M` (vs the `(cols - 1) * rows` of `isReservedWellSkyline_card_le`).
The proof erases the zero-height well column from the column sum, then bounds each remaining column by
`M` through `colHeight_le_maxColHeight` (instead of by `rows` through the skyline bound). On the
post-regulator endpoint, where `maxColHeight ≤ 5` (the bag cycle's reset ceiling), this reads
`card ≤ (10 - 1) * 5 = 45` — the conserved area potential the drain-FORCING pigeonhole consumes: a bag
that would push the surface above this budget must have triggered a line clear, so the regulator's
collapse is not optional but forced by the area ledger. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_card_le_of_maxColHeight_le {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board} {M : ℕ}
    (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) (hM : maxColHeight cfg b ≤ M) :
    b.card ≤ (cfg.cols - 1) * M := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound, w, hw, hw0⟩ := hb
  rw [card_skyline, ← Finset.sum_erase (Finset.range cfg.cols) hw0]
  calc ∑ j ∈ (Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w, h j
      ≤ ((Finset.range cfg.cols).erase w).card • M :=
        Finset.sum_le_card_nsmul _ _ _
          (fun j hj => by
            have hjr : j < cfg.cols := Finset.mem_range.mp (Finset.mem_of_mem_erase hj)
            have h1 : colHeight (skyline cfg h) j ≤ M :=
              le_trans (colHeight_le_maxColHeight hjr) hM
            rwa [colHeight_skyline hjr] at h1)
    _ = (cfg.cols - 1) * M := by
        rw [Finset.card_erase_of_mem (Finset.mem_range.mpr hw), Finset.card_range, smul_eq_mul]

/-- **The reserved-well carrier re-enters itself under the vertical-I well-clear.**
The generic-surface generalisation of `isFlatTopRWSkyline_vertI_reenters`: on *any*
reserved-well skyline whose non-well columns all stand at height `≥ 4` (`hothers`),
dropping a vertical `I` into the empty well clears four full rows and lowers every
non-well column by exactly `4`, landing back in a reserved-well skyline with the
*same* empty well. No flatness is required — the well-clear is the height regulator
on the whole reserved-well family, not just the flat-top face. The post-move profile
`j ↦ if j = w then 0 else h j - 4` is bounded (`h j - 4 ≤ h j ≤ rows`) and keeps the
well empty, so the carrier membership is immediate from `applyStep_vertI_well`. This
is the height-regulating `I` brick a wider all-seven carrier (task #72) needs on its
roughened, non-flat surfaces. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_vertI_reenters {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hwell : h w = 0) (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h j) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }) := by
  rw [applyStep_vertI_well hw hwell hothers]
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else h j - 4, rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    show (if j = w then 0 else h j - 4) ≤ cfg.rows
    split
    · exact Nat.zero_le _
    · have := hbound j hj; omega
  · show (if w = w then 0 else h w - 4) = 0
    rw [if_pos rfl]

/-- **Assembled `hstep` fragment: vertical I on the generic reserved-well carrier.**
The generic-surface analogue of `isFlatTopRWSkyline_vertI_hstep`: packages
`isReservedWellSkyline_vertI_reenters` into the `∃ pl, piece ∧ Valid ∧ carrier`
shape a closure obligation consumes. Validity is just `w < cfg.cols` (the vertical
`I` is one column wide — every shape cell has column offset `0`), and re-entry maps
any reserved-well surface whose non-well columns stand at `≥ 4` back into the
reserved-well family, lowered by four. So on *any* reserved-well surface (not only
the flat face) the height-regulating `I` branch of `hstep` is closed outright — the
sole cell-removing move that offsets the other pieces' upward raises, now available
to a wider all-seven carrier (task #72). -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_vertI_hstep {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hwell : h w = 0) (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h j) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.I ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_vertI_reenters hw hbound hwell hothers⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have h0 : ∀ c ∈ Piece.I.shapeUp 1, c.1 = 0 := by decide
  have hcz := h0 cell hcell
  show w + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **Vertical-I *filler* preserves the reserved-well carrier.** The complement of the
well-clear fragment (`isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_vertI_well`): dropping the vertical I
on a *non-well* column `c ≠ w` that has four cells of headroom (`h c + 4 ≤ cfg.rows`) lands
back inside `IsReservedWellSkyline`. Since the well stays empty, no row completes
(`applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear`), so the move is the bare profile raise
`Function.update h c (h c + 4)`: column `c` rises by four and stays under the ceiling
(`hroom`), every other real column is untouched and inherits its bound (`hbound`), and the
reserved well `w` is left at `0` (`w ≠ c`). This is the *stack-building* move a per-bag
schedule iterates — raising the well's neighbours toward height `≥ 4` one column at a time —
before the terminal well-clear (`isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_vertI_well`) cashes them in
for a four-row drop. Together the filler and the clear close *both* vertical-I roles on the
reserved-well carrier, the engine of the I-only height regulator (#72). -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_vertI {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hroom : h c + 4 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_vertI_skyline_noclear hc hw hwc hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update h c (h c + 4), rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    by_cases hjc : j = c
    · rw [hjc, Function.update_self]; exact hroom
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc]; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0

/-- **The vertical-I fill fold stays inside the reserved-well carrier.** The membership
companion to `foldl_vertI_skyline`: filling a duplicate-free list `cs` of non-well columns
with vertical-I drops — each column real and distinct from the reserved well `w` (`hcs`,
`hnodup`, `hwnotmem`) and starting with four cells of headroom
(`hbound : h j + 4 ≤ cfg.rows`) — lands on a board that is still an `IsReservedWellSkyline`.
The fold rewrites to `skyline cfg (cs.foldl … h)` (`foldl_vertI_skyline`); every column
rises by at most four (`foldl_update_nodup_le`) so stays under the ceiling, and the well is
never touched (`foldl_update_skip`) so it stays empty. This lifts the single-column filler
(`isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_vertI`) to a whole fill *phase*: a bag schedule may pile
the well's neighbours up column-by-column and remain in the carrier the entire time, with
the once-per-bag well-clear still pending. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_foldl_vertI_fill {cfg : GameConfig} {cs : List ℕ}
    {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ} (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hcs : ∀ c ∈ cs, c < cfg.cols ∧ c ≠ w)
    (hnodup : cs.Nodup) (hwnotmem : w ∉ cs) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j + 4 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      ((cs.map (fun c => ({ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := c } : Placement))).foldl
          (fun acc pl => pl.applyStep cfg acc) (skyline cfg h)) := by
  rw [foldl_vertI_skyline hw cs hcs hw0]
  refine ⟨cs.foldl (fun g c => Function.update g c (g c + 4)) h, rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    have hle := foldl_update_nodup_le hnodup (h := h) j
    have hb := hbound j hj
    omega
  · rw [foldl_update_skip cs hwnotmem]; exact hw0

/-- **The empty board is a reserved-well skyline (`hinit`).** The zero profile is
the empty board, trivially capped, and column `0` (real since `cfg.cols_pos`)
serves as the initial empty well. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_empty (cfg : GameConfig) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg Board.empty :=
  ⟨fun _ => 0, (skyline_zero_eq_empty cfg).symm, fun _ _ => Nat.zero_le _,
    ⟨0, cfg.cols_pos, rfl⟩⟩

/-- **The reserved-well carrier follows from a height bound plus a sub-row packing
budget.** A bounded skyline holding strictly fewer than `cfg.cols` cells is a
reserved-well skyline: the height cap supplies the bound field and
`exists_colHeight_eq_zero_of_card_lt` supplies the empty well. This packages the
packing→reserved-well bridge as a carrier membership, so a card-budget invariant can
hand the seven no-clear move witnesses their required well with no extra work. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_of_bounded_card_lt {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hcard : (skyline cfg h).card < cfg.cols) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (skyline cfg h) := by
  obtain ⟨w, hw, hzero⟩ := exists_colHeight_eq_zero_of_card_lt hcard
  rw [Finset.mem_range] at hw
  rw [colHeight_skyline hw] at hzero
  exact ⟨h, rfl, hbound, w, hw, hzero⟩

/-- **A reserved-well skyline within the non-well card budget has a safe landing
site.** The predicate-level lift of `exists_ne_well_colHeight_le_of_card_le`:
unpacking the carrier exposes its empty well `w` (`colHeight b w = 0`) and, when the
cell count fits the non-well budget `(cfg.cols - 1) * M`, exhibits a *real* column
`j ≠ w` at height `≤ M`. Because an ordinary (non-clearing) placement must avoid the
reserved well, the landing column has to come from the non-well columns; this lemma
delivers it straight from carrier membership plus a card bound, with no explicit
profile in sight. With `M := cfg.rows - 2` this is exactly the guaranteed flat-shelf
landing the seven per-piece `hstep` fragments consume — the existence half of a
reserved-well closure, reduced to a single area inequality on the board. -/
theorem exists_ne_well_landing_of_isReservedWellSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    {M : ℕ} (hb : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b) (h2 : 2 ≤ cfg.cols)
    (hcard : b.card ≤ (cfg.cols - 1) * M) :
    ∃ w, w < cfg.cols ∧ colHeight b w = 0 ∧
      ∃ j ∈ Finset.range cfg.cols, j ≠ w ∧ colHeight b j ≤ M := by
  obtain ⟨h, rfl, hbound, w, hw, hw0⟩ := hb
  refine ⟨w, hw, ?_, ?_⟩
  · rw [colHeight_skyline hw]; exact hw0
  · exact exists_ne_well_colHeight_le_of_card_le hw h2 hcard

/-- **The notched surface is a reserved-well skyline.** The exact profile the
all-seven dispatch (`isNotchedFlatTopRWSkyline_all7_hstep`) feeds on —
`0` at the well `w`, a single bump `base + 1` at column `m`, flat `base`
everywhere else — is a member of the `IsReservedWellSkyline` carrier whenever the
well is real and the bump fits (`base + 1 ≤ cfg.rows`). The well column reads `0`
unconditionally (the `j = w` guard fires first, even at `w = m`), so this exhibits
the *input* of the all-seven step as a genuine carrier state, the entry side of
any closure that routes a bag through a notched surface. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_notched {cfg : GameConfig} {base w m : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hbase : base + 1 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (skyline cfg (fun j => if j = w then 0 else if j = m then base + 1 else base)) := by
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else if j = m then base + 1 else base, rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j _
    show (if j = w then 0 else if j = m then base + 1 else base) ≤ cfg.rows
    split
    · exact Nat.zero_le _
    · split
      · exact hbase
      · omega
  · show (if w = w then 0 else if w = m then base + 1 else base) = 0
    rw [if_pos rfl]

/-- **The well-clear step preserves the reserved-well carrier.** Dropping the
vertical I into an empty well whose neighbours are all `≥ 4` lands back inside
`IsReservedWellSkyline`: by `applyStep_vertI_well` the result is the skyline with
the well still at `0` and every other column lowered by `4`, so the well column
stays empty and the height cap is inherited (`h j - 4 ≤ h j ≤ cfg.rows`). This is
the height-reset companion to the seven per-piece flat/notch fragments — together
they give *both* moves a closure argument needs: the flat-shelf placements that
grow the stack and the once-per-bag well clear that pays the growth back, each
returning to the same reserved-well carrier. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_vertI_well
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwell : h w = 0)
    (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h j)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }) := by
  rw [applyStep_vertI_well hw hwell hothers]
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else h j - 4, rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    show (if j = w then 0 else h j - 4) ≤ cfg.rows
    split
    · exact Nat.zero_le _
    · exact le_trans (Nat.sub_le _ _) (hbound j hj)
  · show (if w = w then 0 else h w - 4) = 0
    rw [if_pos rfl]

/-- **The well-clear step as an `hstep` witness for `I`.** Packages
`isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_vertI_well` into the exact existential shape the
bag-indexed reduction's `hstep` consumes: the vertical-I placement `{I, 1, w}` is
valid (`valid_vertI`) and lands in a reserved-well skyline. So when the adversary
draws `I` on a reserved-well surface whose non-well columns are all `≥ 4`, the
well clear *is* a discharging witness — the I-piece branch of any per-piece
closure, mirroring `isReservedWellSkyline_O_hstep` and its flat-shelf siblings. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_vertI_well_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwell : h w = 0)
    (hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h j)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.I ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) :=
  ⟨{ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }, rfl, valid_vertI hw,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_vertI_well hw hwell hothers hbound⟩

/-- **Well-avoiding adjacent pair always exists (when `cfg.cols ≥ 4`).** Every
flat-shelf reserved-well fragment (`isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_O` and its
T/L/J/horizontal-I siblings) needs a real adjacent column pair `(c, c+1)` that
dodges the reserved well `w` — `w ≠ c ∧ w ≠ c + 1`. Carrier membership alone does
not name such a pair; this lemma *supplies* it, discharging the "selection" half
of the per-piece `hstep`. The board has at least four columns, so picking `c = 2`
when the well sits in the low pair `{0,1}` and `c = 0` otherwise always lands a
disjoint in-range pair. (Four columns are necessary: with `cfg.cols = 3` and
`w = 1` every adjacent pair touches the well.) -/
theorem exists_nonwell_adjacent_pair {cfg : GameConfig} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (h4 : 4 ≤ cfg.cols) :
    ∃ c, c + 1 < cfg.cols ∧ w ≠ c ∧ w ≠ c + 1 := by
  rcases lt_or_ge w 2 with hlt | hge
  · exact ⟨2, by omega, by omega, by omega⟩
  · exact ⟨0, by omega, by omega, by omega⟩

/-- **Well-avoiding adjacent quad always exists (when `cfg.cols ≥ 8`).** The
flat-shelf dispatch `isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep` consumes a *four*-wide
flat window `(d, d+1, d+2, d+3)` clear of the reserved well `w` — subsuming the
O (2-wide), T/L/J (3-wide), and horizontal-I (4-wide) footprints in one shelf.
Carrier membership alone names no such window; this lemma supplies it. With at
least eight columns a single well position can never block both the low quad
`[0,3]` and the shifted quad `[4,7]`: pick `d = 4` when `w` sits in `{0,1,2,3}`
and `d = 0` otherwise. (Eight columns are necessary: with `cfg.cols = 7` and
`w = 3` every 4-wide window touches the well.) The wider companion to
`exists_nonwell_adjacent_pair`, completing the window-selection bricks the
flat-shelf `hstep` needs to fire on a reserved-well surface. -/
theorem exists_nonwell_adjacent_quad {cfg : GameConfig} {w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (h8 : 8 ≤ cfg.cols) :
    ∃ d, d + 3 < cfg.cols ∧ w ≠ d ∧ w ≠ d + 1 ∧ w ≠ d + 2 ∧ w ≠ d + 3 := by
  rcases lt_or_ge w 4 with hlt | hge
  · exact ⟨4, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega⟩
  · exact ⟨0, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega⟩

/-! ### Per-piece `hstep` fragments against the reserved-well carrier

The reduction `tetrisSolvableValid_of_reservedWell_closed` leaves one obligation:
closure of `IsReservedWellSkyline` under the adversarial bag. That obligation is a
conjunction over the seven pieces; the lemmas below discharge it one piece at a
time. Each consumes the matching `applyStep_*_skyline_noclear` move witness and
re-establishes the three carrier fields (`= skyline`, height-bounded, well empty).
The flat-shelf pieces (O here; T, L, J, horizontal-I identical) only need the
landing columns to clear the height cap; S/Z instead need a staircase notch and
remain the genuinely open case. -/

/-- **O-piece preserves the reserved-well skyline (first `hstep` fragment).** From
a reserved-well skyline `skyline cfg h` with a flat pair `(c, c+1)` — both real and
distinct from the well `w` — and headroom `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`, the no-clear O
placement `{O, 0, c}` lands flush and the result is again a reserved-well skyline:
the well column is untouched (so it stays empty), and the only raised columns
(`c`, `c+1`) remain within the height cap. This is the template every flat-shelf
piece follows. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_O
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_O_skyline_noclear hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2)) (c + 1) (h c + 2),
    rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 1) with rfl | hjc1'
    · rw [Function.update_self]; exact hslack
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1']
      rcases eq_or_ne j c with rfl | hjc'
      · rw [Function.update_self]; exact hslack
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc']; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]; exact hw0

/-- **Assembled `hstep` fragment for O on a generic reserved well.** Unlike the
staircase fragments (which need the notch), the flat-shelf pieces close off the
*bare* reserved-well carrier directly: given any flat pair `(c, c+1)` away from
the well with headroom, the `{O, 0, c}` placement is valid — `O` (rot 0) spans
columns `0..1`, so `c + 1 < cfg.cols` certifies it — and `isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_O`
gives membership. This is the most reusable form: the precondition is a generic
reserved-well skyline plus a local flat shelf, no global notch structure. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_O_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (heq : h c = h (c + 1))
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.O ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.O, rot := 0, col := c }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_O hc hc1 heq hw hwc hwc1 hw0 hbound hslack⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have hle : ∀ ce ∈ Piece.O.shapeUp 0, ce.1 ≤ 1 := by decide
  have hcb := hle cell hcell
  show c + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **T-piece preserves the reserved-well skyline.** Same template as the O
fragment on a flat three-column shelf `(c, c+1, c+2)` away from the well `w`: the
no-clear `{T, 2, c}` placement raises the profile to `(h c+1, h c+2, h c+1)`,
peaking at the centre, so headroom `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows` keeps every column capped
while the well stays empty. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_T
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.T, rot := 2, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_T_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 1))
    (c + 1) (h c + 2)) (c + 2) (h c + 1), rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 2) with rfl | hjc2'
    · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2']
      rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 1) with rfl | hjc1'
      · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1']
        rcases eq_or_ne j c with rfl | hjc'
        · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc']; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]
    exact hw0

/-- **Assembled `hstep` fragment for T on a generic reserved well.** Flat-shelf
companion to the O fragment: a flat triple `(c, c+1, c+2)` away from the well
hosts the `{T, 2, c}` placement. `T` (rot 2) spans columns `0..2`, so the
landing lemma's `c + 2 < cfg.cols` certifies validity tightly, and
`isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_T` supplies membership. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_T_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.T ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.T, rot := 2, col := c }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_T hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have hle : ∀ ce ∈ Piece.T.shapeUp 2, ce.1 ≤ 2 := by decide
  have hcb := hle cell hcell
  show c + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **L-piece preserves the reserved-well skyline.** Flat three-column shelf
`(c, c+1, c+2)` away from the well `w`: the no-clear `{L, 0, c}` placement raises
the profile to `(h c+1, h c+1, h c+2)`, peaking at the right column, so headroom
`h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows` keeps every column capped while the well stays empty. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_L
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_L_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 1))
    (c + 1) (h c + 1)) (c + 2) (h c + 2), rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 2) with rfl | hjc2'
    · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2']
      rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 1) with rfl | hjc1'
      · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1']
        rcases eq_or_ne j c with rfl | hjc'
        · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc']; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]
    exact hw0

/-- **L-piece hstep fragment.** Bundles the parametric L landing with placement
validity: on a flat three-column shelf `(c, c+1, c+2)` away from the well, the
no-clear `{L, 0, c}` placement is valid (3-wide, needs `c + 2 < cfg.cols`) and
preserves the reserved-well skyline. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_L_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.L ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_L hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have hle : ∀ ce ∈ Piece.L.shapeUp 0, ce.1 ≤ 2 := by decide
  have hcb := hle cell hcell
  show c + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **J-piece preserves the reserved-well skyline.** Mirror of the L fragment on a
flat three-column shelf `(c, c+1, c+2)` away from the well `w`: the no-clear
`{J, 0, c}` placement raises the profile to `(h c+2, h c+1, h c+1)`, peaking at the
left column, so headroom `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows` keeps every column capped while the
well stays empty. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_J
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_J_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 2))
    (c + 1) (h c + 1)) (c + 2) (h c + 1), rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 2) with rfl | hjc2'
    · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2']
      rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 1) with rfl | hjc1'
      · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1']
        rcases eq_or_ne j c with rfl | hjc'
        · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc']; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]
    exact hw0

/-- **J-piece hstep fragment.** Bundles the parametric J landing with placement
validity: on a flat three-column shelf `(c, c+1, c+2)` away from the well, the
no-clear `{J, 0, c}` placement is valid (3-wide, needs `c + 2 < cfg.cols`) and
preserves the reserved-well skyline. With this the full single-piece set
(O/T/L/J/horizI flat-shelf, S/Z notch, vertI re-entry) is bundled. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_J_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.J ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_J hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have hle : ∀ ce ∈ Piece.J.shapeUp 0, ce.1 ≤ 2 := by decide
  have hcb := hle cell hcell
  show c + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **Horizontal-I preserves the reserved-well skyline.** On a flat four-column
shelf `(c, c+1, c+2, c+3)` away from the well `w`, the no-clear `{I, 0, c}`
placement raises all four landing columns by exactly one to `h c + 1`, so the
lightest headroom `h c + 1 ≤ cfg.rows` keeps every column capped while the well
stays empty. The last of the five flat-shelf fragments; only S/Z (notch) remain. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_horizI
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c) (heq3 : h (c + 3) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 1 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_horizI_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3
    hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update (Function.update (Function.update
    (Function.update h c (h c + 1)) (c + 1) (h c + 1)) (c + 2) (h c + 1))
    (c + 3) (h c + 1), rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 3) with rfl | hjc3'
    · rw [Function.update_self]; exact hslack
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc3']
      rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 2) with rfl | hjc2'
      · rw [Function.update_self]; exact hslack
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2']
        rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 1) with rfl | hjc1'
        · rw [Function.update_self]; exact hslack
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1']
          rcases eq_or_ne j c with rfl | hjc'
          · rw [Function.update_self]; exact hslack
          · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc']; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc3, Function.update_of_ne hwc2,
        Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]
    exact hw0

/-- **Horizontal-I hstep fragment.** Bundles the parametric horizontal-I landing
with placement validity: on a flat four-column shelf `(c, c+1, c+2, c+3)` away
from the well, the no-clear `{I, 0, c}` placement is valid (4-wide, needs
`c + 3 < cfg.cols`) and preserves the reserved-well skyline. Completes the
flat-shelf set (O/T/L/J/horizI). -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_horizI_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c) (heq3 : h (c + 3) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 1 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.I ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.I, rot := 0, col := c }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_horizI hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0 hbound hslack⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have hle : ∀ ce ∈ Piece.I.shapeUp 0, ce.1 ≤ 3 := by decide
  have hcb := hle cell hcell
  show c + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **Flat-shelf dispatch.** A single uniform `hstep` over the five flat-shelf
pieces. Given a flat four-column shelf `(c, c+1, c+2, c+3)` away from the well
`w`, with headroom `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`, *any* adversary draw from
`{O, T, L, J, I}` admits a valid no-clear placement that lands back in a
reserved-well skyline. The four-wide flat shelf subsumes every flat-shelf
piece's footprint (O 2-wide, T/L/J 3-wide, I 4-wide), and `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`
subsumes the lighter `h c + 1` slack horizontal-I needs. This collapses the
five per-piece bundles into one dispatch the bag-phase carrier can call without
re-casing on the piece. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ} {p : Piece}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (heq1 : h (c + 1) = h c) (heq2 : h (c + 2) = h c) (heq3 : h (c + 3) = h c)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hp : p = Piece.O ∨ p = Piece.T ∨ p = Piece.L ∨ p = Piece.J ∨ p = Piece.I) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = p ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  rcases hp with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_O_hstep hc hc1 heq1.symm hw hwc hwc1 hw0 hbound hslack
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_T_hstep hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_L_hstep hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_J_hstep hc hc1 hc2 heq1 heq2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_horizI_hstep hc hc1 hc2 hc3 heq1 heq2 heq3
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3 hw0 hbound (by omega)

/-- **S-piece preserves the reserved-well skyline — conditional on a notch.**
Unlike the five flat-shelf pieces, S needs a *staircase notch* `(h c = h(c+1)`,
`h(c+2) = h c + 1)` to land without a hole; given that notch (away from the well
`w`) and headroom `h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`, the no-clear `{S, 0, c}` placement raises
the profile to `(h c+1, h c+2, h c+2)` and the result is again a reserved-well
skyline. The notch is taken as an explicit hypothesis: the bare carrier does *not*
assert one exists, so a full `hstep` cannot supply it from a flat surface — this
lemma documents precisely what S consumes (a notch) and produces. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_S
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h c = h (c + 1)) (hnotch : h (c + 2) = h c + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_S_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hnotch hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h c + 1))
    (c + 1) (h c + 2)) (c + 2) (h c + 2), rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 2) with rfl | hjc2'
    · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2']
      rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 1) with rfl | hjc1'
      · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1']
        rcases eq_or_ne j c with rfl | hjc'
        · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc']; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]
    exact hw0

/-- **Assembled `hstep` fragment: S on the generic reserved-well carrier.** Packages
`isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_S` into the `∃ pl, piece ∧ Valid ∧ carrier` shape a
closure obligation consumes, the S-notch analogue of `isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep`.
Validity discharges tightly: `S` (rot 0) spans columns `0..2`, so the very `c + 2 < cfg.cols`
the notch landing already needs certifies every shape cell in-bounds. Brings the generic
reserved-well carrier's hstep family to S (joining the five flat-shelf pieces and the
well-clearing vertical I); only the mirror Z notch remains for a uniform all-seven dispatch
on the bare carrier. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_S_hstep {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h c = h (c + 1)) (hnotch : h (c + 2) = h c + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h c + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.S ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_S hc hc1 hc2 heq hnotch hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have hle : ∀ ce ∈ Piece.S.shapeUp 0, ce.1 ≤ 2 := by decide
  have hcb := hle cell hcell
  show c + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **Z-piece preserves the reserved-well skyline — conditional on the mirror
notch.** The mirror of the S fragment: Z needs the notch `(h(c+1) = h(c+2)`,
`h c = h(c+1) + 1)`; given it (away from the well `w`) and headroom
`h(c+1) + 2 ≤ cfg.rows`, the no-clear `{Z, 0, c}` placement raises the profile to
`(h(c+1)+2, h(c+1)+2, h(c+1)+1)` and the result is again a reserved-well skyline.
Like S, the notch is an explicit hypothesis the bare carrier does not supply; this
completes the seven per-piece fragments (five flat-shelf + two notch-conditional). -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_Z
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h (c + 1) = h (c + 2)) (hnotch : h c = h (c + 1) + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h (c + 1) + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  rw [applyStep_Z_skyline_noclear hc hc1 hc2 heq hnotch hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0]
  refine ⟨Function.update (Function.update (Function.update h c (h (c + 1) + 2))
    (c + 1) (h (c + 1) + 2)) (c + 2) (h (c + 1) + 1), rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 2) with rfl | hjc2'
    · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc2']
      rcases eq_or_ne j (c + 1) with rfl | hjc1'
      · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
      · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc1']
        rcases eq_or_ne j c with rfl | hjc'
        · rw [Function.update_self]; omega
        · rw [Function.update_of_ne hjc']; exact hbound j hj
  · rw [Function.update_of_ne hwc2, Function.update_of_ne hwc1, Function.update_of_ne hwc]
    exact hw0

/-- **Assembled `hstep` fragment: Z on the generic reserved-well carrier.** The mirror
of `isReservedWellSkyline_S_hstep`: packages `isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_Z` into the
`∃ pl, piece ∧ Valid ∧ carrier` shape, conditioned on the down-step Z-notch
`(h c = h(c+1)+1`, `h(c+1) = h(c+2))`. Validity discharges tightly: `Z` (rot 0) spans
columns `0..2`, so the notch's `c + 2 < cfg.cols` certifies every shape cell in-bounds.
With this the generic reserved-well carrier has a uniform `∃ pl`-shaped `hstep` fragment
for **all seven** pieces — five flat-shelf (`isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep`), the
two staircase notches (S/Z), and the well-clearing vertical I — each consuming its required
local surface shape. What the *bare* carrier still does not supply is the existence of those
shapes on an arbitrary member; that surface-shape guarantee is the remaining crux (#66/#72). -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_Z_hstep {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (heq : h (c + 1) = h (c + 2)) (hnotch : h c = h (c + 1) + 1)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows) (hslack : h (c + 1) + 2 ≤ cfg.rows) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.Z ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }, rfl, ?_,
    isReservedWellSkyline_applyStep_Z hc hc1 hc2 heq hnotch hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hbound hslack⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have hle : ∀ ce ∈ Piece.Z.shapeUp 0, ce.1 ≤ 2 := by decide
  have hcb := hle cell hcell
  show c + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **All-seven dispatch on the bare reserved-well carrier (explicit shapes).** The
generic-surface generalisation of `isNotchedFlatTopRWSkyline_all7_hstep`: on *any*
reserved-well skyline `h`, if the surface exhibits a flat four-column shelf `[d, d+3]`
(for the five flat-shelf pieces `O/T/L/J/I`), an up-step S-notch `[s, s+2]`
(`h s = h(s+1)`, `h(s+2) = h s + 1`), and a down-step Z-notch `[z, z+2]`
(`h(z+1) = h(z+2)`, `h z = h(z+1)+1`) — all three windows clear of the well `w` and
under the height ceiling — then *every* adversary draw from the full seven-piece set is
absorbed into a valid no-clear placement that lands back in a reserved-well skyline. This
is the precise factoring of the closure crux: it isolates the **solved** placement problem
(each piece has a brick consuming its local shape) from the **open** shape-existence problem
(does an arbitrary carrier member actually present all three windows?). A wider carrier
(#72) closes by additionally proving these three shapes always coexist on its surfaces. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep
    {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ} {w d s z : ℕ} {p : Piece}
    (hd3 : d + 3 < cfg.cols)
    (hdeq1 : h (d + 1) = h d) (hdeq2 : h (d + 2) = h d) (hdeq3 : h (d + 3) = h d)
    (hwd : w ≠ d) (hwd1 : w ≠ d + 1) (hwd2 : w ≠ d + 2) (hwd3 : w ≠ d + 3)
    (hdslack : h d + 2 ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hs2 : s + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hseq : h s = h (s + 1)) (hsnotch : h (s + 2) = h s + 1)
    (hws : w ≠ s) (hws1 : w ≠ s + 1) (hws2 : w ≠ s + 2)
    (hsslack : h s + 2 ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hz2 : z + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hzeq : h (z + 1) = h (z + 2)) (hznotch : h z = h (z + 1) + 1)
    (hwz : w ≠ z) (hwz1 : w ≠ z + 1) (hwz2 : w ≠ z + 2)
    (hzslack : h (z + 1) + 2 ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hbound : ∀ j < cfg.cols, h j ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hp : p = Piece.O ∨ p = Piece.T ∨ p = Piece.L ∨ p = Piece.J ∨ p = Piece.I
          ∨ p = Piece.S ∨ p = Piece.Z) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = p ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline cfg (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  have hd : d < cfg.cols := by omega
  have hd1 : d + 1 < cfg.cols := by omega
  have hd2 : d + 2 < cfg.cols := by omega
  have hs : s < cfg.cols := by omega
  have hs1 : s + 1 < cfg.cols := by omega
  have hz : z < cfg.cols := by omega
  have hz1 : z + 1 < cfg.cols := by omega
  rcases hp with rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl | rfl
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep hd hd1 hd2 hd3 hdeq1 hdeq2 hdeq3
      hw hwd hwd1 hwd2 hwd3 hw0 hbound hdslack (Or.inl rfl)
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep hd hd1 hd2 hd3 hdeq1 hdeq2 hdeq3
      hw hwd hwd1 hwd2 hwd3 hw0 hbound hdslack (Or.inr (Or.inl rfl))
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep hd hd1 hd2 hd3 hdeq1 hdeq2 hdeq3
      hw hwd hwd1 hwd2 hwd3 hw0 hbound hdslack (Or.inr (Or.inr (Or.inl rfl)))
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep hd hd1 hd2 hd3 hdeq1 hdeq2 hdeq3
      hw hwd hwd1 hwd2 hwd3 hw0 hbound hdslack (Or.inr (Or.inr (Or.inr (Or.inl rfl))))
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_flatShelf_hstep hd hd1 hd2 hd3 hdeq1 hdeq2 hdeq3
      hw hwd hwd1 hwd2 hwd3 hw0 hbound hdslack (Or.inr (Or.inr (Or.inr (Or.inr rfl))))
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_S_hstep hs hs1 hs2 hseq hsnotch hw hws hws1 hws2 hw0 hbound hsslack
  · exact isReservedWellSkyline_Z_hstep hz hz1 hz2 hzeq hznotch hw hwz hwz1 hwz2 hw0 hbound hzslack

/-- **A concrete standard surface carrying every window `isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep`
needs.** The bare reserved-well all-7 dispatch (`isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep`) consumes,
on a *single* height profile, a flat 4-window, an `S`-notch, a `Z`-notch, and an empty well —
11 columns' worth of structure if laid out disjointly, one more than the standard board owns.
The dispatch's hypotheses, however, never force the three shelves apart: each branch reads only
its own window plus the well. So the windows may *overlap*, and the profile
`fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base`
packs all four into ten columns: well at `0`; flat shelf at `base` across `[1,4]` (flat 4-window
`d = 1`); the rise at `5` over the flat `6,7` is a `Z`-notch (`z = 5`); the flat `6,7` under the
rise at `8` is an `S`-notch (`s = 6`). This lemma certifies that surface is a reserved-well
member (`base + 1 ≤ rows`), the standard-board membership companion an
`isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep_standard` reduction steps *from* — the bare-carrier analogue of
`isNotchedFlatTopRWSkyline_standard_surface`. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_richStandard_surface {base : ℕ}
    (hbase : base + 1 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline GameConfig.standard
      (skyline GameConfig.standard
        (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base)) :=
  ⟨fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base,
    rfl,
    fun j _ => by dsimp only; split_ifs <;> omega,
    0, by decide, by simp⟩

/-- **All-seven dispatch off the rich standard surface.** Pinning
`isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep`'s four windows to the overlapping layout that
`isReservedWellSkyline_richStandard_surface` certifies — well `0`, flat-4 `d = 1`,
`S`-notch `s = 6`, `Z`-notch `z = 5` — every position obligation collapses to a closed
numeral fact (`by decide`) and every height equation to an `if`-reduction (`by simp`); the
three slack obligations all reduce to the single headroom `base + 2 ≤ rows`. The result: on
the concrete 10-column reserved-well surface
`fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base`,
**every** one of the seven pieces admits a valid placement landing back in a reserved-well
skyline. This is the bare-carrier analogue of `isNotchedFlatTopRWSkyline_all7_hstep_standard`,
specialising the all-seven step to the very config `TetrisSolvableValid` is about. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep_standard {base : ℕ} {p : Piece}
    (hbase : base + 2 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows)
    (hp : p = Piece.O ∨ p = Piece.T ∨ p = Piece.L ∨ p = Piece.J ∨ p = Piece.I
          ∨ p = Piece.S ∨ p = Piece.Z) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = p ∧ pl.Valid GameConfig.standard ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline GameConfig.standard
        (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          pl) :=
  isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep (cfg := GameConfig.standard)
    (w := 0) (d := 1) (s := 6) (z := 5)
    (hd3 := by decide)
    (hdeq1 := by simp) (hdeq2 := by simp) (hdeq3 := by simp)
    (hwd := by decide) (hwd1 := by decide) (hwd2 := by decide) (hwd3 := by decide)
    (hdslack := by simpa using hbase)
    (hs2 := by decide)
    (hseq := by simp) (hsnotch := by simp)
    (hws := by decide) (hws1 := by decide) (hws2 := by decide)
    (hsslack := by simpa using hbase)
    (hz2 := by decide)
    (hzeq := by simp) (hznotch := by simp)
    (hwz := by decide) (hwz1 := by decide) (hwz2 := by decide)
    (hzslack := by simpa using hbase)
    (hw := by decide) (hw0 := by simp)
    (hbound := by intro j _; split_ifs <;> omega)
    (hp := hp)

/-- **One-step immortality of the rich standard surface against any adversary draw.** The
rich-carrier analogue of `isNotchedFlatTopRWSkyline_all7_not_isLost_standard` (the notched
surface) and the flat-top survival certificate: from the concrete *rich* standard surface —
well at column `0`, two bumps at columns `5` and `8`, flat `base` elsewhere, with two cells of
headroom (`base + 2 ≤ 20`) — *whatever* piece `p` the adversary draws, the player has a **valid**
placement whose post-move board is **not lost**. It packages the rich all-7 landing
(`isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep_standard`, which lands in a reserved-well skyline) into the
actual safety predicate via the reserved-well non-loss certificate
(`not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline`). This completes the trio of move-level immortality
certificates (flat, notched, rich), and pins the survival guarantee to the very surface the
height-regulator (`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear`) walks down its four-rows-per-step
descent ladder — so the carrier the closure actually iterates carries its own per-state
non-loss witness, one the bag-phase closure accumulates into infinite play. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_all7_not_isLost_standard {base : ℕ} {p : Piece}
    (hbase : base + 2 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows)
    (hp : p = Piece.O ∨ p = Piece.T ∨ p = Piece.L ∨ p = Piece.J ∨ p = Piece.I
          ∨ p = Piece.S ∨ p = Piece.Z) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = p ∧ pl.Valid GameConfig.standard ∧
      ¬ Board.isLost GameConfig.standard
        (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          pl) := by
  obtain ⟨pl, hpiece, hvalid, hrw⟩ := isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep_standard hbase hp
  exact ⟨pl, hpiece, hvalid, not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline hrw⟩

/-- **The well-clear re-enters the rich standard surface, four rows lower.** The first genuine
*self*-re-entry for the rich reserved-well surface: dropping a vertical `I` into the empty well
(column `0`) of
`fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base`
(with `base ≥ 4`, so every non-well column stands at least four high) completes and clears the
bottom four rows, dropping every non-well column by `4`. The result is *the very same rich
surface* at height `base - 4` — well at `0`, the two bumps at columns `5, 8` preserved one above
the lowered flat, the flat shelf and both notch windows intact. Where the seven all-7 dispatches
land in a *generic* reserved-well skyline (shape forgotten), this is the height-regulator move
that returns the carrier to *itself*, so iterating it walks the rich surface down a four-row
ladder. The `I` half of a rich-carrier closure; the open part is re-establishing the windows
after the *six* surface-roughening pieces. -/
theorem applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear {base : ℕ} (hbase4 : 4 ≤ base) :
    Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard
        (skyline GameConfig.standard
          (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 }
      = skyline GameConfig.standard
          (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base - 4 + 1
            else if j = 8 then base - 4 + 1 else base - 4) := by
  rw [applyStep_vertI_well (by decide) (by simp)
        (by intro j _ hj0;
            show (4 : ℕ) ≤ (if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1
              else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base);
            split_ifs <;> omega)]
  congr 1
  funext j
  dsimp only
  split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **After the well-clear, the rich surface still hosts every piece.** Chaining the
height-regulator (`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear`: the well-clear returns the rich surface
at `base - 4`) into the all-7 dispatch (`isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep_standard` at the
lowered height, whose headroom `(base - 4) + 2 ≤ rows` follows from `base + 2 ≤ rows`): from a
rich standard surface with `4 ≤ base` and `base + 2 ≤ rows`, the two-ply *(clear, then any
piece `p`)* ends in a reserved-well skyline. This certifies the well-clear leaves a *fully
functional* carrier state — every one of the seven pieces the adversary can next draw still
lands cleanly — so the height-regulator never strands the surface in a stuck configuration. The
inductive continuation of the descent ladder: each clear drops the surface four rows and hands
back a board that again absorbs the whole alphabet. -/
theorem applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_all7 {base : ℕ} {p : Piece}
    (hbase4 : 4 ≤ base) (hbase : base + 2 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows)
    (hp : p = Piece.O ∨ p = Piece.T ∨ p = Piece.L ∨ p = Piece.J ∨ p = Piece.I
          ∨ p = Piece.S ∨ p = Piece.Z) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = p ∧ pl.Valid GameConfig.standard ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline GameConfig.standard
        (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard
          (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard
            (skyline GameConfig.standard
              (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
            { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 })
          pl) := by
  rw [applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear hbase4]
  exact isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep_standard (base := base - 4) (by omega) hp

/-- **The descent ladder, iterated.** Folding `n` copies of the regulator placement
`{I, rot 1, col 0}` over the rich standard surface — i.e. dropping a vertical `I` into the
empty well `n` times in succession — walks the surface straight down a four-rows-per-step ladder
to `base - 4·n`, provided it starts tall enough (`4·n ≤ base`, so every intermediate clear still
finds its well four rows deep). Each fold step re-enters the *same* rich surface
(`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear`) four rows lower, so the whole descent stays inside the
carrier and never strands the board: `n` regulator moves debit exactly `36·n` cells. The closed
form of the height regulator's reach. -/
theorem applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate :
    ∀ (n base : ℕ), 4 * n ≤ base →
      List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          (List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 })
        = skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base - 4 * n + 1
              else if j = 8 then base - 4 * n + 1 else base - 4 * n) := by
  intro n
  induction n with
  | zero => intro base _; simp
  | succ k ih =>
    intro base hk
    rw [List.replicate_succ, List.foldl_cons,
        applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear (show (4 : ℕ) ≤ base by omega),
        ih (base - 4) (by omega)]
    congr 1
    funext j
    dsimp only
    split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Area certificate for the rich standard surface.** Summing the rich profile's
ten column heights through `card_skyline`: the well `j = 0` contributes `0`, the two
notch shelves `j = 5` and `j = 8` contribute `base + 1` each, and the remaining seven
real columns contribute `base` apiece — totalling `9 · base + 2` filled cells. This is
the conserved quantity the descent ladder tracks: the I-regulator's well-clear lowers
`base` by `4`, and `card_richStandard_surface` reads off exactly how the cell budget
shrinks (`9·base + 2 ↦ 9·(base−4) + 2`), pinning the surface to a single integer handle
for any potential/pigeonhole accounting downstream. -/
theorem card_richStandard_surface {base : ℕ} :
    (skyline GameConfig.standard
        (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base)).card
      = 9 * base + 2 := by
  rw [card_skyline]
  simp only [GameConfig.standard_cols, Finset.sum_range_succ, Finset.sum_range_zero]
  norm_num
  omega

/-- **The well-clear debits exactly 36 cells from the rich surface.** Composing the
height-regulator (`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear`: the vertical-I well-clear returns the
rich surface at `base - 4`) with the area certificate (`card_richStandard_surface` read at
`base - 4`): the cleared board carries `9 · (base − 4) + 2` cells. Since the pre-clear surface
holds `9 · base + 2` (`card_richStandard_surface`), the I-regulator removes a flat
`9·base + 2 − (9·(base−4) + 2) = 36` cells per application — the four full rows of the
`10`-wide field minus the `4` well cells the vertical I never had to fill. This pins the
descent's cell budget to an exact arithmetic ledger, the conserved quantity any
potential/pigeonhole bound on the surviving cycle must respect. -/
theorem card_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear {base : ℕ} (hbase4 : 4 ≤ base) :
    (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard
        (skyline GameConfig.standard
          (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 }).card
      = 9 * (base - 4) + 2 := by
  rw [applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear hbase4, card_richStandard_surface (base := base - 4)]

/-- **The iterated descent's cell budget.** After `n` successive vertical-`I` well-clears
(`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate`: the fold lands on the rich surface at
`base - 4·n`) the board carries exactly `9·(base − 4·n) + 2` cells — the area certificate
(`card_richStandard_surface`) read at the lowered height. Since each clear debits a fixed
`36` cells (`9·base + 2 ↦ 9·(base−4) + 2`), `n` of them debit `36·n`, pinning the descent's
running cell count to one closed integer. The area face of the iterated regulator, completing
the closed-form/survival/budget trio for the `n`-fold well-clear. -/
theorem card_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate {n base : ℕ} (h : 4 * n ≤ base) :
    (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
        (skyline GameConfig.standard
          (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
        (List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 })).card
      = 9 * (base - 4 * n) + 2 := by
  rw [applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate n base h, card_richStandard_surface]

/-- **The rich standard surface is never lost.** Its tallest columns are the two notch
shelves `j = 5, 8` at height `base + 1`; every other real column sits at `base` or `0`. So
once `base + 1 ≤ rows` the whole profile fits inside the visible field and
`not_isLost_skyline` discharges `¬ isLost` directly. This is the *survival face* of the rich
carrier — the descent ladder (`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear`) only ever lowers `base`,
so a single `base + 1 ≤ rows` certificate at the top of the ladder guarantees no state along
the descent is ever a topped-out board. -/
theorem not_isLost_richStandard_surface {base : ℕ}
    (hbase : base + 1 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost GameConfig.standard
        (skyline GameConfig.standard
          (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base)) := by
  apply not_isLost_skyline
  intro j _
  dsimp only
  split_ifs <;> omega

/-- **Every rung of the descent ladder is survivable.** The endpoint of `n` successive
vertical-`I` well-clears on the rich standard surface (`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate`:
the fold lands on the rich surface at `base - 4·n`) is never lost, as long as the *top* rung is
safe (`base + 1 ≤ rows`) and the descent has room to run (`4·n ≤ base`). Since each clear only
*lowers* the surface, one height certificate at the tallest configuration discharges loss-freedom
for the entire ladder — no descent state is ever topped out. The survival face of the iterated
regulator, complementing the closed form (`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate`) and the
area ledger (`card_richStandard_surface`). -/
theorem not_isLost_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate {n base : ℕ}
    (hn : 4 * n ≤ base) (hbase : base + 1 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost GameConfig.standard
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          (List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 })) := by
  rw [applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate n base hn]
  exact not_isLost_richStandard_surface (by omega)

/-- **Every step of the descent ladder is survivable, not merely its endpoint.** The
transient strengthening of `not_isLost_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate`: for
*every* prefix length `m`, the partial descent — the first `m` of `n` vertical-`I` well-clears
on the rich standard surface — is never lost. Survival is a transient property (the board must
be un-lost at *each* intermediate drop, not only after the last), so this is the form a play
schedule actually consumes, the descent-ladder companion to the bag-level
`not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline`. Proof: a prefix of a `replicate` is a shorter
`replicate` (`List.take_replicate` collapses `take m (replicate n x)` to `replicate (min m n) x`),
and the `min m n`-fold descent still has room to run (`4 · min m n ≤ 4 · n ≤ base`), so the
endpoint lemma discharges it at the truncated length. With one height certificate at the tallest
rung (`base + 1 ≤ rows`) the whole staircase, and every partial play along it, is certified
topple-free. -/
theorem not_isLost_take_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate {n base : ℕ} (m : ℕ)
    (hn : 4 * n ≤ base) (hbase : base + 1 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost GameConfig.standard
        (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          ((List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 }).take m)) := by
  rw [List.take_replicate]
  exact not_isLost_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate (by omega) hbase

/-- **Bag-burst transient survival of the rich surface.** Where
`not_isLost_take_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate` certifies survival under the
*specific* vertical-`I` descent schedule, this certifies survival under an **arbitrary** list of
placements `pls`: starting from the rich standard surface (max height `base + 1`), no prefix of
`pls` ever tops out, provided the whole burst fits the headroom budget `(base + 1) + 4·k ≤ rows`
(`k = pls.length`). Each placement lifts the surface by at most four, so the running peak stays
`≤ (base + 1) + 4·k`; the prefix lemma `not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline` discharges every
intermediate board at once. This is the height-bound half a per-bag carrier quotes for the
mid-bag phase, where the adversary's pieces land in shape-forgetting positions and only the loose
`+4` budget — not any concrete surface invariant — keeps the board alive until the next
well-clear regenerates slack. -/
theorem not_isLost_take_foldl_richStandard {base : ℕ} (pls : List Placement) (n : ℕ)
    (hcap : (base + 1) + 4 * pls.length ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows) :
    ¬ Board.isLost GameConfig.standard
        ((pls.take n).foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))) := by
  apply not_isLost_foldl_take_applyStep_skyline (B := base + 1)
  · intro j _
    dsimp only
    split_ifs <;> omega
  · exact hcap

/-- **The whole descent ladder still hosts every piece.** Generalising
`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_all7` (its `n = 1` case) to arbitrary depth: after `n`
successive vertical-`I` well-clears (`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate` lands the fold
on the rich surface at `base - 4·n`), *any* piece the adversary next draws still has a valid
placement keeping the board a reserved-well skyline — provided the ladder has room to run
(`4·n ≤ base`) and the lowered surface keeps its two-row headroom (`base + 2 ≤ rows`, which the
descent only relaxes). So no number of regulator clears ever strands the surface in a stuck
configuration; the descent hands back a fully functional carrier at every rung. -/
theorem applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate_all7 {n base : ℕ} {p : Piece}
    (hn : 4 * n ≤ base) (hbase : base + 2 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows)
    (hp : p = Piece.O ∨ p = Piece.T ∨ p = Piece.L ∨ p = Piece.J ∨ p = Piece.I
          ∨ p = Piece.S ∨ p = Piece.Z) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = p ∧ pl.Valid GameConfig.standard ∧
      IsReservedWellSkyline GameConfig.standard
        (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard
          (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
            (skyline GameConfig.standard
              (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
            (List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 }))
          pl) := by
  rw [applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate n base hn]
  exact isReservedWellSkyline_all7_hstep_standard (base := base - 4 * n) (by omega) hp

/-- **The descent regulator can target any height band.** Given any rich standard surface at
height `base` and any target floor `lo ≤ base`, there is a clear-count `n` (namely `(base - lo) / 4`)
whose `n`-fold vertical-`I` well-clear descent lands the board on a rich surface at some `base'`
inside the three-wide band `lo ≤ base' ≤ lo + 3`. Proof: instantiate the closed-form descent
`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate` at `n = (base - lo) / 4`, which lands at
`base' = base - 4·n`; Euclidean arithmetic gives `base - 4·((base - lo) / 4) = lo + (base - lo) % 4`,
and the remainder is `< 4`, so `base'` sits in `[lo, lo + 3]`. This is the regulator's *reachability*
face: where `applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate` says *where* a fixed number of clears
lands, this says the descent can hit *any* prescribed low band — the height-controller's guarantee
that an over-tall rich surface can always be regulated back down to a canonical clearing height
(e.g. the `base ∈ [4, 7]` window where one more bag both fits the headroom and keeps four
fillable rows), regardless of how high the surface had grown. The leftover packing crux is
unchanged; this only certifies the height knob has full downward range in steps of four. -/
theorem exists_richStandard_vertI_descent_to_band {base lo : ℕ} (hlo : lo ≤ base) :
    ∃ (n base' : ℕ),
      List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          (List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 })
        = skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base' + 1 else if j = 8 then base' + 1 else base')
      ∧ lo ≤ base' ∧ base' ≤ lo + 3 := by
  refine ⟨(base - lo) / 4, base - 4 * ((base - lo) / 4), ?_, by omega, by omega⟩
  exact applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate ((base - lo) / 4) base (by omega)

/-- **Safe descent to a target band: the regulator's complete between-bags guarantee.**
Fuses the regulator's two faces into one certificate. From any rich standard surface at height
`base` with the top-rung headroom cert `base + 1 ≤ rows`, and any target floor `lo ≤ base`, there
is a clear-count `n` (the same `(base - lo) / 4` as `exists_richStandard_vertI_descent_to_band`)
such that: (i) the `n`-fold vertical-`I` descent lands on a rich surface at `base'` inside the band
`lo ≤ base' ≤ lo + 3` (the reachability face), AND (ii) *every* prefix of that descent is un-lost
(the transient-survival face, from `not_isLost_take_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate`).
So the height controller may regulate an over-tall rich surface down into any canonical clearing
window without ever topping out along the way — the single fact a between-bags phase quotes. Proof:
explicit witness `n := (base - lo) / 4`; conjunct (i) is the closed-form descent
`applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate`, the band bounds are `omega` (Euclid on `/4`), and
conjunct (ii) is the transient lemma at the same `n`, its `4·n ≤ base` obligation again `omega`. -/
theorem exists_richStandard_vertI_descent_to_band_not_isLost {base lo : ℕ}
    (hlo : lo ≤ base) (hbase : base + 1 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows) :
    ∃ (n base' : ℕ),
      List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          (List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 })
        = skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base' + 1 else if j = 8 then base' + 1 else base')
      ∧ lo ≤ base' ∧ base' ≤ lo + 3
      ∧ (∀ m, ¬ Board.isLost GameConfig.standard
            (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
              (skyline GameConfig.standard
                (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
              ((List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 }).take m))) := by
  refine ⟨(base - lo) / 4, base - 4 * ((base - lo) / 4), ?_, by omega, by omega, ?_⟩
  · exact applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate ((base - lo) / 4) base (by omega)
  · intro m
    exact not_isLost_take_applyStep_richStandard_vertI_clear_replicate m (by omega) hbase

/-- **Regulating into the canonical clearing window `[4, 7]`.** The named specialization of
`exists_richStandard_vertI_descent_to_band_not_isLost` at target floor `lo = 4`: from any rich
standard surface at `base ≥ 4` with headroom `base + 1 ≤ rows`, the vertical-`I` descent safely
(no prefix tops out) lands on a rich surface at `base' ∈ [4, 7]`. This pins the constant the height
controller actually targets. The lower bound `base' ≥ 4` is the *clear-ready* condition: columns
`1..9` stand at least four high, so the bottom four rows are full except the well and one more
vertical-`I` drop fires a four-line clear. The upper bound `base' ≤ 7` is the *packing-headroom*
condition: the surface peak `base' + 1 ≤ 8`, leaving `rows − 8 ≥ 12` empty rows for the next bag's
packing burst before any topple risk. So `[4, 7]` is exactly the band where a rich surface is both
ready to clear and roomy enough to rebuild — the canonical between-bags rendezvous height. Proof:
`obtain` the band witness, then the upper bound `base' ≤ 4 + 3` is `omega`. -/
theorem exists_richStandard_vertI_descent_to_clearing_window {base : ℕ}
    (hbase4 : 4 ≤ base) (hbase : base + 1 ≤ GameConfig.standard.rows) :
    ∃ (n base' : ℕ),
      List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
          (skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
          (List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 })
        = skyline GameConfig.standard
            (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base' + 1 else if j = 8 then base' + 1 else base')
      ∧ 4 ≤ base' ∧ base' ≤ 7
      ∧ (∀ m, ¬ Board.isLost GameConfig.standard
            (List.foldl (Placement.applyStep GameConfig.standard)
              (skyline GameConfig.standard
                (fun j => if j = 0 then 0 else if j = 5 then base + 1 else if j = 8 then base + 1 else base))
              ((List.replicate n { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := 0 }).take m))) := by
  obtain ⟨n, base', heq, hlo, hhi, hsafe⟩ :=
    exists_richStandard_vertI_descent_to_band_not_isLost (lo := 4) hbase4 hbase
  exact ⟨n, base', heq, hlo, by omega, hsafe⟩

/-! ### Flat-top reserved well: a carrier with guaranteed landing sites

The per-piece fragments above each need their landing region to satisfy a surface
precondition (a flat shelf, or a notch). The bare `IsReservedWellSkyline` carrier
guarantees neither. The *flat-top reserved well* pins the surface concretely: every
real column except one well `w` sits at a single common height `base`. On such a
board **any** adjacent pair of non-well columns is automatically a flat shelf, so
the flat-shelf preconditions (O, T, L, J, horizontal-I) become pure index
arithmetic; only the S/Z notch — which a uniform flat top lacks — stays open. -/

/-- A *flat-top reserved-well* board: all real columns except a single well `w`
sit at a common height `base ≤ cfg.rows`, and the well is empty. -/
def IsFlatTopRWSkyline (cfg : GameConfig) (b : Board) : Prop :=
  ∃ base w : ℕ, w < cfg.cols ∧ base ≤ cfg.rows ∧
    b = skyline cfg (fun j => if j = w then 0 else base)

/-- A flat-top reserved-well board is a reserved-well skyline: the constant-`base`
profile (with a `0` at the well) is height-bounded and keeps the well empty, so the
flat-top carrier inherits the `hinit`/`hheight` discharges unchanged. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_of_isFlatTopRWSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsFlatTopRWSkyline cfg b) : IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b := by
  obtain ⟨base, w, hw, hbase, rfl⟩ := hb
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else base, rfl, ?_, w, hw, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj
    show (if j = w then 0 else base) ≤ cfg.rows
    split
    · exact Nat.zero_le _
    · exact hbase
  · show (if w = w then 0 else base) = 0
    rw [if_pos rfl]

/-- A *spread-bounded reserved-well* board: a reserved-well skyline whose every
real (non-well) column sits in a bounded band `[floor, floor + spread]` above a
common `floor`, with the well `w` empty and the band capped by `cfg.rows`. The
flat-top carrier is exactly the `spread = 0` slice (`floor = base`); a single
flat-shelf placement lifts a 2–4 column window by `1`–`2`, so it lands here with
`spread ≤ 2`. Unlike `IsFlatTopRWSkyline`, this carrier therefore **admits the
roughened surfaces a placement produces** — the documented obstruction to closing
the flat-top carrier under a single move (see
`tetrisSolvableValid_of_flatTop_closed`). The band cap `floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows`
keeps every column under the ceiling, so the carrier still discharges `hheight`. -/
def IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline (cfg : GameConfig) (spread : ℕ) (b : Board) : Prop :=
  ∃ (h : ℕ → ℕ) (floor w : ℕ), b = skyline cfg h ∧ w < cfg.cols ∧ h w = 0 ∧
    (∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread) ∧
    floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows

/-- **The raw-band smart constructor.** Builds an `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline`
witness directly from a height function `h` and its band data, with no structured
refinement (`IsLevelPocketBand`, `IsFlatTopRWSkyline`) in between. Every other
intro into this carrier ultimately unfolds to this `⟨h, floor, w, rfl, …⟩` shape;
exposing it as a named lemma lets the descent dispatchers and any future band
scheduler conclude membership for a freshly primed surface `skyline cfg h` from
its raw band data, instead of routing through a pocket/flat predicate that may not
hold for the surface in hand. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {h : ℕ → ℕ} {floor w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread)
    (hslack : floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread (skyline cfg h) :=
  ⟨h, floor, w, rfl, hw, hw0, hband, hslack⟩

/-- **A pocket band: a spread band with a guaranteed level width-four pocket.**
Refines `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline` by additionally exhibiting four consecutive
non-well columns `c, c+1, c+2, c+3`, all resting at the band floor. This is exactly
the hypothesis bundle the five flat-piece valley lemmas consume
(`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_flat5_valley_of_levelPocket`): the guaranteed level
shelf is what lets every flat piece find a flush landing, ruling out the
alternating-band trap states (heights `floor, floor+1, floor, …`) that make the
bare spread predicate's closure false. It is the candidate refined invariant for
the reachability route (`tetrisSolvableValid_of_strategy_invariant`). -/
def IsLevelPocketBand (cfg : GameConfig) (spread : ℕ) (b : Board) : Prop :=
  ∃ (h : ℕ → ℕ) (floor w c : ℕ),
    b = skyline cfg h ∧ w < cfg.cols ∧ h w = 0 ∧
    (∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread) ∧
    floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows ∧
    c + 3 < cfg.cols ∧ w ≠ c ∧ w ≠ c + 1 ∧ w ≠ c + 2 ∧ w ≠ c + 3 ∧
    h c = floor ∧ h (c + 1) = floor ∧ h (c + 2) = floor ∧ h (c + 3) = floor

/-- **Forgetting the pocket recovers the bare spread band.** The inclusion that
lets `IsLevelPocketBand` inherit every spread-band discharge without re-proving
it — in particular the height cap `colHeight_le_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline`, so
the `hI_height` obligation of the reachability route is immediate for this refined
invariant. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_of_isLevelPocketBand {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {b : Board} (hb : IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread b) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread b := by
  obtain ⟨h, floor, w, c, hbskyline, hw, hw0, hband, hcap, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _⟩ := hb
  exact ⟨h, floor, w, hbskyline, hw, hw0, hband, hcap⟩

/-- **A flat top is a spread-bounded reserved well with zero spread.** The
canonical embedding of the flat-top carrier into the wider band carrier: take
`floor = base` and `spread = 0`, so the degenerate band `[base, base]` pins every
non-well column to `base` exactly, matching the flat profile. This is the trivial
base case of the spread carrier — the entry point a future closure argument
re-enters after a well clear re-flattens the surface (collapsing `spread` back to
`0`). -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_of_isFlatTopRWSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsFlatTopRWSkyline cfg b) : IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg 0 b := by
  obtain ⟨base, w, hw, hbase, rfl⟩ := hb
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else base, base, w, rfl, hw, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp
  · intro j hj hjw
    simp only [if_neg hjw]
    omega
  · omega

/-- **A zero-spread band carrier is a flat top.** The converse of
`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_of_isFlatTopRWSkyline`, completing the equivalence the band
carrier's definition asserts ("the flat-top carrier is exactly the `spread = 0` slice"):
at `spread = 0` the degenerate band `[floor, floor]` pins every non-well column to
`floor`, so the profile agrees with the canonical flat-top profile
`fun j => if j = w then 0 else floor` on every real column, hence the two skylines are
equal. The well, the height cap (`floor ≤ cfg.rows`), and the flat base read straight off
the band data. Paired with the forward map this makes `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg 0` and
`IsFlatTopRWSkyline` interchangeable, so a closure argument may re-enter the flat-top
machinery the instant a well clear collapses the spread back to zero. -/
theorem isFlatTopRWSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_zero {cfg : GameConfig} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg 0 b) : IsFlatTopRWSkyline cfg b := by
  obtain ⟨h, floor, w, rfl, hw, hw0, hband, hcap⟩ := hb
  refine ⟨floor, w, hw, by omega, ?_⟩
  ext ⟨j, r⟩
  simp only [mem_skyline]
  by_cases hj : j < cfg.cols
  · rcases eq_or_ne j w with rfl | hjw
    · simp [hw0]
    · obtain ⟨hlo, hhi⟩ := hband j hj hjw
      have hjf : h j = floor := by omega
      simp [hj, hjw, hjf]
  · simp [hj]

/-- **A spread-bounded reserved well is a reserved-well skyline (forget the band).**
The forgetful map down to the bare reserved-well carrier: drop the `floor`/`spread`
band structure, keeping only the well and the height bound. The bound is recovered
from the band cap — a non-well column sits at `h j ≤ floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows`, and
the well sits at `0 ≤ cfg.rows`. This is the inclusion that lets the spread carrier
inherit every reserved-well discharge (`hheight`, non-loss, the scalar ceiling)
without re-proving them, exactly as `isReservedWellSkyline_of_isFlatTopRWSkyline`
does for the flat top. -/
theorem isReservedWellSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline {cfg : GameConfig}
    {spread : ℕ} {b : Board} (hb : IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread b) :
    IsReservedWellSkyline cfg b := by
  obtain ⟨h, floor, w, rfl, hw, hw0, hband, hcap⟩ := hb
  refine ⟨h, rfl, ?_, w, hw, hw0⟩
  intro j hj
  rcases eq_or_ne j w with rfl | hjw
  · rw [hw0]; exact Nat.zero_le _
  · obtain ⟨_, hub⟩ := hband j hj hjw
    omega

/-- **Spread-bounded reserved wells discharge `hheight`.** Inherited through the
forgetful map to the reserved-well carrier
(`isReservedWellSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline`): every column of a
band-bounded board sits at or below `cfg.rows`. The spread-carrier analogue of
`colHeight_le_of_isFlatTopRWSkyline`, supplying the height obligation for any
bag-indexed invariant that occupies the band phase. -/
theorem colHeight_le_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {b : Board} (hb : IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread b) (j : ℕ) :
    colHeight b j ≤ cfg.rows :=
  colHeight_le_of_isReservedWellSkyline
    (isReservedWellSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline hb) j

/-- **The band carrier exposes a usable empty well (`colHeight` readback).** The
live-carrier face of `exists_well_colHeight_eq_zero_of_isReservedWellSkyline`,
obtained through the forgetful map to the reserved-well carrier. Downstream
regulator and `hP_step` reasoning works directly with `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline`,
so quoting `∃ w, colHeight b w = 0` here avoids re-forgetting to the reserved-well
form at every well-drop: the spread band always offers a fully clear column. -/
theorem exists_well_colHeight_eq_zero_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline {cfg : GameConfig}
    {spread : ℕ} {b : Board} (hb : IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread b) :
    ∃ w, w < cfg.cols ∧ colHeight b w = 0 :=
  exists_well_colHeight_eq_zero_of_isReservedWellSkyline
    (isReservedWellSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline hb)

/-- **A spread-bounded reserved well is never lost.** The survival certificate for
the band carrier, inherited through the forgetful map
(`isReservedWellSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline`) and the reserved-well non-loss
fact (`not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline`): every band-bounded board keeps all
cells under the ceiling, so no row spills out. The spread-carrier analogue of
`not_isLost_of_isFlatTopRWSkyline`. -/
theorem not_isLost_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {b : Board} (hb : IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread b) : ¬ Board.isLost cfg b :=
  not_isLost_of_isReservedWellSkyline
    (isReservedWellSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline hb)

/-- **The empty board is a spread-bounded reserved well (`hinit`, zero spread).**
The all-zero profile with `floor = 0`, `spread = 0`, well `w = 0` is the empty
board: every (vacuously non-well) column sits in the degenerate band `[0, 0]`, the
well is empty, and the cap `0 ≤ cfg.rows` holds. The spread-carrier analogue of
`isFlatTopRWSkyline_empty` / `isReservedWellSkyline_empty`, supplying the
initial-state discharge for a band-indexed invariant occupying the flat phase from
the start. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_empty (cfg : GameConfig) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg 0 Board.empty := by
  refine ⟨fun _ => 0, 0, 0, (skyline_zero_eq_empty cfg).symm, cfg.cols_pos, rfl, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj hjw; simp
  · simp

/-- **The empty board is a spread-`s` band for every `s ≤ rows` (`hinit`, general
spread).** Generalises `isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_empty` (spread `0`) to an arbitrary
spread bound: the all-zero profile with `floor = 0`, well `w = 0` is the empty board,
every vacuously non-well column sits in the band `[0, s]`, the well is empty, and the
only nontrivial obligation is the ceiling `0 + s ≤ rows`, supplied by `hs`. This is the
initial-state discharge for a band-indexed invariant pinned to a fixed spread, the
`hinit` half of a forthcoming `tetrisSolvableValid_of_spreadBand_closed`. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_empty_of_le (cfg : GameConfig) {spread : ℕ}
    (hs : spread ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread Board.empty := by
  refine ⟨fun _ => 0, 0, 0, (skyline_zero_eq_empty cfg).symm, cfg.cols_pos, rfl, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro j hj hjw; simp
  · simpa using hs

/-- **The empty board is a pocket band (`hI_init` for the refined invariant).**
The all-zero profile is flat, so any four non-well columns form a level pocket;
taking `floor = 0`, well `w = 0`, and pocket origin `c = 1` (valid once
`4 < cfg.cols`) discharges the initial-state obligation of
`tetrisSolvableValid_of_strategy_invariant` at `I := IsLevelPocketBand`. -/
theorem isLevelPocketBand_empty (cfg : GameConfig) {spread : ℕ}
    (hs : spread ≤ cfg.rows) (hcols : 4 < cfg.cols) :
    IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread Board.empty := by
  refine ⟨fun _ => 0, 0, 0, 1, (skyline_zero_eq_empty cfg).symm, cfg.cols_pos, rfl,
    ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_, rfl, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩
  · intro j hj hjw; simp
  · simpa using hs
  · omega
  · omega
  · omega
  · omega
  · omega

/-- **A pocket band is height-capped (`hI_height` for the refined invariant).**
Immediate from the forgetful map into the bare spread band and its ceiling bound;
the second of the two free obligations of the reachability route at
`I := IsLevelPocketBand`. -/
theorem colHeight_le_of_isLevelPocketBand {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ} {b : Board}
    (hb : IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread b) (j : ℕ) : colHeight b j ≤ cfg.rows :=
  colHeight_le_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline (isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_of_isLevelPocketBand hb) j

/-- **Flatness regenerates a level pocket: any flat profile is a pocket band.**
The pocket-regeneration source for the reachability route. A flat profile (every
non-well column at the common height `base`, the well `w` empty) automatically
carries a level width-four pocket: on at least eight columns a four-wide window
`(c, c+1, c+2, c+3)` dodging the single well always exists (pick `c = 4` when
`w < 4`, else `c = 0`), and on a flat top every such window already rests at the
common floor `base`. So whenever the strategy returns the surface to a flat top —
the re-entry state a well clear / perfect clear produces — a fresh level pocket
reappears for free, with the band cap `base + spread ≤ rows` the only external
obligation. This is the codomain-completion half the pocket-band `hI_step` needs:
the band landings (`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_lands_of_isLevelPocketBand`) supply
"lands in a band", and this lemma re-exhibits the pocket once the surface has been
re-flattened. -/
theorem isLevelPocketBand_flat {cfg : GameConfig} {spread base w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (h8 : 8 ≤ cfg.cols) (hcap : base + spread ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread (skyline cfg (fun j => if j = w then 0 else base)) := by
  obtain ⟨c, hc3, hwc, hwc1, hwc2, hwc3⟩ :
      ∃ c, c + 3 < cfg.cols ∧ w ≠ c ∧ w ≠ c + 1 ∧ w ≠ c + 2 ∧ w ≠ c + 3 := by
    rcases Nat.lt_or_ge w 4 with hlt | hge
    · exact ⟨4, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega⟩
    · exact ⟨0, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega, by omega⟩
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else base, base, w, c, rfl, hw, ?_, ?_, hcap, hc3,
    hwc, hwc1, hwc2, hwc3, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · simp
  · intro j hj hjw; simp only [if_neg hjw]; omega
  · simp only [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc)]
  · simp only [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc1)]
  · simp only [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc2)]
  · simp only [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc3)]

/-- **The well clear regenerates the pocket: a vertical-I clear on a flat profile
lands in a level pocket band.** The reflatten-then-regenerate composite that closes
the pocket-regeneration story. On a flat profile (every non-well column at the
common height `base ≥ 4`, the well `w` empty), dropping a vertical `I` into the well
completes and clears four full rows, dropping every non-well column by four to the
flat height `base - 4` (`applyStep_vertI_well`); that re-flattened surface then
carries a fresh level pocket by `isLevelPocketBand_flat`. So the once-per-bag well
clear is precisely the move that re-exhibits the level-4 pocket the flat-shelf fills
consume — the missing middle between `isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_lands_of_isLevelPocketBand`
(fills lift the surface into a band) and the regeneration source. The only external
obligations are eight columns and the lowered-floor cap `base - 4 + spread ≤ rows`. -/
theorem isLevelPocketBand_vertI_well_of_flat {cfg : GameConfig} {spread base w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (h8 : 8 ≤ cfg.cols) (hbase4 : 4 ≤ base)
    (hcap : base - 4 + spread ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread
      (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (skyline cfg (fun j => if j = w then 0 else base))
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }) := by
  have hwell : (fun j => if j = w then 0 else base) w = 0 := by
    show (if w = w then 0 else base) = 0; rw [if_pos rfl]
  have hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w →
      4 ≤ (fun j => if j = w then 0 else base) j := by
    intro j hj hjw; show 4 ≤ (if j = w then 0 else base); rw [if_neg hjw]; exact hbase4
  rw [applyStep_vertI_well hw hwell hothers]
  have hprofile : (fun j => if j = w then 0 else (fun j => if j = w then 0 else base) j - 4)
      = (fun j => if j = w then 0 else base - 4) := by
    funext j; by_cases hjw : j = w
    · simp [hjw]
    · simp [hjw]
  rw [hprofile]
  exact isLevelPocketBand_flat hw h8 hcap

/-- **The well clear descends the whole band by four (the height regulator).** The
spread-band generalisation of `isLevelPocketBand_vertI_well_of_flat` (which needs a FLAT
input): on ANY spread-`spread` reserved-well band whose floor is at least four, dropping a
vertical `I` into the empty well completes and clears four full rows, lowering every non-well
column by exactly four (`applyStep_vertI_well`). The result is again a spread-`spread` band,
now floored at `floor - 4`, well still empty. This is the amortised height regulator the
per-bag schedule turns on: the valley fills (`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_lands_of_isLevelPocketBand`,
`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_SZ_valley_of_notch`) each raise the band, and this once-per-bag clear
repays the drift, descending the floor by four while the band width never grows. Unlike the
flat-input regenerator it keeps no pocket, but it works on the rough band the fills leave behind
— the missing descent half of a bounded-band closure. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_vertI_well_descend {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {h : ℕ → ℕ} {floor w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0) (hfloor4 : 4 ≤ floor)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread)
    (hslack : floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }) := by
  have hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h j :=
    fun j hj hjw => le_trans hfloor4 (hband j hj hjw).1
  rw [applyStep_vertI_well hw hw0 hothers]
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else h j - 4, floor - 4, w, rfl, hw, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · show (if w = w then 0 else h w - 4) = 0
    rw [if_pos rfl]
  · intro j hj hjw
    obtain ⟨hlo, hhi⟩ := hband j hj hjw
    simp only [if_neg hjw]
    omega
  · omega

/-- **The well clear descends a level-pocket band by four, keeping the pocket.** The
pocket-preserving strengthening of `isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_vertI_well_descend` (ABLI,
which keeps only the bare band) and the rough-input generalisation of
`isLevelPocketBand_vertI_well_of_flat` (which needs a *flat* input): on ANY level-pocket
band of spread `spread` whose floor is at least four — the off-well columns living in
`[floor, floor+spread]`, with a width-four LEVEL shelf `c..c+3` resting exactly at the
floor — dropping a vertical `I` into the empty well clears four full rows
(`applyStep_vertI_well`), lowering every non-well column by four. The shelf columns,
sitting at `floor`, descend to `floor - 4`, which is precisely the *new* floor, so the
pocket survives intact one band lower. This is the height regulator the per-bag schedule
needs in the exact `tetrisSolvableValid_of_levelPocketBand_closed` carrier: the valley
fills consume the shelf and raise the surface, and this once-per-bag clear both repays the
height drift AND re-exhibits a landing site for the next bag — closing the regeneration gap
the flat-input version could not (an arbitrary post-fill band is not flat, but it still
carries its floor shelf). -/
theorem isLevelPocketBand_vertI_well_descend {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {h : ℕ → ℕ} {floor w c : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0) (hfloor4 : 4 ≤ floor)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread)
    (hcap : floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1)
    (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hpc : h c = floor) (hpc1 : h (c + 1) = floor)
    (hpc2 : h (c + 2) = floor) (hpc3 : h (c + 3) = floor) :
    IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h)
        { piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }) := by
  have hothers : ∀ j, j < cfg.cols → j ≠ w → 4 ≤ h j :=
    fun j hj hjw => le_trans hfloor4 (hband j hj hjw).1
  rw [applyStep_vertI_well hw hw0 hothers]
  refine ⟨fun j => if j = w then 0 else h j - 4, floor - 4, w, c, rfl, hw, ?_, ?_, ?_,
    hc3, hwc, hwc1, hwc2, hwc3, ?_, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · show (if w = w then 0 else h w - 4) = 0
    rw [if_pos rfl]
  · intro j hj hjw
    obtain ⟨hlo, hhi⟩ := hband j hj hjw
    simp only [if_neg hjw]
    omega
  · omega
  · show (if c = w then 0 else h c - 4) = floor - 4
    rw [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc), hpc]
  · show (if c + 1 = w then 0 else h (c + 1) - 4) = floor - 4
    rw [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc1), hpc1]
  · show (if c + 2 = w then 0 else h (c + 2) - 4) = floor - 4
    rw [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc2), hpc2]
  · show (if c + 3 = w then 0 else h (c + 3) - 4) = floor - 4
    rw [if_neg (Ne.symm hwc3), hpc3]

/-- **Validity-carrying ∃-form of the pocket-preserving well-clear (the σ-dispatch I-branch).**
Packages `isLevelPocketBand_vertI_well_descend` into the `∃ pl, pl.piece = I ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
carrier` shape a strategy's per-piece response consumes — the height-regulator analogue of the
fills' existing existential forms (`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_lands_of_isLevelPocketBand`,
`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_SZ_valley_of_notch`). Validity is immediate: the vertical `I` is one
column wide (every shape cell sits at column offset `0`), so it is in-bounds wherever the well
column `w < cfg.cols` is. With the descent already proven to re-enter `IsLevelPocketBand` one
band lower (keeping the floor shelf), this is the ready-to-use `I` clause of σ's per-piece
dispatch on the `tetrisSolvableValid_of_levelPocketBand_closed` carrier: when the bag hands the
player an `I`, σ plays it into the well and stays admissible. -/
theorem isLevelPocketBand_vertI_well_descend_exists {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {h : ℕ → ℕ} {floor w c : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0) (hfloor4 : 4 ≤ floor)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread)
    (hcap : floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows)
    (hc3 : c + 3 < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1)
    (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2) (hwc3 : w ≠ c + 3)
    (hpc : h c = floor) (hpc1 : h (c + 1) = floor)
    (hpc2 : h (c + 2) = floor) (hpc3 : h (c + 3) = floor) :
    ∃ pl : Placement, pl.piece = Piece.I ∧ pl.Valid cfg ∧
      IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) pl) := by
  refine ⟨{ piece := Piece.I, rot := 1, col := w }, rfl, ?_,
    isLevelPocketBand_vertI_well_descend hw hw0 hfloor4 hband hcap hc3 hwc hwc1 hwc2 hwc3
      hpc hpc1 hpc2 hpc3⟩
  intro cell hcell
  have h0 : ∀ c ∈ Piece.I.shapeUp 1, c.1 = 0 := by decide
  have hcz := h0 cell hcell
  show w + cell.1 < cfg.cols
  omega

/-- **Floor-exposing level pocket band.** Identical to `IsLevelPocketBand` except the
common `floor` of the off-well columns is lifted OUT of the existential and exposed as an
explicit index. This is the missing primitive for chaining the vertical-I well descent:
`IsLevelPocketBand` hides `floor`, so a regulator that needs `4 ≤ floor` cannot consume the
predicate directly. With `floor` visible we can both forget down to `IsLevelPocketBand` and
state a closed `floor` to `floor - 4` descent. -/
def IsLevelPocketBandAt (cfg : GameConfig) (spread floor : ℕ) (b : Board) : Prop :=
  ∃ (h : ℕ → ℕ) (w c : ℕ),
    b = skyline cfg h ∧ w < cfg.cols ∧ h w = 0 ∧
    (∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread) ∧
    floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows ∧
    c + 3 < cfg.cols ∧ w ≠ c ∧ w ≠ c + 1 ∧ w ≠ c + 2 ∧ w ≠ c + 3 ∧
    h c = floor ∧ h (c + 1) = floor ∧ h (c + 2) = floor ∧ h (c + 3) = floor

/-- Forgetting the exposed `floor` recovers the ordinary `IsLevelPocketBand`. -/
theorem isLevelPocketBand_of_isLevelPocketBandAt {cfg : GameConfig} {spread floor : ℕ}
    {b : Board} (hb : IsLevelPocketBandAt cfg spread floor b) :
    IsLevelPocketBand cfg spread b := by
  obtain ⟨h, w, c, hbsky, hw, hw0, hband, hcap, hc3, hwc, hwc1, hwc2, hwc3,
    hpc, hpc1, hpc2, hpc3⟩ := hb
  exact ⟨h, floor, w, c, hbsky, hw, hw0, hband, hcap, hc3, hwc, hwc1, hwc2, hwc3,
    hpc, hpc1, hpc2, hpc3⟩

/-- **A floor-pinned spread band: the closed vocabulary the reservoir actually lands in.**
`IsLevelPocketBandAt` minus the width-four pocket clauses: a reserved-well skyline whose every
off-well column sits in the band `[floor, floor + spread]`, with `floor` exposed as a tracked
parameter (unlike `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline`, which hides it existentially). This is the honest
target of an arbitrary-member non-`I` drop: the proven `_pocket_of_isLevelPocketBandAt` fill arcs
*consume* the width-four pocket (e.g. an `O` raises its two pocket columns to `floor + 2`), so the
landing surface is no longer a pocket band — but it is still a floor-pinned spread band at the same
floor. Tracking the floor as a parameter is what lets the reservoir ledger account for the
once-per-bag drain even after the pocket has been spent. -/
def IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt (cfg : GameConfig) (spread floor : ℕ) (b : Board) : Prop :=
  ∃ (h : ℕ → ℕ) (w : ℕ), b = skyline cfg h ∧ w < cfg.cols ∧ h w = 0 ∧
    (∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread) ∧
    floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows

/-- Forgetting the width-four pocket of a level pocket band recovers the floor-pinned spread
band at the same floor. The fill arcs route through this forgetful edge: a pocket band is the
*input* shape (it guarantees a flush landing), and the floor-pinned spread band is the *output*
shape (the pocket is consumed but the floor and the spread bound survive). -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_of_isLevelPocketBandAt {cfg : GameConfig} {spread floor : ℕ}
    {b : Board} (hb : IsLevelPocketBandAt cfg spread floor b) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg spread floor b := by
  obtain ⟨h, w, _c, hbsky, hw, hw0, hband, hcap, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, -⟩ := hb
  exact ⟨h, w, hbsky, hw, hw0, hband, hcap⟩

/-- The floor-pinned spread band forgets to the ordinary (existential-floor) spread band, so
every height and survival face already proven for `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline` transports across it.
-/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_of_isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt {cfg : GameConfig}
    {spread floor : ℕ} {b : Board} (hb : IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg spread floor b) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkyline cfg spread b := by
  obtain ⟨h, w, hbsky, hw, hw0, hband, hcap⟩ := hb
  exact ⟨h, floor, w, hbsky, hw, hw0, hband, hcap⟩

/-- **The floor-pinned raw-band smart constructor.** The floor-tracking analogue of
`isSpreadBoundedRWSkyline_skyline`: builds an `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg spread floor` witness
directly from a height function `h` and its band data, keeping `floor` visible in the conclusion.
Every floor-pinned fill arc closes through this constructor, which is exactly the existing raw-band
intro with the `floor` argument promoted from existential to explicit. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline {cfg : GameConfig} {spread : ℕ}
    {h : ℕ → ℕ} {floor w : ℕ}
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → floor ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ floor + spread)
    (hslack : floor + spread ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg spread floor (skyline cfg h) :=
  ⟨h, w, rfl, hw, hw0, hband, hslack⟩

/-- **The L-then-S round trip re-enters the floor-pinned spread band.** The carrier-vocabulary
payoff of the rebuild-and-consume atom: on a flat triple `c, c+1, c+2` at the band floor `base`,
inside a floor-`base` spread-`s` band (`3 ≤ s`) with a reserved empty well `w` and the band fitting
under the ceiling (`base + s ≤ rows`), playing the L (which re-digs the S-notch) and then the bag's
owed S lands the board back in `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base` at the *same* floor. The board
is again a reserved-well skyline (`applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well`), every off-well column stays in
`[base, base + s]` (`colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band`), and the well remains empty, so the
floor-pinned smart constructor closes it. This is the spread-carrier face of hosting an owed `S` when
an `L` is available to manufacture its landing site first — the surface neither drifts up across the
pair (the floor is unchanged) nor leaves the carrier, so the once-per-bag I drain still resets it.
The remaining gap to the all-orders closure is purely the *ordering*: this atom needs the L played
before the S, which the adversary may not concede, so the bag-phase invariant must instead reserve
the notch ahead of the staircase draw. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_LS_flat {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcb : h c = base) (hc1b : h (c + 1) = base) (hc2b : h (c + 2) = base)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hs3 : 3 ≤ s) (hslack : base + s ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base
      (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  obtain ⟨hLS, hLSeq, hLSw0⟩ :=
    applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega) hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hLSeq]
  refine isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline (floor := base) (w := w) hw hLSw0 ?_ hslack
  intro j hj hjw
  have hbridge : hLS j = Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
      { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) j := by
    rw [hLSeq, colHeight_skyline hj]
  rw [hbridge]
  exact colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band hc hc1 hc2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hcb hc1b hc2b
    hband hs3 j hj hjw

/-- **The L-then-S round trip re-enters the reservoir band when the valley sits above the floor —
the level-`f` generalization of `isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_LS_flat`.** Drops the
floor-pinned `h c = base` (and `3 ≤ s`) for a flat triple at any in-band level `f` with `base ≤ f`
and `f + 3 ≤ base + s`: playing the L (which re-digs the S-notch) then the owed S lands the board
back in `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base` at the *same* floor `base`, even though the
re-dug valley sat at level `f` rather than at the exposed floor. So the carrier can host an owed S
hole-free whenever an L is available to re-dig the valley first, at any band level — the form the
bag-phase invariant needs when the reserved S-valley is parked above the floor. Proof: obtain the
post-LS skyline with empty well (`applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well`), rewrite to it, apply the smart
constructor `isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline` at floor `base`, and discharge band membership by
bridging `hLS j` to the post-LS `colHeight` (via `colHeight_skyline`) and citing the level-`f`
L-then-S band bound (iter444). The ordering caveat is unchanged: this atom still needs the L played
before the S, so the bag-phase invariant must reserve the valley ahead of the staircase draw. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_LS_flat_at {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s f c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcf : h c = f) (hc1f : h (c + 1) = f) (hc2f : h (c + 2) = f)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hflo : base ≤ f) (hfhi : f + 3 ≤ base + s) (hslack : base + s ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base
      (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  obtain ⟨hLS, hLSeq, hLSw0⟩ :=
    applyStep_LS_skyline_flat_well hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega) hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hLSeq]
  refine isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline (floor := base) (w := w) hw hLSw0 ?_ hslack
  intro j hj hjw
  have hbridge : hLS j = Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c })
      { piece := Piece.S, rot := 0, col := c }) j := by
    rw [hLSeq, colHeight_skyline hj]
  rw [hbridge]
  exact colHeight_applyStep_LS_skyline_band_at hc hc1 hc2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hcf hc1f hc2f
    hband hflo hfhi j hj hjw

/-- **Level-`f` JZ re-entry: a J-then-Z round trip on an interior flat triple stays in the band.**
The level-`f` generalization of `isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_JZ_flat`, the mirror of
`isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_LS_flat_at`. On a flat triple `c, c+1, c+2` resting at an
arbitrary interior level `f` (with `base ≤ f` and `f + 3 ≤ base + s`) inside a spread-`s` band with
reserved empty well `w`, playing the J (which manufactures the Z-notch) then the owed Z lands the
board back in `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base` at the *same* floor `base`. This is the
level-flexible form the bag-phase carrier needs: the JZ atom no longer has to sit on the band floor,
only on a triple whose top has three rows of head-room below the ceiling. Proof: obtain the post-JZ
skyline with empty well, rewrite, apply the floor-`base` smart constructor, and discharge band
membership via the level-`f` JZ band bound (iter446). The ordering caveat is unchanged: the J must be
played before the Z, so the all-orders invariant must reserve the Z-notch ahead of the staircase. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_JZ_flat_at {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s f c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcf : h c = f) (hc1f : h (c + 1) = f) (hc2f : h (c + 2) = f)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hflo : base ≤ f) (hfhi : f + 3 ≤ base + s) (hslack : base + s ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base
      (Placement.applyStep cfg
        (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
        { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  obtain ⟨hJZ, hJZeq, hJZw0⟩ :=
    applyStep_JZ_skyline_flat_well hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega) hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hJZeq]
  refine isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline (floor := base) (w := w) hw hJZw0 ?_ hslack
  intro j hj hjw
  have hbridge : hJZ j = Board.colHeight (Placement.applyStep cfg
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c })
      { piece := Piece.Z, rot := 0, col := c }) j := by
    rw [hJZeq, colHeight_skyline hj]
  rw [hbridge]
  exact colHeight_applyStep_JZ_skyline_band_at hc hc1 hc2 hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0 hcf hc1f hc2f
    hband hflo hfhi j hj hjw

/-- **Single `L` on an interior flat triple stays in the band, manufacturing an S-notch along the
way.** The single-piece companion of `isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_LS_flat_at`: playing just
the `L` on a flat triple `c, c+1, c+2` resting at interior level `f` (with `base ≤ f` and
`f + 2 ≤ base + s`) inside a spread-`s` band with reserved empty well `w` lands the board back in
`IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base` at the *same* floor — its profile is `f+1, f+1, f+2`, all
inside `[base, base + s]`, and the well stays empty. Unlike the two-piece round trip this edge needs
only the `L` (no companion `S`), so it is order-free: an `L` can always be absorbed flat-band-safely
whenever a level triple with two rows of head-room exists. The payoff is that the post-`L` board now
carries an S-notch at `c, c+1, c+2` (heights `f+1, f+1, f+2`), the very landing site a later owed `S`
consumes — so this is the bag-phase carrier's notch-reservation step in band vocabulary. Proof:
obtain the post-`L` S-notch skyline with its off-triple frame, rewrite, apply the floor-`base` smart
constructor, and discharge band membership per column — the three worked columns by `omega` on the
absolute heights, the rest by the frame plus the input band bound. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_L_flat_at {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s f c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcf : h c = f) (hc1f : h (c + 1) = f) (hc2f : h (c + 2) = f)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hflo : base ≤ f) (hfhi : f + 2 ≤ base + s) (hslack : base + s ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.L, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  obtain ⟨hL, hLeq, hLc, hLc1, hLc2, hLw0, hLframe⟩ :=
    applyStep_L_skyline_flat_isSnotchSkyline_absFrame hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hLeq]
  refine isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline (floor := base) (w := w) hw hLw0 ?_ hslack
  intro j hj hjw
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · subst hjc; rw [hLc]; omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · subst hjc1; rw [hLc1]; omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · subst hjc2; rw [hLc2]; omega
      · rw [hLframe j hjc hjc1 hjc2]; exact hband j hj hjw

/-- **Single `J` on an interior flat triple stays in the band, manufacturing a Z-notch along the
way.** The mirror of `isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_L_flat_at`: playing just the `J` on a flat
triple `c, c+1, c+2` resting at interior level `f` (with `base ≤ f` and `f + 2 ≤ base + s`) inside a
spread-`s` band with reserved empty well `w` lands the board back in `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s
base` at the *same* floor — its profile is `f+2, f+1, f+1`, all inside `[base, base + s]`, and the
well stays empty. Order-free like its `L` counterpart: a `J` can always be absorbed
flat-band-safely whenever a level triple with two rows of head-room exists, and the post-`J` board
now carries a Z-notch at `c, c+1, c+2` (heights `f+2, f+1, f+1`), the very landing site a later owed
`Z` consumes. So this is the mirror notch-reservation step the bag-phase carrier needs, in band
vocabulary. Proof: identical to the `L` case via `applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_absFrame`,
discharging the three worked columns by `omega` and the rest by the frame plus the input band. -/
theorem isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_J_flat_at {cfg : GameConfig} {h : ℕ → ℕ}
    {base s f c w : ℕ}
    (hc : c < cfg.cols) (hc1 : c + 1 < cfg.cols) (hc2 : c + 2 < cfg.cols)
    (hw : w < cfg.cols) (hwc : w ≠ c) (hwc1 : w ≠ c + 1) (hwc2 : w ≠ c + 2)
    (hw0 : h w = 0)
    (hcf : h c = f) (hc1f : h (c + 1) = f) (hc2f : h (c + 2) = f)
    (hband : ∀ j < cfg.cols, j ≠ w → base ≤ h j ∧ h j ≤ base + s)
    (hflo : base ≤ f) (hfhi : f + 2 ≤ base + s) (hslack : base + s ≤ cfg.rows) :
    IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base
      (Placement.applyStep cfg (skyline cfg h) { piece := Piece.J, rot := 0, col := c }) := by
  obtain ⟨hJ, hJeq, hJc, hJc1, hJc2, hJw0, hJframe⟩ :=
    applyStep_J_skyline_flat_isZnotchSkyline_absFrame hc hc1 hc2 (by omega) (by omega)
      hw hwc hwc1 hwc2 hw0
  rw [hJeq]
  refine isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_skyline (floor := base) (w := w) hw hJw0 ?_ hslack
  intro j hj hjw
  by_cases hjc : j = c
  · subst hjc; rw [hJc]; omega
  · by_cases hjc1 : j = c + 1
    · subst hjc1; rw [hJc1]; omega
    · by_cases hjc2 : j = c + 2
      · subst hjc2; rw [hJc2]; omega
      · rw [hJframe j hjc hjc1 hjc2]; exact hband j hj hjw

/-- **Single `O` on an interior flat pair stays in the band, no notch.** The two-column companion of
`isSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt_applyStep_L_flat_at`: playing just the `O` on a flat PAIR `c, c+1` resting
at interior level `f` (with `base ≤ f` and `f + 2 ≤ base + s`) inside a spread-`s` band with reserved
empty well `w` lands the board back in `IsSpreadBoundedRWSkylineAt cfg s base` at the same floor — both
worked columns rise to `f+2`, inside `[base, base + s]`, and the well stays empty. Unlike `L` and `J`
the `O` digs no notch; it is the pure flat absorber. This is the first of the three band-membership
faces (`O`, horizontal-`I`, `T`) still missing as standalone lemmas, needed to assemble a notch-free
flat-filler dispatcher for the flat phase, where a generic landing window on ten columns has no room
to also host a reserved 3-wide notch. Proof: rewrite by `applyStep_O_skyline_noclear`, apply the
floor-`base` smart constructor, peel both updates for the empty well, and split the per-column band
